hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"4801","5132 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Atiyyah, a man from Banu Qurayzah, who informed him that the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stripped him on the day of Qurayzah and did not see the razor had passed over his hair, meaning his pubic hair, so they spared him from killing."
"4802","5133 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Atiyyah al-Qurazi, he said: I was a boy on the day Sa'd ibn Muadh judged concerning Banu Qurayzah that their fighters be killed and their offspring taken captive; they doubted about me, and did not find me with grown pubic hair, so here I am among you."
"4803","5134 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Atiyyah, the like of it."
"4804","5135 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, he said: Atiyyah al-Qurazi narrated to me, and he mentioned the like of it."
"4805","5136 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Atiyyah, the like of it."
"4806","5137 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr informed us, he said: Atiyyah narrated to me, and he mentioned the like of it."
"4807","5138 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. 5139 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us. 5140 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Ja'far al-Khatmi, from Umarah ibn Khuzaymah, from Kathir ibn al-Sa'ib, he said: The sons of Qurayzah narrated to me that they were presented to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Qurayzah; whoever had a wet dream or whose pubic hair had grown was killed, and whoever had not was spared. Abu Ja'far said: Some people adopted these reports and said: No one is judged to have reached puberty except by wet dream or growth of pubic hair. They also mentioned from the Companions after the Messenger of Allah what follows."
"4808","5141 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Umar ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from Nafi', from Aslam the freedman of Umar, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to the commanders of the armies: Do not impose jizyah except upon him upon whom the razor has passed."
"4809","5142 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ayyub and Ubaydullah informed us, from Nafi', from Aslam, from Umar, the like of it."
"4810","5143 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, from his father, I think he said: Uthman was brought a boy who had stolen, and he said: Look, has his waist-wrapper grown green with hair? If it has, cut him; if not, do not cut him."
"4811","5144 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Harmalah ibn Imran al-Tujibi narrated to me, that Tamim ibn Far' al-Mahri narrated to him that he was in the army that conquered Alexandria the last time, and Amr ibn al-As did not apportion to me anything from the fay' and said: A boy who has not had a wet dream, until there was almost strife between my people and some Quraysh over it. The people said: Among you are men from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so ask them; so they asked Abu Nadrah al-Ghifari and Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani, the two Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they said: Look, if he has grown hair, apportion to him. He said: Some of the people looked at me, and I had grown hair, so he apportioned to me. Abu Ja'far said: Others opposed them and said: Puberty may be by these two signs or by a third: fifteen years passing over the boy without wet dream or growth, and he is thereby in the ruling of adults. They argued with what follows."
"4812","5145 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: I was presented to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud when I was fourteen years old, and he did not permit me among the fighters; and I was presented to him on the day of the Trench when I was fifteen, and he permitted me among the fighters. Nafi' said: I narrated this hadith to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz and he said: This is most like the boundary between offspring and fighters, so he ordered the commanders to enroll one younger than fifteen among the offspring and one at fifteen among the fighters."
"4813","5146 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim Abu Yusuf, from Ubaydullah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4814","5147 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, but did not mention Nafi's statement about Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz to the end. They said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted Ibn Umar at fifteen and rejected him below that, it is established that the ruling of a fifteen-year-old is the ruling of adults in all rulings, and one younger is a non-adult except one whose puberty appeared earlier by wet dream or growth. Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz's adoption of it strengthened this; this is the saying of Abu Yusuf and a group of our companions, except that Muhammad ibn al-Hasan did not deem growth evidence of puberty. Abu Hanifah did not deem one who reaches fifteen without wet dream or growth as adult until seventeen years pass."
"4815","5148 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to me, from his father, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. And something contrary to that was also narrated from him."
"4816","5149 - Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sama'ah narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Yusuf say: Abu Hanifah said: When eighteen years pass over him, he has thereby entered the rulings of men. They did not differ from him in either narration concerning the slave-girl: when seventeen years pass over her she is thereby like one who has menstruated. Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, treated boy and girl alike, making fifteen years the threshold of adulthood for both. Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on him, followed Abu Yusuf for the boy and Abu Hanifah for the girl. Among Abu Hanifah's proof against them on Ibn Umar's hadith: it is possible the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rejected him at fourteen not for minority but for weakness he saw, and permitted him at fifteen not for adulthood but for strength and hardiness; and it is possible the Prophet did not know his age in either case. What the Prophet did with Samurah ibn Jundub supports this too."
"4817","5150 - Ahmad ibn Mas'ud al-Khayyat narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Isa ibn al-Tabba' narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from his father, from Samurah ibn Jundub, that his mother was a beautiful woman from Banu Fazarah who took him to Madinah as a boy; her suitors were many and she kept saying: I will only marry one who guarantees care of this son of mine, so a man married her on that condition. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned stipends to the boys of the Ansar and not to him, as if deeming him weak, he said: O Messenger of Allah, you assigned to a boy but not to me; I can wrestle him. He said: Wrestle him; so I wrestled him and threw him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned to him. Since the Prophet permitted Samurah for throwing the Ansari, not for puberty, his treatment of Ibn Umar may likewise have been for strength not puberty, and his rejection for weakness not minority. Thus that hadith is no proof for Abu Yusuf, and Abu Hanifah does not deny stipends for boys who can bear fighting and attend war even if minors. And something contrary to Ibn Umar's report was narrated from al-Bara ibn Azib about the Prophet's treatment of Ibn Umar."
"4818","5151 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) inspected me and Ibn Umar on the day of Badr and deemed us small, then permitted us on the day of Uhud. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith the Prophet permitted Ibn Umar on Uhud day when he was fourteen, contradicting Ibn Umar's report. So that hadith proves neither side, and we sought the ruling by reasoning: Allah made the waiting period of a menstruating woman three courses, and of one who does not menstruate from youth or age three months, making a month substitute for a menstruation, upon the prevalent case of one menstruation a month. Likewise, since wet dream confers adulthood and all agree there is a substitute when absent, some saying fifteen years and others more, we set the substitute at the prevalent age of wet dreams, fifteen years, not less or more, considering the general not the exceptional case, as with menstruation. So by sound reasoning Abu Yusuf's view is established."
"4819","5152 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Ata ibn Dinar, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said about: Do not approach the orphan's wealth except in the best manner until he reaches his full strength: that is eighteen years; and the like of it in the chapter of Banu Isra'il."
"4820","Chapter 4: What is forbidden to kill of women and children in the abode of war. 5153 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Ikrimah, he said: Najdah wrote to Ibn Abbas asking him about killing children. So he wrote to him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not kill them."
"4821","5154 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Qays narrating from Yazid ibn Hurmuz, he said: Najdah wrote to Ibn Abbas asking: Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kill any of the polytheists' children? Ibn Abbas wrote to him, while I was present: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not kill any of them."
"4822","5155 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent out his armies he said: Do not kill children."
"4823","5156 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Nafi' narrated to us, from Ibn Umar, he said: A slain woman was found in one of the expeditions, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them from killing women and children."
"4824","5157 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Nafi', from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, but he did not mention Ibn Umar."
"4825","5158 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Juwayriyah narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4826","5159 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas and others narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade killing women and children."
"4827","5160 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik informed me, from his uncle, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing women and children when he sent the expedition to Ibn Abi al-Huqayq."
"4828","5161 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from Ka'b ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade those who killed Ibn Abi al-Huqayq, when they went out to him, from killing children and women."
"4829","5162 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Abis narrated to us, from Aban ibn Taghlib, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a detachment he said to them: Do not kill a child nor a woman."
"4830","5163 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us. 5164 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an army, among what he enjoined upon them was: Do not kill a child. 5165 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi said in his hadith: Alqamah said: I narrated it to Muqatil ibn Hayyan, and he said: Muslim ibn Haysam narrated to me, from al-Nu'man ibn Muqarrin, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4831","5166 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us. 5167 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, they said: al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Shu'bah ibn al-Hajjaj, from Alqamah ibn Marthad al-Hadrami, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah al-Aslami, from his father, that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed a commander over an army or detachment, among what he enjoined was: Do not kill a child."
"4832","5168 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, he said: Umayr ibn Abdullah narrated to me, from Atiyyah al-Awfi, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing women and children. He said: They belong to the victor as captives."
"4833","5169 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: al-Mughirah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Qurashi narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, he said: al-Muraqqa' ibn Sayfi narrated to me, from his grandfather Rabah ibn Abi Hanzalah the scribe, that he went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a raid he raided, with Khalid ibn al-Walid at the vanguard, until the Messenger of Allah caught up with them on his she-camel; they parted from a slain woman they were looking at, so he sent to Khalid ibn al-Walid forbidding him from killing women and children."
"4834","5170 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, he said: al-Muraqqa' ibn Sayfi informed me, from his grandfather Rabah ibn Rabi', that he went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: Do not kill offspring nor a hireling."
"4835","5171 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Mughirah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4836","5172 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abdullah ibn Dhakwan, from al-Muraqqa' ibn Sayfi, from Hanzalah the scribe, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he passed by a woman of striking build with people gathered over her; when he arrived they parted, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She was not fighting. Then the Messenger of Allah instructed Khalid not to kill a woman nor a hireling."
"4837","5173 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: Some people held that killing women and children in the abode of war is never permissible, and that it is unlawful to aim at killing others if their death is thereby risked: if the enemy shields themselves with their children and Muslims cannot shoot them except by hitting their children, shooting them is forbidden in their view, and likewise if they fortify themselves and place children inside, shooting is forbidden if women and children may be hit. They argued with the reports at the head of this chapter. Others agreed on their authenticity but said the prohibition concerns intending to kill women and children; aiming at others though their death results is unobjectionable. They argued with what follows."
"4838","5174 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththamah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the households of polytheists attacked by night, when their women and children are hit, and he said: They are of them."
"4839","5175 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththamah, he said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, our horses trampled children of the polytheists? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They are of their fathers."
"4840","5176 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Surayj ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Abdullah ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabiah, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththamah, he said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, a polytheist dwelling we capture in a raid, and we strike children under horses' bellies unknowingly? He said: They are of them. Abu Ja'far said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not forbid raids though women and children are struck therein, what he permitted differs from what he prohibited: the prohibited is intending to kill women and children, the permitted is intending the polytheists even if others perish incidentally. Likewise he ordered raids without being deterred by certain incidental deaths, for their aim was not those deaths. Reason supports this too. And it was narrated about one who bit another's arm and lost his teeth when it was pulled out, that no blood-money is due, as follows."
"4841","5177 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Safwan ibn Abdullah ibn Ya'la, from his two uncles Salamah ibn Umayyah and Ya'la ibn Umayyah, they said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the raid of Tabuk, and a companion of ours fought a Muslim man; the man bit his arm and he pulled it from his mouth, knocking out his front tooth. The man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking blood-money, and he said: One of you goes to his brother and bites him like a stallion bites, then comes demanding blood-money? There is no blood-money for it; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) annulled it."
"4842","5178 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, that Safwan ibn Ya'la ibn Umayyah narrated to him from Ya'la ibn Umayyah, he said: I had a hireling who fought a man; one bit the other, and he pulled his finger so his two front teeth fell. He came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he voided his teeth. Ata said: I think Safwan said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Would he leave his hand in your mouth for you to munch it like a camel munches?"
"4843","5179 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to me, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from Ya'la ibn Umayyah, and he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: Like the munching of a young camel."
"4844","5180 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Hibban narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Imran ibn Husayn, that a man bit another man's arm and he pulled his arm away, so the biter's two front teeth fell. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you mean to munch your brother's hand as a stallion munches? And he annulled it."
"4845","5181 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from Qatadah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: Since the bitten one pulled his hand though another's teeth perished, while intending their removal without extracting his hand was forbidden, aiming at other than harm is not like aiming at harm in sin or blood-money; likewise whoever takes what is his though another's property perishes incidentally may aim at taking his right though forbidden property perishes, with no liability. So too the enemy: fighting them is lawful while killing their women and children is forbidden; intending the forbidden is unlawful, intending the permitted though forbidden deaths result is lawful, with no liability. This is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all."
"4846","Chapter 5: May an old man be killed in the abode of war or not? 5182 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Burayd ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Burdah, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished Hunayn, he sent Abu Amir with an army to Awtas; he met Durayd ibn al-Simmah, and Durayd was killed and Allah routed his companions. Abu Ja'far said: Some people adopted this and said: There is no harm in killing an old man in war, arguing with this hadith, since Durayd was then unable to fight."
"4847","5183 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) directed a force toward Awtas; Rabi' ibn Rufay' overtook Durayd ibn al-Simmah, seizing his camel's halter thinking he was a woman, but he was a very old man. He said: What do you want from me? He said: To kill you. Then he struck him with his sword but it availed nothing. He said: Evil is what your mother armed you with; take this sword of mine from the back of my saddle, then strike avoiding the bones and the brain, for thus I used to kill men. They said: Since Durayd was killed as a decrepit old man unable to defend himself and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not blame them, the very old man may be killed and his ruling is like youths not women. Others opposed them and said: Old men should not be killed; they are like women and offspring. They argued with what follows."
"4848","5184 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Abis narrated to us, from Aban ibn Taghlib, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a detachment he said: Do not kill a very old man. In this hadith is prohibition of killing old men. And the Prophet also said concerning the slain woman in Muraqqa's hadith: She was not fighting, indicating only one who fights may be killed. Reconciling with Durayd's hadith: the prohibition concerns old men with no aid to war by fighting or counsel; Durayd's case concerns old men whose counsel aids war, which may be weightier than fighting, so they may be killed even if not fighting. Likewise a woman with war planning may be killed like such an old man."
"4849","5185 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Ismail ibn Abi Habibah al-Ashhali informed us, from Dawud ibn Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent his armies he said: Do not kill the people of monasteries. Since the Prophet's practice spared cloistered monks cut off from people with Muslims safe from them, likewise every woman, decrepit old man, or child from whom Muslims are safe is not killed. This is the chapter's purport and the saying of Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, by analogy the saying of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on them all."
"4850","Chapter 6: A man kills a slain one in the abode of war - does his spoil belong to him or not? 5186 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn al-Majishun narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned the spoil to the killer."
"4851","5187 - al-Hasan ibn Abdullah ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, from Sharik, from Abd al-Karim, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A man from the polytheists stepped forth, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered al-Zubayr who went out to him and killed him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him his spoil."
"4852","5188 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Amr al-Saksaki, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from Khalid ibn al-Walid and Awf ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged the spoil for the killer."
"4853","5189 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr narrated to me, from his father, from Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i, he said: I said to Khalid ibn al-Walid on the day of Mu'tah: Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not take a fifth from spoil? He said: Yes indeed."
"4854","5190 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amr ibn Kathir ibn Aflah, from Abu Muhammad, from Abu Qatadah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted Abu Qatadah as extra share the spoil of a slain man he had killed."
"4855","5191 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amr ibn Kathir ibn Aflah, from Abu Muhammad the freedman of Abu Qatadah, from Abu Qatadah ibn Rib'i, that he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of Hunayn. When we met, the Muslims first wavered. I saw a polytheist overpowering a Muslim, so I circled behind him and struck him with my sword on his shoulder until I cut the strap of his armor; he turned on me and embraced me till I smelt death, then death overtook him and he released me. I met Umar ibn al-Khattab and asked: How are the people? He said: Allah's decree. Then the people returned. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever kills a slain one with evidence for him, his spoil is his. I stood and said: Who will testify for me? Then I sat; he repeated it a second and third time, then I stood. The Prophet asked: What is with you, O Abu Qatadah? I told him the story. A man said: He spoke truth, O Messenger of Allah, and that slain one's spoil is with me, so compensate me for it. Abu Bakr al-Siddiq said: No, by Allah; he would not take a lion of Allah's lions fighting for Allah and His Messenger and give you his spoil. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He spoke truth, so give it to him. Abu Qatadah said: So he gave it to me; I sold the armor and bought with it a palm-grove among Banu Salamah, the first wealth I acquired in Islam."
"4856","5192 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ibn Lahiah, from Abdullah ibn Abi Ja'far, from al-A'raj, from Abu Qatadah, that he killed a man from the polytheists, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted him his spoil and armor as extra share, and he sold it for five ounces."
"4857","5193 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Hunayn: Whoever kills a slain one, his spoil is his; so Abu Talhah killed twenty men that day and took their spoils."
"4858","5194 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Iyas ibn Salamah narrated to me, he said: Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrated to me, he said: We raided Hawazin with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I killed a man among them, then came leading his camel with its saddle and weapons; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met me with the people and said: Who killed the man? They said: Ibn al-Akwa'; so he said: All his spoil is his."
"4859","5195 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Umays narrated to us, from Ibn al-Akwa', from his father, he said: A spy from the polytheists came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while on a journey; he sat talking with his companions then slipped away, so the Prophet of Allah said: Seek him and kill him; I outran them to him, killed him and took his spoil, and he granted it to me as extra share. Abu Ja'far said: Some people held that whoever kills a slain one in the abode of war gets his spoil, arguing with these reports. Others said: The spoil belongs to the killer only if the Imam has said: Whoever kills a slain one, his spoil is his - if said to urge fighting when needed, it stands; if he said nothing, the spoil is booty with booty's ruling. Their proof: Khalid and Awf's report that he judged spoil for the killer may rest on a prior such statement, and likewise the Hunayn grants."
"4860","5196 - Among what proves spoil is not obligatory for the killer is what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Majishun narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, he said: I was standing on the day of Badr between two young boys; I wished I were between stronger men. One nudged me and said: Uncle, do you know Abu Jahl? I said: What do you need of him, nephew? He said: I was told he reviles the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); by Him in whose hand is my soul, if I see him my body shall not part his until the sooner of us dies. I marveled; the other nudged me and said the like. Soon I saw Abu Jahl strutting among the people and said: Do you not see him, that is your man; so they rushed him and struck him with their swords until they killed him. Then they came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him; he said: Which of you killed him? Each said: I killed him. He said: Did you wipe your swords? They said: No. He looked at the swords and said: Both of you killed him, and he awarded his spoil to Muadh ibn Amr ibn al-Jamuh; the two men were Muadh ibn Amr ibn al-Jamuh and Muadh ibn Afra. Do you not see the Prophet said to both: You killed him, then gave the spoil to one not the other? Had spoil been obligatory by killing, both would deserve it and he would not take from one to give the other. Just as if the Imam said: Whoever kills ... and two together killed, each would share, not one alone."
"4861","5197 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Makhul, from Abu Sallam, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to Badr and met the enemy; when Allah routed them, a group of Muslims pursued killing them, a group surrounded the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a group seized the camp and plunder. When Allah repelled the enemy and the pursuers returned, they said: The extra share is ours, we pursued. Those around the Prophet said: You deserve no more than us; it is ours, we shielded the Prophet lest the enemy catch him unaware. Those who seized the camp said: By Allah you deserve no more; we gathered it. So Allah revealed: They ask you about the spoils, say: The spoils are for Allah and the Messenger, until: if you are believers; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided it among them equally at once. Do you not see he did not prefer the killers over others? So the slain's spoil is not obligatory for the killer except by the Imam's grant for the Muslims' benefit in urging battle."
"4862","5198 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Budayl ibn Maysarah al-Uqayli, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from a man of Bulqayn, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Wadi al-Qura and said: O Messenger of Allah, to whom belongs the booty? He said: Allah has a share and these have four shares. I said: Is anyone more entitled to any booty than another? He said: No, even an arrow one of you takes from his side is no more his than his brother's."
"4863","5199 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from a man of Bulqayn, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: Do you not see the Prophet made the booty one-fifth for Allah and four-fifths for his companions, explaining that even if one is shot and pulls the arrow out he is no more entitled than his brother? So all fighters share alike what each does. If one says the Abu Jahl spoil and Ubadah's hadith were at Badr before spoils were granted to killers, then abrogated at Hunayn by: Whoever kills a slain one, his spoil is his - we reply: That Hunayn statement may mean only that battle, as: Whoever lays down arms is safe meant only that conquest. Since pre-Hunayn rule denied automatic spoil, and Hunayn's statement may or may not abrogate, we do not deem it abrogating without certainty. And among what proves it:"
"4864","5200 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik, that al-Bara ibn Malik the brother of Anas dueled Marzuban al-Za'rah, stabbed him breaking the saddle-bow, reached him and killed him; his spoil was valued at thirty thousand. After dawn prayer Umar came to us and said to Abu Talhah: We used not to take a fifth from spoils, but al-Bara's spoil has reached wealth and I see only taking its fifth; so we valued it at thirty thousand and paid Umar six thousand. So Umar said: We used not to take fifths, then took a fifth from al-Bara's spoil, proving they may or may not take fifths, and spoils are not obligatory for killers apart from the army. Umar had witnessed Hunayn's statement yet did not deem it universal for every killer with or without the Imam's grant, but specific to that battle. Abu Talhah had also witnessed Hunayn and received spoils there, yet did not deem it contrary to Umar's act; Anas likewise witnessed both and agreed with Umar. So these Companions understood it as battle-specific."
"4865","5201 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Thabit ibn Thawban narrated to me, that his father informed him that he asked Makhul: Is a fifth taken from spoil? He said: Anas ibn Malik narrated to me that al-Bara ibn Malik dueled a Persian notable and killed him and took his spoil; he wrote about it to Umar. Umar wrote to the commander: Take its fifth to you and give him the rest; so the commander took its fifth. So Makhul also held our view on spoils."
"4866","5203 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: I heard a man asking Ibn Abbas about the anfals. Ibn Abbas said: The horse is of the nafal. He repeated his question, and Ibn Abbas said that again. Then the man said: What are the anfals Allah mentioned in His Book? Al-Qasim said: He kept diverting him until he almost expelled him."
"4867","5204 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, that a man asked Ibn Abbas about the anfals, and he said: Spoil and horse are of the anfals."
"4868","5205 - Yunus and Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, they said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to me, he said: al-Zuhri informed me, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I was sitting with him when a man from Iraq came and asked him about spoil. He said: Spoil is of the nafal, and in nafal is the fifth. So Ibn Abbas put a fifth in spoil and made it of the anfals, though he knew what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did at Badr and in giving al-Zubayr the slain's spoil. This shows the Badr practice was not abrogated in his view, and the Zubayr grant rested on a prior Imam statement or another reason. This is the chapter's ruling by reconciling reports. As for reasoning: if the Imam sends a detachment while he stays behind and it gains booty, it is shared with the whole army though they did not fight; and if he grants them a fifth of it, that stands and the rest is shared. So a detachment deserves nothing exclusively, and likewise a killer deserves nothing exclusively."
"4869","5206 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: Duhaym narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Safwan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, from his father, from Awf. 5207 - al-Walid said: And Thawr narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from Jubayr, from Awf ibn Malik, that a reinforcement man accompanied them at Mu'tah; a Roman pressed the Muslims, so the reinforcement man lay under a rock, hamstrung his horse, the Roman fell, and he killed him with his sword. He came with his horse, sword, saddle, bridle, belt and weapons all gilded with gold and jewels to Khalid ibn al-Walid; Khalid took a portion and granted him the rest as extra. I said: O Khalid, what is this? Do you not know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted the killer all the spoil? He said: Yes, but I deemed it large. I said: By Allah I shall report you to the Messenger of Allah. When we came to the Prophet I told him; he summoned Khalid and ordered him to pay the reinforcement man the rest of his spoil. As Khalid turned to pay, I said: How do you see, O Khalid? Did I not fulfill my threat? The Prophet became angry and said: O Khalid, do not give him; then said to me: Will you leave my commanders? For you their fair dealing and upon them their hardship. Do you not see the Prophet first ordered payment then forbade it when Awf spoke so? So spoil is not obligatory for the killer but subject to the Imam's discretion for the Muslims' benefit."
"4870","5208 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: On the day of Badr the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever does such-and-such gets such-and-such. The young men went forth and the elders sat under the banners. At division the youths demanded their extra shares. The elders said: Do not prefer yourselves over us, for we were under the banners and had you been routed we would have been your reserve. So Allah revealed: They ask you about the spoils, reciting until: As your Lord brought you out of your house in truth though a party of believers were averse. That is: obey me in this matter as you saw the good outcome when you came out averse; and he divided equally among them. In this hadith the Prophet withheld from the youths what he had granted them, proving spoils are not obligatory for killers; otherwise he would not withhold. It was withheld because the grant was for the Muslims' benefit, but they abandoned the banners and their guards, leaving the ground for entitlement. And Allah knows best."
"4871","Chapter 7: The share of near relatives. 5209 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrating from Ali ibn Abi Talib, that Fatimah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complaining of the mill's mark on her hand, having heard captives had come to the Prophet; she came asking for a servant but did not meet him, and A'ishah met her and she told her. When the Prophet came she told him. He said: So the Messenger of Allah came to us after we had taken our beds; we rose, and he said: Stay in your places; he sat between us till I felt his feet's coolness on my chest. He said: Shall I guide you to better than what you asked: magnify Allah thirty-four times, glorify thirty-three, and praise thirty-three when you take your beds, for it is better for you than a servant."
"4872","5210 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from Ali, that he said to Fatimah one day: Allah has brought your father abundance and slaves, so go ask him for a servant. She came and mentioned it to him, and he said: By Allah I shall not give you and leave the people of the Suffah folding their stomachs with hunger, finding nothing to spend on them; but I shall sell them and spend on them. Shall I guide you to better than what you asked? Jibril taught me: magnify ten times after each prayer, glorify ten, praise ten, and when you go to bed, then he mentioned the like of Sulayman's hadith."
"4873","5211 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, he said: Ayyash ibn Uqbah narrated to me, he said: al-Fadl ibn Hasan ibn Amr narrated to me, from Ibn Umm al-Hakam, that his mother narrated to him that she and her sister went to Fatimah, then all went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who had returned from an expedition with slaves, and asked him to assign servants. The Messenger of Allah said: The orphans of Badr's people have preceded you. Abu Ja'far said: Some people held that the Prophet's kinsmen have no fixed share of the fifth distinct from others; Allah mentioned them only for poverty and need alongside the poor. As a poor man or orphan loses it when rich, so do kinsmen when rich. Had kinship alone entitled, Fatimah, nearest to him, would have received a servant from the captives, but he consigned her to Allah's remembrance as better. Abu Bakr and Umar after the Prophet divided the whole fifth seeing no distinct right for kinship, with no Companion objecting, so consensus is established. And Ali when he ruled carried the people on that too. And they mentioned what follows."
"4874","5212 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: I asked Abu Ja'far: What did Ali ibn Abi Talib do about the kinsmen's share when he ruled Iraq? He said: By Allah he followed the path of Abu Bakr and Umar. I said: How, while you say what you say? He said: By Allah his family issued only from his view. I said: What prevented him? He said: By Allah he disliked being accused of opposing Abu Bakr and Umar. So Ali carried it as they did, deeming it just; had his view differed, with his knowledge and merit, he would have reversed it. And they also argued with what follows."
"4875","5213 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Qays ibn Muslim, he said: I asked al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali about Allah's saying: Know that of what you gain a fifth is for Allah. He said: Allah's fifth is an opening of speech; to Allah belongs this world and the next; for the Messenger, the kinsmen, orphans and poor. People differed after the Prophet's death: some said the kinsmen's share is for the caliph's kin; some said the Prophet's share is for the caliph after him. Then they agreed to put both shares into horses and equipment in Allah's path, in the rule of Abu Bakr and Umar. They said: Do you not see the Companions agreed it reverted to mounts and arms for fighting the enemy? Had it belonged to the Prophet's kin they would not have been denied, nor would it be hidden from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad. Abdullah ibn Abbas likewise answered Najdah about the kinsmen's share. And they mentioned what follows."
"4876","5214 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, he said: My uncle Juwayriyah ibn Asma narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihab, that Yazid ibn Hurmuz narrated to him that Najdah the chief of Yamamah wrote to Ibn Abbas asking him about the kinsmen's share. Ibn Abbas wrote to him: It is ours; Umar ibn al-Khattab had invited us to marry off our unmarried from it and pay our debtors from it, but we refused except that he hand it all to us, deeming it ours."
"4877","5215 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Qays narrating from Yazid ibn Hurmuz, he said: Najdah ibn Amir wrote to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, asking about the kinsmen's share Allah mentioned and ordained; he wrote to him while I was witness: We deemed them the kin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but our people denied it to us. So Ibn Abbas informs that their people denied it to them, without wronging the denier. This proves what was meant by the Prophet's kinship is poverty and need as we mentioned. These are the proofs of those who deny kinsmen a share of the fifth in the Prophet's time or after. Others opposed them and said: They had a share in the Prophet's time, a fifth of the fifth, which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) might place among whom he willed of them."
"4878","5216 - They mentioned what Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar and Ali ibn Shaybah al-Baghdadiyyan narrated to us, they said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Jubayr ibn Mut'im, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided the kinsmen's share, he gave Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib, and gave nothing to Banu Umayyah and Banu Nawfal. So I and Uthman came to the Messenger of Allah and said: O Messenger of Allah, these Banu Hashim Allah favored through you; what of us and Banu al-Muttalib? We and they are one in lineage. He said: Banu al-Muttalib did not part from me in pre-Islamic times nor in Islam. They said: Since he gave part of kin and deprived equally related kin, Allah meant only some kin, leaving disposal to the Messenger of Allah to place among whom he willed; when he died his discretion ended, as his right to select the choicest spoil for himself ended with death. Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad held this. Others said: Rather the kinsmen meant are Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib; he gave them by Allah's assignation with no right to give Banu Umayyah or Nawfal, for they were not in the verse. With this dispute we must examine each saying to extract the sound one. Beginning with the denial of kinship right, claiming poverty was the cause: we find the Prophet gave all Banu Hashim and Muttalib including rich and poor. Had poverty been the cause, the rich would not share with the poor. So kinship, not poverty, was the cause."
"4879","Chapter 8: Extra share after finishing fighting the enemy and securing booty. 5217 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Ziyad ibn Jariyah, from Habib ibn Maslamah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted a fourth at the outset and a third on return. Abu Ja'far said: Some people held the Imam may grant from secured booty before division as before, arguing with this hadith. Others said: After securing, only from the fifth, for the rest is owned by fighters with no Imam disposal. They said the return third may mean a third of the fifth after the outset fourth. The first group replied: As the outset fourth was before the fifth, so the return third is before the fifth, else mentioning the third is meaningless. They were told: Rather it has sound meaning: the outset fourth of what he may grant, likewise the return third of what he may grant, namely the fifth. The first group said Habib's hadith is narrated in wording supporting us, and mentioned what follows."
"4880","5218 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Thawban informed us, from his father, from Makhul, from Ziyad ibn Jariyah, from Habib ibn Maslamah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would grant a fourth at the outset and a third on return after the fifth."
"4881","5219 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Makhul, from Ziyad ibn Jariyah, from Habib ibn Maslamah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted a third after the fifth."
"4882","5220 - Fahd and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from al-Ala ibn al-Harith, from Makhul, from Ziyad ibn Jariyah, from Habib ibn Maslamah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would grant in a raid a fourth after the fifth, and when returning a third after the fifth. They said: This proves the return third was after the fifth. They were told: This too admits our interpretation. And they also argued with what follows."
"4883","5221 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Makhul, from Abu Sallam, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would grant them a fourth when going out and a third when returning. They were told: This too admits the meaning of Habib's hadith as transmitted by most from Makhul. Ubadah may also mean returning from one battle to another; if the third granted is before the fifth, that is permissible in our view too, for it urges the people against the enemy. But after fighting ends, no extra grant is permissible, for no Muslim benefit remains. The first group also argued with what follows."
"4884","5222 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar and Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Majid al-Hanafi narrated to us, they said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from his father, he said: When we neared the polytheists Abu Bakr ordered us and we launched a raid; Abu Bakr granted me as extra a Fazari woman I brought from the raid. I brought her to Madinah and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked her of me; I gave her to him and he ransomed Muslim captives with her. The others' proof against them: the hadith does not state whether Abu Bakr granted before or after fighting ended, so no proof. And they also argued with what follows."
"4885","5223 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a detachment including Ibn Umar; they gained much booty, each man's share being twelve camels, and each was granted an extra camel besides. They said: Ibn Umar reports they were granted after their shares without the Prophet disapproving. They were told: You have no proof; it is proof against you, for their shares reached twelve camels and they were granted a camel each, proving the grant was from other than their shares, namely the fifth. So no proof for granting from other than the fifth. Since none of the first group's reports necessitates their view, we examined the other group's reports."
"4886","5224 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Makhul, from Abu Sallam, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took on the day of Hunayn a tuft of wool from a camel's side and said: O people, nothing is lawful to me of what Allah has bestowed upon you except the fifth, and the fifth is returned among you; so render the thread and needle. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked extra shares and said: Let the strong of the believers return to the weak. Do you not see he said: Nothing is lawful to me except the fifth? So all but the fifth belongs to fighters with no Imam disposal. Then he disliked extra shares, saying the strong should not be preferred for strength over the weak but all equal. It is inconceivable he granted of the shares he disliked; so the unobjectionable extra share is from the fifth. Thus what Ubadah reported he granted was from the fifth. And there was also narrated from the Prophet what proves this school."
"4887","5225 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from Abu al-Juwayriyah, from Ma'n ibn Yazid al-Sulami, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No extra share except after the fifth. The meaning of: except after the fifth, in our view, is: until the fifth is divided; once divided, the fighters' right of four-fifths stands apart. So the Imam's grant thereafter is from the fifth, not from the fighters' four-fifths. And what proves it is what follows."
"4888","5226 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Anas ibn Malik was with Ubaydullah ibn Abi Bakrah on a raid; they captured prisoners, and Ubaydullah wanted to give Anas from the captives before division. Anas said: No, but divide then give me from the fifth. Ubaydullah said: No, except from all the booty; Anas refused to accept from him, and Ubaydullah refused to give him anything from the fifth."
"4889","5227 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Kahmas ibn al-Hasan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Anas, the like of it. So Anas, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, accepted extra share only from the fifth, and the like was narrated from Jabalah ibn Amr."
"4890","5228 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ibn Lahiah, from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that they were with Muawiyah ibn Hudayj on the western campaign; he granted the people extra shares, and with us were Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who did not object except Jabalah ibn Amr."
"4891","5229 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ibn Lahiah, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran, he said: I asked Sulayman ibn Yasar about extra shares in raids. He said: I saw none do it but Ibn Khudayj; he granted us in Ifriqiyah half after the fifth, with many early Emigrant Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) present, but Jabalah ibn Amr refused to take anything. If one says the Companions besides Jabalah accepted, we reply: True, and we do not deny people differed; some permitted the Imam extra grants before the fifth and some not, and the Companions differed therein. We cited Anas and Jabalah only to show our saying has support among the Companions. If one cites Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, we mention what follows."
"4892","5230 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from a man of his people called Shabr ibn Alqamah, he said: I dueled a man on the day of Qadisiyyah and killed him; his spoil reached twelve thousand, and Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas granted it to me as extra. It was said: Sa'd may have granted while fighting had not ended, which is our view too; or after its end, possibly from the fifth. If from other than the fifth, that is the cited disagreement, so no proof for either side. We must then uncover the chapter by reasoning: the Imam saying during battle: Whoever kills a slain one, his spoil is his, is permissible, as is: Whoever kills gets so many dirhams. But: Whoever kills gets a tenth of our gains, is impermissible, for then all booty might go to fighters voiding Allah's fifth. So pre-battle grants are only in what the grantee himself takes by his sword, or as hire-like payment such as ten dirhams. Grants from what others take are impermissible. By analogy post-battle grants from secured shares others own are impermissible except from the fifth."
"4893","Chapter 9: Reinforcements arriving after fighting ends in the abode of war before the army returns - do they share or not? 5231 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash informed us, from Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydi, from Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri, that Anbasah ibn Said informed him that he heard Abu Hurayrah narrating to Said ibn al-As. Abu Hurayrah said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Aban ibn Said with a detachment from Madinah toward Najd; Aban and his companions came to the Prophet at Khaybar after our conquest, their horses' saddle-girths of fiber, and Aban said: Apportion to us, O Messenger of Allah. Abu Hurayrah said: I said: Apportion them nothing, O Prophet of Allah. Aban said: You there, O wabr coming down upon us from a lote-tree top. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sit, O Aban, and apportioned them nothing. Abu Ja'far said: Some people held only those present at the battle share. Others said: All who witnessed it and those absent for its causes share: one who set out intending it but joined the Imam in the abode of war before his exit shares. They argued with what follows."
"4894","5232 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Kulayb ibn Wa'il narrated to us, he said: Hani ibn Qays narrated to me, from Habib ibn Abi Mulaykah, he said: I was sitting beside Ibn Umar when a man came and said: Did Uthman witness Badr? He said: No, but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on Badr day: Uthman has gone on Allah's errand and His Messenger's, so he struck for him a share, striking for no other absentee."
"4895","5233 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah ibn Amr al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, from Kulayb ibn Wa'il, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it except here. Do you not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck for Uthman a share of Badr's spoils though absent, because absent on Allah's and His Messenger's errand, making him like one present? Likewise whoever is absent from the battle on the Imam's business - sent to another front, or sent to Islamic territory for arms and men returning after booty is gained, or turned back by the Imam for Muslim affairs - shares as one present. On this ground, in our view, the Prophet gave Uthman a Badr share; otherwise he would not, as he gave no other absentee. Badr's spoils belonged to those present and to whoever offered himself but was diverted by the Imam to other Muslim business. As for Abu Hurayrah's hadith, the Prophet had sent Aban toward Najd, and Aban came after Khaybar's conquest seeking a share though he neither witnessed nor was diverted from it but was on a separate mission; so he was refused."
"4896","5234 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, he said: I heard Tariq ibn Shihab say that the people of Basrah raided Nahawand and the people of Kufah reinforced them, and they triumphed. The Basrans wanted not to share with the Kufans, while Ammar was over the Kufans; a man of Banu Utarid said: O maimed one, do you seek to share our spoils? He said: My ear will grow back. He wrote about it to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and Umar wrote: Booty belongs to whoever witnessed the battle. They said: So Umar held booty is for battle witnesses, agreeing with us. They were told: Nahawand may have become Islamic territory with booty secured and divided before the Kufans arrived, in which we too say it belongs to witnesses, undisputed. If the Kufans joined them before leaving polytheist territory after fighting ended, Umar's reply is countered by the claim of Ammar ibn Yasir and other Companions among them demanding shares; neither saying outweighs the other without proof from Book, Sunnah, or sound reasoning. Considering: detachments sent from the war abode share what they gain with their comrades alike, for all offered themselves as those captured did. By analogy, whoever offers himself as battle witnesses did shares like them under the stated conditions."
"4897","Chapter 10: Conquered land - what should the Imam do with it? 5235 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Hisham ibn Sa'd informed me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, he said: Were it not that people would be left destitute with nothing, no town Allah conquers but I would divide it as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided Khaybar."
"4898","5236 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sa'd, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say the like of it. Some people held that when the Imam conquers land by force he must divide it like other booty and may not retain it, arguing with this hadith. Others said: The Imam has choice: if he wills he takes a fifth and divides four-fifths, and if he wills he leaves it as kharaj land without dividing."
"4899","5237 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us with that, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Abu Hanifah and Sufyan with that, and it is the saying of Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. Among their proof is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), including what follows."
"4900","5238 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to me, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from Abu al-Qasim, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted Khaybar for half, then sent Ibn Rawahah to divide with them."
"4901","5239 - Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that in the year of Khaybar the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dealt with Khaybar's people for half of what came out of crops."
"4902","5240 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Awn al-Ziyadi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, he said: Allah bestowed Khaybar as fay'; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left them as they were and made it between him and them. So he sent Abdullah ibn Rawahah who assessed it upon them."
"4903","5241 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So it is established the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not divide all Khaybar but divided a part, as Umar cited in the first hadith, and left a part undivided, as narrated from Ibn Abbas, Ibn Umar, and Jabir. What was divided was al-Shiqq and al-Bitah, the rest left; so he divided with right to divide, and left with right to leave. So conquered lands follow: the Imam divides if he sees Muslim benefit, as the Prophet divided part of Khaybar, or leaves if he sees benefit, as the Prophet left part, acting upon his best judgment for Muslims. Umar likewise left the Sawad as kharaj land for present and future Muslims. If one says Umar acted only with universal Muslim consent, evidenced by jizyah on their necks - either as tribute for being their slaves or as jizyah on freemen to spare blood - we see their women, elders, disabled and children spared though able to earn more than some adults, proving the levy is for dhimmah not ownership. And he set varying dues on vineyards and wheat, neglecting palms; this cannot be sale of years or of what one lacks, impossible for Umar. Rather he granted them kharaj tenure. People accepted it, validating his act. They said: So Sawad's people own their land as freemen by prior cause with the conquerors' consent; without it they would remain owned. Likewise, they say, every force-conquered land must be divided, a fifth for Allah and four-fifths for conquerors, unless they willingly leave it as Sawad's conquerors did for Umar. The others' proof: Had Sawad been so, Allah's fifth would be due among its folk, which the Imam may not assign to dhimmis; yet all Sawad was in their hands as dhimmis. So Umar acted on another ground: no fifth was due. Likewise he freed their persons, confirmed their lands, and imposed kharaj. So the Imam may remove Muslim bondage from persons and Muslim ownership from lands, granting them to locals with kharaj, as Umar did before the Companions."
"4904","5242 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: When Jarir ibn Abdullah and Ammar ibn Yasir came with Muslims to Umar ibn al-Khattab, Umar said to Jarir: O Jarir, by Allah were I not a divider held accountable, you would remain upon what I apportioned you, but I deem we return it to the Muslims; so he returned it. A fourth of the Sawad belonged to Bajilah, so he took it from them and gave them eighty dinars."
"4905","5243 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah informed us, he said: Ismail narrated to us, from Qays, from Jarir, he said: Umar had given Bajilah a fourth of the Sawad, which we held three years. Then Jarir came to Umar with Ammar ibn Yasir, and Umar said: By Allah, were I not a divider held accountable, I would leave you upon what I gave you, but I deem we return it to the Muslims; so he did, and Umar compensated me with eighty dinars. They said: This shows Umar had divided the Sawad then compensated them to return it. It was said: Rather Umar may have granted only a portion to Bajilah, then took it for the Muslims compensating from Muslim funds. That portion became Muslims' by compensation, the rest under the prior ruling; otherwise the Sawad would be tithe land not kharaj land. And if they argue with what follows."
"4906","5244 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to me, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: A woman of Bajilah came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: My people accepted from you of the Sawad what I did not accept, and I shall not accept until you fill my palm with gold or my camel with food, or words to this meaning; so Umar did that with her. They were told: That too, in our view, concerns the portion Umar had handed to Bajilah which they owned, then he sought to reclaim it with their goodwill; that woman's share did not leave except with what pleased her, so Umar gave her what she demanded until she was pleased, and she surrendered her share as the rest surrendered theirs. This, in our view, is the whole chapter by reports and reasoning, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Sufyan, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all. And there was narrated from Umar about Egypt's land also what follows."
"4907","5245 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Qays al-Sakuni, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: When Amr ibn al-As conquered Egypt he gathered the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with him and consulted them about dividing its land among those who witnessed it, as he divided their spoils among them and as the Messenger of Allah divided Khaybar among its witnesses, or leaving it standing until referring to the Commander of the Faithful. Some among them, including al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, said: By Allah that is not up to you nor Umar; it is land Allah conquered for us, our horses and men charged upon it, we encompassed what is in it, so its division is no less entitled than its wealth's division. Others said: We shall not divide it until we refer to the Commander of the Faithful. They agreed to write to Umar informing him of their views. Umar wrote to them: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful: What you agreed upon to usurp the Muslims' grants and provisions of those who raid the enemy and disbelievers has reached me; if I divide it among you, those after you will have no resource to strengthen against your enemy. Were it not for what I mount in Allah's path, defend Muslims' provisions, and grant their weak and pensioners, I would divide it among you; so leave it as fay' for remaining Muslims until the last raiding band perishes. Peace upon you. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is proof on conquered lands as we mentioned, that their ruling differs from other seized wealth. If one says the Companions mentioned the Prophet divided Khaybar among its witnesses, negating proof for leaving lands standing - we reply: This hadith did not detail all the Prophet did at Khaybar, and other reports clarified it."
"4908","5246 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Said, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided Khaybar into halves, half for his contingencies and needs, and half among the Muslims, dividing it among them into eighteen shares. In this hadith is explanation of what the Prophet did at Khaybar: he reserved half for his needs and divided half among its Muslim witnesses. What he reserved is what he gave the Jews as sharecropping, per Ibn Umar and Jabir's hadiths, which Umar later divided among Muslims when he expelled the Jews from Khaybar. And what we explained strengthens Abu Hanifah and Sufyan's view on reserving lands and leaving division when the Imam sees fit."
"4909","Chapter 11: A man needing to fight upon a mount from the booty. 5247 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed me, from Ja'far ibn Rabiah, from Abu Marzuq al-Tujibi, from Hanash ibn Abdullah, from Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said in the year of Khaybar: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not take a mount from the spoils and ride it until when he has worn it he returns it to the spoils; and whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not wear a garment from the spoils until when he has worn it out he returns it to the spoils."
"4910","5248 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Rabiah ibn Sulaym al-Tujibi, from Hanash, from Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Some people, among them al-Awza'i, held that a man takes arms from booty to fight only in the thick of battle while needy, and does not delay return past war's end exposing it to ruin and price loss, arguing with this hadith. Others opposed them, among them Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, in what follows."
"4911","5249 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Yusuf, and they said: There is no harm for a man to take arms from the booty when needy, without the Imam's permission, and fight with it until war ends, then return it to the booty. Abu Yusuf said: What the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said cited by al-Awza'i has reached us, but the Prophet's hadith has meanings and aspects only one aided by Allah grasps. This hadith, in our view, concerns one who does that while rich, keeping his own mount and garment, or taking intending treachery. As for a Muslim in the war abode with no mount, and Muslims have no spare but booty mounts, unable to walk - Muslims may not abandon him; he may ride it whether they will or not. Likewise garments, likewise arms, even clearer. Do you not see if Muslims' swords break or are lost, with no Muslim spare, they may take swords from booty and fight while in the war abode? If they need them not in the thick but two days later when the enemy raids, shall they face the enemy unarmed? How shall they act - surrender? This view weakens Muslim strategy. How is it lawful in the thick yet forbidden after? And:"
"4912","5250 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Yusuf, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi al-Mujalid, from Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa the Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Khaybar; one of us would come to booty food and take his need. Since food may be taken, eaten and consumed for Muslim need, likewise mounts, arms and garments may be taken and used for need, so that Ibn Abi Awfa's hadith and Ruwayfi's hadith do not contradict. And it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them, and we adopt it."
"4913","Chapter 12: A man embraces Islam in the war abode with more than four wives. 5251 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Khalaf narrated to us, he said: Abd al-A'la ibn Abd al-A'la al-Sami narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that Ghaylan ibn Salamah embraced Islam with ten wives under him, and the Prophet, peace be upon him, said to him: Take four of them. Abu Ja'far said: Some people held that when a man embraces Islam with more than four wives married in the war abode as a polytheist, he chooses four to keep and parts the rest, whether married in one contract or separate contracts; among them Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on him. Others said: If married in one contract, all their marriage is void and he is separated from all; if in separate contracts, the first four's marriage stands and he is separated from the rest; among them Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf. Their proof: this hadith is disconnected, not as Abd al-A'la and his Basran colleagues narrated from Ma'mar. Its origin is what follows."
"4914","5252 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, that he said: It has reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man of Thaqif who embraced Islam with more than four wives: Keep four of them and part the rest."
"4915","5253 - Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Ibn Shihab, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4916","5254 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Ibn Shihab, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So this is the origin of this hadith, as Malik narrated from al-Zuhri, and as Abd al-Razzaq and Ibn Uyaynah narrated from Ma'mar from al-Zuhri; Uqayl also narrated from al-Zuhri what indicates the source al-Zuhri took it from."
"4917","5255 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abu Salih Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: It reached me from Uthman ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Suwayd that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ghaylan ibn Salamah al-Thaqafi when he embraced Islam with ten wives under him: Take four of them and part the rest. So Uqayl clarifies from al-Zuhri the outlet of this hadith: he took it from what reached him from Uthman ibn Muhammad, from the Prophet. It is inconceivable al-Zuhri had it from Salim from his father yet abandons that proof to argue with a report from Uthman ibn Muhammad. Rather Ma'mar erred here: he had from al-Zuhri on Ghaylan's story two hadiths, this one of them, and the other from Salim from his father that Ghaylan divorced his wives and divided his wealth, and Umar ordered him to take back his wives and wealth, saying: Had you died upon that I would stone your grave as Abu Righal's grave was stoned in pre-Islamic times. So Ma'mar mistakenly attached this hadith about Umar's speech's chain to the hadith with the Prophet's speech, corrupting its chain. Even if sound as Abd al-A'la narrated, it would still be no proof against Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf, for Ghaylan's marrying was in pre-Islamic times, as Said ibn Abi Arubah clarified from Ma'mar in this hadith."
"4918","5256 - Khallad ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Shuja' narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah informed us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of Ahmad ibn Dawud's hadith, adding: He had married them in pre-Islamic times. So Ghaylan's marrying those wives present when he embraced Islam was at a time when marrying that number was lawful and marriage established, each wife's standing like the tenth's. Then Allah ordained another ruling forbidding more than four, a supervening prohibition upon Ghaylan's marriage; so the Prophet ordered him to keep the number Allah permitted and part the rest, like a man with four wives divorcing one: he chooses one to apply the divorce to and keeps the other. So say Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf. As for one marrying ten women after Allah's prohibition in one contract, he contracted a void contract with no marriage established. Do you not see if he married a close unmarriageable kin in the war abode as a polytheist then embraced Islam, she is not left with him, though contracted there as a polytheist? So his ruling reverts to Muslim marriages contracted in Islamic territory. If he married the ten in one contract, all void; if in separate contracts, the first four stand and the rest are void. If one says Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf abandoned their principle concerning a warrior captive with four wives captured with him, ruling all their marriages void and separating them - whereas upon Ghaylan's analogy he should choose two and part two, since four was lawful and bondage supervened forbidding more than two, as the ruling above four supervened for Ghaylan - we reply: They did not abandon their principle. That man married the four when more than two was already forbidden for a slave; marrying as a warrior then being captured reverts to a prior prohibition, as if he married them after becoming a slave. It is like one marrying two small girls then a woman suckles them together: both are separated with no choice, for the suckling prohibition supervened after marriage. Likewise supervening bondage differs from Ghaylan's case, for Ghaylan's prohibition did not precede his marriage but supervened after its full establishment, entailing choice as in divorce. And if they argue with what follows."
"4919","5257 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla informed us, from Humaydah ibn al-Shamardal, from al-Harith ibn Qays, he said: I embraced Islam with eight wives under me, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me to choose four of them."
"4920","5258 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah informed us, from one of al-Harith ibn Qays's sons, from al-Harith ibn Qays, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. It was said to him: That admits what we mentioned on Ghaylan's hadith. It is also possible the Prophet meant by: Choose four of them, that is: choose four and marry them anew. Neither meaning is proven by this hadith. And if they argue with what follows."
"4921","5259 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad and Hassan ibn Ghalib narrated to us, they said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Abu Wahb al-Jayshani, from al-Dahhak ibn Fayruz al-Daylami, from his father, he said: I embraced Islam with two sisters under me; I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: Divorce one of them."
"4922","5260 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from his father, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Wahb al-Jayshani, from al-Dahhak ibn Fayruz al-Daylami, from his father, he said: I embraced Islam with two sisters under me; I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him, and he said: Divorce whichever of them you will. It was said to them: This entails choice as you mentioned, clearer than Harith ibn Qays's hadith. But the Prophet may have given choice because his marriage was in pre-Islamic times before Allah forbade more than four; so its meaning is like Ghaylan ibn Salamah's hadith. So what Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf held is established, and what Muhammad ibn al-Hasan held is invalidated. And some predecessors held what Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf held."
"4923","5261 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Khalaf narrated to us, he said: Ghundar or Abd al-A'la narrated to us, from Said, from Qatadah, he said: He takes the first, second, third, and fourth."
"4924","Chapter 13: A warrior woman embraces Islam in the war abode and comes to Islamic territory, then her husband comes after as a Muslim. 5262 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned his daughter Zaynab to Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi' upon the first marriage, after three years."
"4925","5263 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned to Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl Umm Hakim bint al-Harith ibn Hisham after months or near a year. Abu Ja'far said: Some people held that when a woman embraces Islam in the war abode and comes to us Muslim, then her husband comes after and reaches her while in waiting period, she remains his wife as before; if he reaches her only after it ends, he has no claim. They argued with this hadith. Others said: He has no claim in either case; her exit from the war abode severs the bond and separates her. They argued with what follows."
"4926","5264 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Hafs, meaning Ibn Ghiyath, narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah returned Zaynab to Abu al-As with a new marriage."
"4927","5265 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hafs narrated to us, from Dawud, from al-Sha'bi, the like of it. They said: In this hadith of Abdullah ibn Amr is contrary to Ibn Abbas's hadith. Abdullah ibn Amr was joined on that by Amir al-Sha'bi, with his knowledge of the Prophet's campaigns. So this is worthier than its opponent, for reasons we shall explain, Allah willing. Among their proof against the first view: Ibn Abbas's hadith only states the Prophet returned her to Abu al-As upon the first marriage, with no evidence he returned her because she was in waiting period, nor what the ruling then was for a polytheist woman embracing Islam with a polytheist husband - does it separate her or leave her his wife? Ibn Abbas's hadith would prove the first view only if it stated he returned her because he reached her in waiting period. Since the cause for returning her is unclear, it may be the waiting period, or it may be that Islam then did not separate her nor remove her prior status."
"4928","5266 - Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Abdah ibn Abdullah ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abu Tawbah al-Rabi' ibn Nafi' narrated to me, he said: I said to Muhammad ibn al-Hasan: Whence came their disagreement about Zaynab? Some said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned her to Abu al-As upon the first marriage. Others said: He returned her with a new marriage. Do you deem each heard from the Prophet what he stated? Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said: Their disagreement did not come thus; rather Allah only forbade believing women returning to disbelievers in the chapter of al-Mumtahanah, after it had been lawful. Abdullah ibn Amr knew that prohibition, then saw the Prophet had returned Zaynab to Abu al-As after knowing her prohibition to him by Allah's forbidding believing women to disbelievers, so he deemed it only with a new marriage and said: He returned her with a new marriage. Abdullah ibn Abbas did not know of Allah's forbidding believing women to disbelievers until he knew of the Prophet returning Zaynab to Abu al-As, so he said: He returned her upon the first marriage, for in his view no annulment stood between their Islams. Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Hence their disagreement, not from differing hearings from the Prophet about first or new marriage. Abu Ja'far said: Muhammad did well in this, and reconciling reports on this sound meaning validates Abdullah ibn Amr's statement. Evidence: Ibn Abbas used to say concerning a Christian woman embracing Islam in Islamic territory with a disbelieving husband what follows."
"4929","5267 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, concerning a Jewish or Christian woman under a Christian or Jew who herself embraces Islam, he said: They are separated; Islam is exalted and nothing is exalted above it."
"4930","5268 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it, except he did not say: Islam is exalted and nothing is exalted above it. Is it conceivable that in his view a Christian woman embracing Islam in Islamic territory with a Christian husband separates, without awaiting his Islam till her waiting period ends, while a non-Scripture warrior woman embracing Islam in the war abode then coming to us Muslim is awaited for her husband to join her Muslim within her waiting period? This is absurd; for if her Islam in Islamic territory separates her from her dhimmi Christian husband, her Islam in the war abode with exit to Islamic territory abandoning her polytheist husband separates her all the more. So Ibn Abbas deemed the bond severed by the woman's Islam, not by exiting waiting period. Once that is established from him, his abandonment of the Prophet's ruling returning Zaynab upon the first marriage for its opposite is only after its abrogation was established with him. This is the chapter by reports. As for reasoning: a woman embracing Islam with a disbelieving husband reaches a state where initiating marriage with her is impermissible, being Muslim and he disbeliever. We considered supervening bars to initiating marriage: Allah forbade foster-sisters; one marrying a girl with no fosterage between them, then his mother suckles her, forbids and dissolves the marriage - supervening suckling equals prior suckling. But some rulings differ whether prior or supervening: Allah forbade marrying a woman in her waiting period, and Muslims agree waiting from intercourse in a void marriage also bars, like from a valid one. But a wife violated under doubt while married, incurring waiting, does not thereby separate; supervening waiting is not like prior waiting. So past and future rulings were distinguished. We considered the Islam-embracing wife: does she separate, with future and past alike as in suckling, or not, with supervening Islam unlike prior Islam, as with waiting? We found supervening waiting entails no separation at its incidence or after; the cited suckling entails separation at once with nothing awaited; and all agree supervening Islam entails separation. Some said: at her Islam's moment, Ibn Abbas's saying. Others said: only after Islam is offered to the husband and he refuses, else she remains his wife, Umar ibn al-Khattab's saying. Others said: she is his wife unless Hijrah territory removes her, Ali ibn Abi Talib's saying, whose chains we shall bring at the chapter's end, Allah willing. Since the wife's supervening Islam entails separation when established, its ruling resembles suckling more than waiting. Since suckling entails separation at once without awaiting her waiting's exit, so does Islam. This is the reasoning view: the woman separates from her husband by her Islam, in Islamic or war territory. Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad opposed this, saying of a warrior woman embracing Islam in the war abode with a disbelieving husband: she remains his wife unless she menstruates thrice or exits to Islamic territory, separating by whichever occurs. They said reasoning required immediate separation by her Islam. And they said: if she embraces Islam with her husband in Islamic territory, she remains his wife until the judge offers him Islam and he accepts, remaining under him, or refuses, and they are separated. They said reasoning required immediate separation, but we followed what was narrated from Umar. And they mentioned what follows."
"4931","5269 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from al-Saffah, from Dawud ibn Kurdus who said: There was a man among us from Banu Taghlib, a Christian, with a Christian wife under him, then she embraced Islam, so the matter was raised to Umar. He said to him: Become Muslim, otherwise I will separate you two. He said to him: I have not abandoned this except out of shyness before the Arabs, lest they say: he became Muslim for the sake of a woman's private part. He said: So Umar separated the two of them."
"4932","5270 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Hilal ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani narrated to us, from al-Saffah, from Dawud ibn Kurdus al-Tha'labi, from Umar, similar to it. So they followed what was reported from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding this case of one whose wife embraced Islam in the abode of Islam, and they set for one whose wife embraced Islam in the abode of war a waiting period: if he embraces Islam within it, otherwise separation falls between him and his wife, in place of the offer they would have presented to him had he been in the abode of Islam, and it is the waiting period, unless the woman leaves before that for the abode of Islam, then the period is cut off thereby and irrevocable separation becomes due. And we in this matter are upon what we reported from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, of the obligation of irrevocable separation by Islam, the moment it comes from the woman. As for what was reported from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding that, then what..."
"4933","5271 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that Ali said: He is more entitled to her marriage as long as she remains in her land of emigration. And it was reported from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, regarding the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returning Zaynab to Abu al-As, that that is abrogated, and they differed as to what abrogated it."
"4934","5272 - Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mu'addib narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, from al-Zuhri, that Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi' was taken captive on the day of Badr, and he was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he returned his daughter to him. Al-Zuhri said: And this was before the obligations were revealed, meaning the daughter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he returned her to her husband."
"4935","5273 - And Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Ali narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned his daughter to Abu al-As. Qatadah said: This was before Surah Bara'ah was revealed."
"4936","Chapter 14 - The Chapter of Ransom. 5274 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from his father who said: Abu Bakr granted me as booty a woman from Fazarah, whom I brought from a raid, so I brought her to Madinah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked her of me as a gift, so he ransomed with her some Muslim persons."
"4937","5275 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Umayr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain similar to it. And he added: They were captives in Makkah."
"4938","5276 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from his uncle, from Imran ibn Husayn, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ransomed with one man of the enemy two men of the Muslims."
"4939","5277 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ayyub informed us, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ransomed two men of the Muslims with one man of the polytheists from Banu Uqayl."
"4940","5278 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mujalid informed us, he said: Abu al-Waddak, Jabr ibn Nawf, informed us, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri who said: We captured a young boy and we wanted to ransom with them, so we asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we said: O Messenger of Allah, a man has a slave girl and he has intercourse with her, then he practices withdrawal from her fearing she may conceive by him? He said: Do whatever seems fit to you, for whatever matter is decreed will be, even if you dislike it. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to the view that there is no harm in ransoming Muslim captives in the hands of the polytheists with those of the people of war whom the Muslims have owned, men and women, and they argued with these reports. And among those who held this view was Abu Yusuf, may Allah's mercy be upon him. And others disliked ransoming with one upon whom Muslim ownership has fallen, because he has thereby gained a covenant by the Muslims owning him, so it is disliked to return him as a belligerent after he had a covenant. And they said: The ransom mentioned in these reports was only at a time when there was no harm in ransoming with those of the people of war who had embraced Islam, returning them to the polytheists on condition they return to the Muslims those they had captured, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made peace with the people of Makkah on condition he return to them whoever came to him from them, even if he was a Muslim. And among what clarifies that that is so..."
"4941","5279 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn who said: Thaqif captured two men of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) captured a man from Banu Amir ibn Sa'sa'ah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was bound. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him and said: For what are you detained? He said: For the crime of your allies. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went on, then he called him and he came to him. The captive said to him: I am a Muslim. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had you said it while you controlled your own affair, you would have succeeded with complete success. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went on, then he called him again and he came. He said: I am hungry, so feed me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I will fulfill your need. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ransomed him with the two men whom Thaqif had captured."
"4942","5280 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn who said: Al-Adba belonged to a man of Banu Uqayl who was captured, and al-Adba was taken from him, and he was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: O Muhammad, for what do you seize me, and seize the foremost camel of the pilgrims, though I have embraced Islam? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: I seize you for the crime of your allies. Thaqif had captured two men of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon a donkey with a velvet cloth over it. He said: O Muhammad, I am hungry so feed me, and thirsty so give me drink. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is your need. Then the man was ransomed with a man, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept al-Adba for his own riding. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith is explicit; Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reported in it that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ransomed that captive after he had acknowledged Islam. And they have agreed that that is abrogated, and that the imam may not ransom a Muslim captive with war captives in his hands who have embraced Islam, and that the saying of Allah the Exalted, {Do not return them to the disbelievers}, has abrogated returning any person of Islam to the disbelievers. Since that is established, and it is established that one who comes to us from them under covenant is not returned to the disbelievers, and that covenant prohibits what Islam prohibits of the blood and property of its people, and that we must prevent its people from breaking it and returning to the abode of war, just as Muslims are prevented from breaking their Islam and leaving for the abode of war upon that, and since whomever we seize of the people of war and own becomes by our owning him a covenant-bound person to us, and had we freed him he would not become a belligerent again, and we may take from him the jizyah as we take from the rest of our covenant people, and we must protect him from what protects them from it, and it was forbidden for us to ransom with our disbelieving slaves born in our land because of the covenant they have gained - then by analogy this belligerent, when we capture him and he becomes covenant-bound to us with our ownership falling upon him, it is forbidden for us to ransom with him and return him to the hands of the polytheists. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, may Allah's mercy be upon him."
"4943","Chapter 15 - What the polytheists seized of Muslim property: do they own it or not? 5281 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn who said: Al-Adba was among the foremost camels of the pilgrims. The polytheists raided the pasture of Madinah and took it, including al-Adba, and they captured a Muslim woman. When they camped they would release their camels in their courtyards. One night, the woman rose while they slept, and she placed her hand on no camel but it growled, until she came to al-Adba - she came to a docile she-camel - so she mounted it and headed toward Madinah, and she vowed: if Allah saves her upon it, she will slaughter it. When she arrived, the she-camel was recognized, so they brought her to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the woman informed him of her vow. He said: Evil is what you have repaid it - or fulfilled to it - there is no fulfillment of a vow in disobedience to Allah, nor in what the son of Adam does not own. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to the view that the war people's spoils of Muslim property are returned to the Muslims before division and after it, because in their view the people of war do not own Muslim property by taking it from the Muslims. They said: The Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying to the woman who took al-Adba, there is no vow for the son of Adam in what he does not own, proves she had not owned it by taking it from the people of war, and that the people of war had not owned it against the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Others opposed them, saying: Whatever the people of war take of Muslim property and secure in their land, they have owned it and Muslim ownership of it has ceased. When the Muslims then raid them and take it from them, if its owner comes before it is divided he takes it for nothing, and if he comes after it was divided he takes it for its value. Their proof in the first hadith was that the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, there is no vow for the son of Adam in what he does not own, was only before the woman owned the she-camel, because she said that while in the abode of war, and all people say one who takes something from the people of war and does not move with it to the abode of Islam has not secured it nor owned it, and his ownership does not fall upon it until he leaves with it to the abode of Islam, and when he does so he has taken it as spoil and owned it. For this reason the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said regarding the woman what he said, because she vowed before she owned it, that if Allah saves her upon it she would slaughter it, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: there is no vow for the son of Adam in what he does not own, because her vow was from her before she owned it. This is the aspect of this hadith, and in it is no proof whether the polytheists had owned it against the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by taking it from him or not, nor whether the people of war own what they raid of Muslim property or not. And that in which the proof of that lies is what..."
"4944","5282 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Tamim ibn Tarafah al-Ta'i, that the enemy seized a camel belonging to a man, then a man among them bought it, and he came with it and its owner recognized it, so he disputed with him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: If you wish, give him the price for which he bought it and it is yours, otherwise it is his."
"4945","5283 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Hafs al-Isbahani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Simak, from Tamim ibn Tarafah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it. This is the report in which lies the aspect of the ruling in this chapter, how it is. And this was reported from a group of the early ones. Among what was reported from them regarding that is what..."
"4946","5284 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Raja ibn Haywah, from Qabisah ibn Dhu'ayb, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said regarding what the polytheists secured and the Muslims then captured and its owner recognized it, he said: If he reaches it before it is divided it is his, and if the arrows have been cast over it then he has nothing."
"4947","5285 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Azhar ibn Sa'd al-Samman narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Raja ibn Haywah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab and Abu Ubaydah said that."
"4948","5286 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ibn Lahi'ah, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Zayd ibn Thabit, similar to it."
"4949","5287 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Za'idah ibn Qudamah, from Layth, from Mujahid who said: When the polytheists seize Muslim captives, then the Muslims capture them, its owner has returned it to him before division, so it is his; and if he gains power over it after division, he is more entitled to it for the price for which it was taken."
"4950","5288 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Asadi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Za'idah said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a slave boy of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, fled to the enemy, and the Muslims prevailed over him, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned him, and he had not been divided."
"4951","5289 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hammad informed us, from Ayyub and Habib and Hisham, from Muhammad, that a man bought a slave girl from the enemy and had intercourse with her, and she bore him a child. Then her owner came and disputed with him before Shurayh. He said: The Muslim is more entitled to return to his brother for the price. He said: But she has borne him a child. He said: Free her; the judgment of the commander Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4952","5290 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Hammad informed us, from al-Hajjaj, from Ibrahim and Amir. He said: And Qatadah said, from Umar, that they said regarding what the polytheists seized of the Muslims, then the Muslims seized it afterwards, they said: If its owner comes before it is divided, he is more entitled to it."
"4953","5291 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Hammad informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that the polytheists seized a horse of Abdullah ibn Umar, then the Muslims seized it afterwards, so Abdullah ibn Umar took it before the divider divided. Nafi' did not mention here before the divider divides except that the ruling after division occurs is otherwise in his view."
"4954","5292 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Qatadah, from Khilas, that Ali ibn Abi Talib said: Whoever buys what the enemy has secured, it is permissible."
"4955","5293 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and al-Hasan, they both said: Whatever the polytheists secure is fay for the Muslims; nothing of it is returned. All of those from whom we reported these reports affirmed the ownership of the polytheists over what they secured of Muslim property; their disagreement is only over what comes after that. Al-Hasan and al-Zuhri said: Whatever the polytheists secure of Muslim property, then the Muslims gain power over it afterwards, its owner has no way to it. Shurayh, Mujahid, Ibrahim and Amir opposed them in that, along with those before them of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Umar, Ali, Abu Ubaydah, Ibn Umar and Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah's pleasure be upon them all. What they said of that is odd beside what we reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Tamim ibn Tarafah, and that is worthier than what we went to, even though analogy opposes what both groups went to. That is because we saw the Muslims capturing the people of war and their property, owning their property as they own their persons, while when the polytheists capture Muslims they do not own their persons. By analogy they should not own their property, and the ruling of Muslim property should be like the ruling of their persons, just as the ruling of polytheist property was like the ruling of their persons. But we were prevented from that by what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled, and by what the Muslims after him ruled. When what they ruled of that was established, we looked at what was disputed of the ruling of what the Muslims gained power over therein and took from the hands of the polytheists, and its owner came after it was divided: does he take it for its value, as some of those we reported from in this chapter said, or does he not take it for value or otherwise, as some of those we reported from in this chapter also said? We looked into that, and we saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled regarding the buyer of the camel from the people of war that its owner may take it from him for the price, and that camel had been owned by the buyer from the belligerents just as one in whose share spoil falls owns what falls in his share of it. By analogy, when the imam divides the spoil and something of it falls into a man's hand, though he had captured that from another's hand, the one from whose hand it was captured is likewise, and he may take what was captured from his hand from the hands of the one in whose share it fell, for its value, just as he takes it from the hands of its buyer whom we mentioned, for its price. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"4956","Chapter 16 - To whom belongs the inheritance of the apostate? 5294 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Amr ibn Uthman, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The disbeliever does not inherit the Muslim, nor the Muslim the disbeliever."
"4957","5295 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his chain similar to it."
"4958","5296 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Amr ibn Uthman, from Usamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The Muslim does not inherit the disbeliever. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to the view that when the apostate is killed upon his apostasy or dies upon it, his property belongs to the Muslim treasury, and they argued with this hadith. Others opposed them, saying: His inheritance belongs to his Muslim heirs. Their proof against the people of the first view was that the disbeliever whom the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant in this hadith was not clarified to us therein: which disbeliever is he? It may be the disbeliever who has a religion, and it may be the disbeliever of every disbelief whatever it is, religion or otherwise. Since it bears that, it may not be directed to one meaning to the exclusion of the other except by a proof indicating that. So we looked: is there in any report what indicates what he meant thereby?"
"4959","5297 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from al-Zuhri who said: Ali ibn Husayn narrated to me, from Amr ibn Uthman, from Usamah ibn Zayd who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People of two religions do not inherit from one another; the Muslim does not inherit the disbeliever, nor the disbeliever the Muslim. When this came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what we mentioned, we knew he meant the disbeliever of a religion. When we saw apostasy is not a religion, and we saw them agreed the apostates do not inherit one another because apostasy is not a religion, it was established that the ruling of their inheritance is the ruling of Muslim inheritance. If one says: But you do not make them inherit from the Muslims, so likewise do not make the Muslims inherit from them - it is said to him: There is in this no proof for you of what you mentioned, for we have seen one prevented from inheritance by an act of his, while that act does not prevent him from being inherited from. Of that is that we saw the killer does not inherit the one he killed, and we saw that if a man wounds a man, then the wounded dies of the wound, and the wounder is the father of the wounded, that he inherits him. So the killed one inherits from the one who killed him, while the killer does not inherit from the one he killed, because the killer was punished for his killing and prevented from inheritance from the one he killed, while the killed was not prevented from inheritance from the one who wounded him the wound that killed him, since he had done nothing. Likewise the apostate is prevented from the inheritance of others as punishment for what he did, while others are not prevented from inheritance from him, since there was from them nothing to be punished for. Thereby the saying of one who makes the Muslim heirs inherit from the apostate is established, and it was also reported from a group of the early ones."
"4960","5298 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Isbahani narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah informed us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, from Ali, that he assigned the inheritance of al-Mustawrid to his Muslim heirs."
"4961","5299 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Simak, from Ibn Ubayd ibn al-Abras, that Ali said to al-Mustawrid: Upon whose religion are you? He said: Upon the religion of Isa. Ali said: And I am upon the religion of Isa; so who is your Lord? The people claimed he said: He is his Lord. So he said: Kill him. And he did not touch his property."
"4962","5300 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl informed us, from al-Walid ibn Jumay', from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: When the apostate dies, his child inherits him."
"4963","5301 - Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, that Ibn Mas'ud said: His inheritance belongs to his Muslim heirs."
"4964","5302 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi Kathir who said: I asked Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib about the inheritance of the apostate, and he said: It belongs to his family."
"4965","5303 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi Kathir who said: I asked Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib about the apostates, and he said: We inherit them, and they do not inherit us."
"4966","5304 - Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Shu'bah and Sufyan informed us, from Musa ibn Abi Kathir, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, similar to it."
"4967","5305 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Musa ibn al-Sabbah - and once he said: from Abu al-Sabbah - from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, similar to it."
"4968","5306 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh narrated to us, from Ash'ath, from al-Hasan, regarding the apostate who joins the abode of war, he said: His property is among his Muslim children, upon the Book of Allah."
"4969","5307 - Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah informed us, from Qatadah, that al-Hasan said: His inheritance belongs to his Muslim heir when he apostatizes from Islam. These whom we mentioned made the inheritance of the apostate belong to his Muslim heirs, and odd beside their saying is what I described in this chapter of what analogy requires. In that is another proof by way of analogy as well, namely that we saw them agreed the apostate before his apostasy has protected blood and property, then when he apostatizes all agreed the prior protection is lifted from his blood so his blood becomes permissible, while his property remains protected in the state of apostasy by the prior protection. We saw the belligerents: the ruling of their blood and their property is one, whether killed or not. So what makes their property permissible is not killing but disbelief, while the apostate's property is not made permissible by his disbelief; since his property is not made permissible by his disbelief, it is not made permissible by his killing. We saw belligerent property becomes permissible through spoils and is owned thereby, and we saw what of their property falls in our land we own against them and take as spoil by the land, even if we did not kill them. Since the apostate's property is not taken as spoil by his apostasy, then by analogy it is also not taken as spoil by shedding his blood. Since his property does not enter the ruling of spoils, it is either inherited by his heirs who would inherit him had he died upon Islam, or it passes to the Muslims. If it passes to his Muslim heirs, it is as we said; and if it passes to all the Muslims, then the Muslims have inherited an apostate. Since the apostate remains in the state of being inherited by the Muslims, and did not leave that by his apostasy, those who inherit him are his heirs who would have inherited him had he died in Islam, and none other. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all. The apostate's ownership only ceases by joining the abode of war, due to his leaving our land for the abode of war by way of deserved forfeiture while being a fighter against us, his blood permissible in our land, by proof of the belligerent who enters to us: when he returns to the abode of war leaving property here, his ownership of it does not cease despite this, and he did not leave as one deserving forfeiture, because he remains under our security until he enters the abode of war."
"4970","Chapter 17 - Reviving dead land. 5308 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Yashkuri, from Jabir ibn Abdullah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever encloses a wall around a land, it is his."
"4971","5309 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Kathir ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever revives a dead land of land, it is his, and the unjust root has no right."
"4972","5310 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah ibn Jundub who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever encloses something, it is his. Abu Ja'far said: Some went to the view that whoever revives a dead land, it is his, whether the imam permitted him therein or not, and whether the imam assigned it to him or not, and they argued with these reports. Among those who held that were Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah's mercy be upon them both. They said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, whoever revives a dead land it is his, the ruling of reviving that is left to whoever wishes, so the imam has no say in that. They said: The proofs of analogy also indicate this. Do you not see that water in seas and rivers: whoever takes of it owns it by taking it, even if the imam did not command him to take it nor assign it to him. Likewise game: whoever hunts it, it is his, needing therein neither permission from the imam nor assignment, and the imam therein and all people are equal. They said: Likewise dead land over which no one has ownership is like an unowned bird; whoever takes of that, it is his by taking it, needing therein neither the imam's command nor his assignment, just as he does not need that from him regarding the water and game we mentioned. Others opposed them, among them Abu Hanifah, may Allah's mercy be upon him, saying: Land is not revived except by the imam's order therein to the one who revives it and his assigning it to him. They said: What was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in this chapter does not refute what we said, because that revival by which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the land belong to its reviver in this hadith was not explained to us what it is. It may be what was done thereof by the imam's order, so his saying, whoever revives a dead land it is his, means: whoever revives it upon the conditions of revival, it is his. And among its conditions are enclosing it, the imam's permission to him therein, and his assigning it to him. This may be the meaning of the hadith, and it may be upon what Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them both, interpreted; except that it may not be decisively attributed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he meant a meaning except by a specification from him, or by agreement of those after him that he meant that meaning. Since we found in this hadith no proof for either group, we looked into other hadiths: is there in them what indicates any of that?"
"4973","5311 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththamah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no sanctuary except for Allah and His Messenger."
"4974","5312 - Yazid and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they both said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them both, from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththamah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) declared al-Naqi' forbidden, and said: There is no sanctuary except for Allah and for His Messenger."
"4975","5313 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Jamrah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sanctuary except for Allah and for His Messenger. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, there is no sanctuary except for Allah and for His Messenger - and sanctuary is what is protected of land - that proves the ruling of lands belongs to the imams, not to others, and that its ruling differs from the ruling of game. We have explained what the first report may bear, and it was worthier for us to carry its aspect upon what does not contradict this second report. As for what supports Abu Hanifah therein by way of analogy, distinguishing dead land from river water and game, it is that we saw game and river water: the imam may not assign that to anyone. We saw that had he assigned a dead land to a man, then assigned it to another man, it is valid; likewise had the imam needed to sell it for a public need of the Muslims, his sale of it is valid, while that is not valid for river water, nor land or sea game. Since that regarding lands belongs to the imam, that proves their ruling belongs to him, and that they are in his hand like the rest of the property in his hand for the Muslims, having no specific owner, and no one owns them by taking them until the imam assigns them to him, upon his good judgment for the Muslims. Since game and water are not for the imam to sell nor assign to anyone, the imam therein is like all people, and ownership of them is due by taking them without the imam. Thereby what Abu Hanifah went to is established, by what we described of the reports and proofs we mentioned. If one argues therein with what..."
"4976","5314 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik and Yunus ibn Yazid informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Whoever revives a dead land, it is his. That was because men were marking out portions of land."
"4977","5315 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, similar to it. It was said to him: You have no proof in this; the meaning of this, in our view, is upon what we mentioned of the meaning of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): whoever revives a dead land it is his. And it was reported from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in other than this hadith, what indicates his intent in this hadith is what we mentioned."
"4978","5316 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah who said: A man of Basra called Abu Abdullah went out to Umar and said: In the land of Basra there is land harming none of the Muslims, and it is not of kharaj land; if you wish to grant it to me, I will make it clover and olives, and palms among my palms, then do so. He was the first to introduce estates in the land of Basra. He said: So Umar wrote to Abu Musa al-Ash'ari: If it is a sanctuary, then grant it to him. Do you not see Umar did not allow him to take it, nor made him own it, except by his deputy's granting that man it; and had it not been so he would have said to him: What need have you of my granting you, for you may revive it without me and cultivate it and own it. That proves revival in Umar's view is what the imam permits therein to the one undertaking it and assigns to him. And that is also proved by what..."
"4979","5317 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Azhar al-Samman narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Muhammad who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: To us belong the necks of land. Abu Ja'far said: That proves the necks of all lands belong to the Muslim imams, and they do not leave their hands except by their bringing them out to what they deem, upon their good judgment for the Muslims, in cultivating their lands and their welfare. This is the saying of Abu Hanifah, may Allah's mercy be upon him."
"4980","Chapter 18 - Breeding donkeys upon horses. 5318 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Ibn Zurayr, from Ali ibn Abi Talib who said: A mule was gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he rode it. Ali said: Had we bred donkeys upon horses we would have the like of this. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Only those who do not know do that."
"4981","5319 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Uthman, from Salim, from Ali ibn Alqamah, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"4982","5320 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. (h). 5321 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, they both said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abu Jahdam, from Abdullah ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not single us out with anything to the exclusion of people except three: perfecting ablution, not eating charity, and not breeding donkeys upon horses. A group went to this, disliking breeding donkeys upon horses, forbidding it and preventing it, arguing with these reports. Others opposed them, seeing no harm in that. Their proof therein was that had that been disliked, riding mules would have been disliked, for were it not for people's desire for mules and riding them, donkeys would not be bred upon horses. Do you not see that when he forbade castrating the sons of Adam, he thereby disliked keeping eunuchs, because in keeping them lies encouragement of their castration, for when people avoid keeping them, wicked people have no desire to castrate them."
"4983","5322 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Afif ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: al-Ala ibn Isa al-Dhahabi narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz was brought a eunuch and he disliked buying him, saying: I would not assist castration. Everything whose abandoning entails abandoning some sinful people their sin should not be kept. Since there is agreement on the permissibility of keeping and riding mules, that proves the prohibition in the first reports was not meant as forbiddance, but another meaning was meant. Among what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding riding mules is what..."
"4984","5323 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq who said: A man said to al-Bara: O Abu Umarah, did you flee on the day of Hunayn? He said: No, by Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not flee, but the hasty people fled; Hawazin met them with arrows. I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon his white mule, with Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith holding its bridle, saying: I am the Prophet, no lie; I am the son of Abd al-Muttalib."
"4985","5324 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq informed us, then he mentioned with his chain similar to it."
"4986","5325 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara, similar to it."
"4987","5326 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Kathir ibn Abbas, that his father al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib said: I witnessed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the day of Hunayn; I and Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith stayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and did not leave him, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon a white mule of his, gifted to him by Farwah ibn Nufathah al-Judhami."
"4988","5327 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Zuhri narrating from Kathir ibn al-Abbas, from his father, similar to it."
"4989","5328 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Haserah narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from his father who said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hunayn, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon his mule."
"4990","5329 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Sulayman ibn Amr ibn al-Ahwas, from his mother who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of sacrifice, at the Jamrah of al-Aqabah, upon his mule."
"4991","5330 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Busr, from his father, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to them riding upon his mule."
"4992","5331 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Thabit al-Bunani and Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon a grey mule of his, and he passed by a wall of Banu al-Najjar, when there was a grave whose occupant was being punished, so the mule shied. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that you would not bury one another, I would have prayed to Allah to make you hear the punishment of the grave."
"4993","5332 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Ma'n ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Fa'id narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Ali ibn Abi Rafi', from his grandfather, that he saw the mule of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) grey, and it was with Ali ibn Husayn."
"4994","5333 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Ikrimah ibn Ammar who said: Iyas ibn Salamah narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: We raided with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Hunayn. Then he mentioned a long hadith therein: I passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) retreating, while he was upon his grey mule."
"4995","5334 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from Aslam ibn Abi Imran, from Uqbah ibn Amir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rode his mule, and I followed him, then he mentioned the hadith. The reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting riding mules have successively come."
"4996","5335 - And it was reported regarding that from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: A'idh ibn Habib narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Sa'id ibn Ashwa', from Hanash ibn al-Mu'tamir who said: I saw Ali brought a mule on the day of sacrifice, so he rode it and kept saying takbir until he reached the desert prayer ground."
"4997","5336 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from al-Hakam who said: I heard Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, that he went out on the day of sacrifice upon a white mule, intending prayer, then a man came and took hold of its halter, asking him about the day of the greater pilgrimage. He said: It is this day of yours; let go its way. If one says: What is the meaning of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, only those who do not know do that? It is said to him: The people of knowledge said its meaning is: Horses have come with reward for keeping them, acquiring them and feeding them, and that is not in mules. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Only those who do not know abandon breeding horse upon horse, yielding what carries reward, and breed donkey upon horse yielding a mule with no reward therein - that is, because by that they abandon producing what keeping it carries reward, and produce what keeping it carries no reward. Among what was reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding reward for keeping horses is what..."
"4998","5337 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sa'd informed me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about horses, and he said: They are for three: for a man a reward, for a man a covering, and for a man a burden. As for one who ties it prepared in the path of Allah, even if he lengthens its rope in a fertile meadow or fertile garden, Allah writes for him the number of what it ate as good deeds, and the number of its dung as good deeds; and were its rope to break and it runs a hill or two, Allah writes the number of its traces as good deeds; and were it to pass by a gushing river not intending to water it, and it drinks from it, Allah writes for him the number of what it drank as good deeds. Whoever keeps it for sufficiency and chastity, then does not forget Allah's right in their necks and backs, it is for him a covering from the Fire. Whoever keeps it for pride, showing off and hostility toward the Muslims, it is for him ruin on the Day of Resurrection. They said: And the donkeys, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Nothing was revealed to me regarding donkeys except this unique verse: {Whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it}."
"4999","5338 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bukayr, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to that as well."
"5000","5339 - Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Horses have good tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection."
"5001","5340 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"5002","5341 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"5003","5342 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"5004","5343 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Talhah ibn Abi Sa'id narrated to us, that Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to him, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever keeps a horse in the path of Allah, out of faith in Allah and belief in Allah's promises, its feed, its drink and its dung will be good deeds in his scale on the Day of Resurrection."
"5005","5344 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, he said: Utbah ibn Abi Hakim informed me, from al-Husayn ibn Harmalah al-Mahri, from Abu al-Musabbih, from Jabir ibn Abdullah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Horses have good and attainment tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection; adorn them with neckbands, and do not adorn them with bowstrings."
"5006","5345 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Umar ibn Sa'id, from Abu Zur'ah, from Jarir ibn Abdullah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Horses have good tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection: reward and spoil."
"5007","5346 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Yunus, then he mentioned with his chain similar to it."
"5008","5347 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: I heard Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrating, he said: Ziyad ibn Nu'aym narrated to me, that he heard Abu Kabshah, a companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrating, saying: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Horses have good tied in their forelocks, their people are aided over it, and the spender upon it is like one extending his hands with charity."
"5009","5348 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris and Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, from Urwah al-Bariqi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Good is tied in the forelocks of horses. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, of what is that? He said: Reward and spoil until the Day of Resurrection. And Ibn Idris added therein: Camels are honor for their people, and sheep are blessing."
"5010","5349 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Fitr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq who said: Urwah al-Bariqi stood over us while we were in our gathering, and he narrated to us, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Good is tied in the forelocks of horses forever until the Day of Resurrection."
"5011","5350 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Ayzar ibn Hurayth, from Urwah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"5012","5351 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Jabir, from Amir, from Urwah al-Bariqi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it, and he added: Reward and spoil."
"5013","5352 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sulayman al-Aftas narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jurashi narrated to us, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr who said: Salamah ibn Nufayl al-Sakuni narrated to me, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Horses have good tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection, and their people are aided over them. If one says: What is the meaning of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singling out Banu Hashim with the prohibition of breeding donkeys upon horses? It is said to him: Because..."
"5014","5353 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: al-Murajja - he is Ibn Raja - narrated to us, he said: Abu Jahdam narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Ubaydullah narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled us out with nothing except three: not eating charity, perfecting ablution, and not breeding a donkey upon a horse. He said: I met Abdullah ibn al-Hasan while he was circumambulating the House, and I narrated to him, and he said: He spoke the truth; horses were few among Banu Hashim, so he wished they increase among them. Abdullah ibn al-Hasan thereby explained, by this interpretation, the reason for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled out Banu Hashim not to breed donkey upon horse, and that it was not for forbiddance; the cause was only the scarcity of horses among them, so when that cause lifted and horses became many in their hands, they became therein like others. The Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singling out the prohibition thereof proves his permitting it to others. Since he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed in keeping horses what we mentioned of reward and recompense, and was asked about keeping donkeys and placed nothing in keeping them, and mules which differ from horses are like them - whoever abandons producing what keeping and acquiring it carries reward, and produces what keeping it carries no reward, is of those who do not know. What we mentioned establishes the permissibility of producing mules for Banu Hashim and others, though producing horses is better than that. And it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"5015","5354 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, and from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In buried treasure is the fifth."
"5016","5355 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it. The questioner said to him: O Abu Muhammad, is Abu Salamah with him? He said: If he is with him, then he is with him. So the ruling of all fay and the fifth of spoils was one ruling. Then people afterwards spoke on the interpretation of His saying, the Mighty and Majestic, in the verse of fay: {belongs to Allah}, and in spoil: {then to Allah belongs}. Some said: A share is thereby due to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, in fay and in the fifth of spoil, so they placed that share in the expenditure of the Ka'bah."
"5017","5356 - And they reported that from Abu al-Aliyah: Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to me, narrating to me from Abu Ubayd, from Hajjaj, from Abu Ja'far al-Razi, from al-Rabi', from Abu al-Aliyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would be brought spoil, and he would strike with his hand; whatever fell therein of something he made for the Ka'bah, and it is the share of the House of Allah. Then he would divide the rest into five: a share for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a share for the near kin, a share for orphans, a share for the poor, and a share for the wayfarer. He said: What he made for the Ka'bah is the share he made for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. Others went to the view that what Allah - exalted His praise - attributed to Himself thereof is an opening of speech, with which He opened what He commanded of dividing fay and fifthing spoils therein. They said: Likewise what He attributed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And they reported that from Ibn Abbas, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them both."
"5018","5357 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj ibn Sulayman al-Hadrami, Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah ibn Rashid al-Basri, and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah al-Kufi - may Allah's mercy be upon them - narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, from Ali ibn Abi Talhah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Spoil would be divided into five fifths; four of them for those who fought over it, and one fifth divided into four: a quarter for Allah and for His Messenger and for the near kin - meaning the kin of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - so what belonged to Allah and to the Messenger belongs to the kin of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took nothing of the fifth; the second quarter for orphans, the third quarter for the poor, and the fourth quarter for the wayfarer, and he is the poor guest who alights with the Muslims. A group went to the view that the meaning of Allah's saying, the Mighty and Majestic, {then to Allah belongs its fifth}, is an opening of speech, and that His saying {and to the Messenger} makes a share due to the Messenger of Allah, and likewise what He attributed to those He mentioned in the verse of the fifth of spoils altogether. And they reported that from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with them."
"5019","5358 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah Musa ibn Mas'ud narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us. (h). 5359 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Qays ibn Muslim who said: I asked al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali about Allah's saying, the Mighty and Majestic: {Know that whatever you gain of anything, then to Allah belongs its fifth}, the verse. He said: As for His saying {then to Allah belongs its fifth}, it is an opening of Allah's speech in this world and the Hereafter: {and to the Messenger, and to the near kin, and orphans, and the poor, and the wayfarer}. People differed after the death of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): one said the share of the near kin belongs to the kin of the caliph; another said the share of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) belongs to the caliph after him. Then their opinion agreed to place these two shares in horses and equipment in the path of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and that was in the rule of Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. When they differed over what fay and the fifth of spoils are divided upon, this disagreement, each group saying what we mentioned from them, we had to look into that to extract a sound saying from their sayings therein. We considered the saying of those who went to the view they are divided into six shares, making what Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, attributed to Himself thereof entail a share, spent in Allah's right, as they mentioned: does it have a meaning or not? We saw spoil had been forbidden to nations besides this nation, then Allah permitted it to this nation out of mercy to it and lightening upon it, and reports came with that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5020","5360 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-A'mash, from Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: Spoil was not lawful to any dark-headed one before us; spoil would come down as fire and consume it. Then was revealed: {Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah, you would have been touched...}, regarding the prior decree."
"5021","5361 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Spoil was not lawful to any dark-headed people before you; a fire would descend from heaven and consume it, until the day of Badr, when they fell upon spoils and differed over them. Then Allah the Exalted revealed: {Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah, you would have been touched for what you took by a great punishment. So eat of what you gained, lawful and good}. Then the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed over the spoils, so Allah took them from them, then made them for His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Allah revealed therein: {They ask you about the spoils; say: The spoils belong to Allah and the Messenger}."
"5022","5362 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Makhul, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to Badr and met the enemy. When Allah routed them, a group of Muslims followed them killing them, a group surrounded the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a group seized the camp and plunder. When Allah repelled the enemy and those who pursued them returned, they said: The extra share is ours; we pursued the enemy, and by us Allah repelled and routed them. Those who surrounded the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are no more entitled than us; we surrounded the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so the enemy could not catch him unaware. Those who seized the camp and plunder said: By Allah, you are no more entitled to it than us; we gathered and seized it. So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {They ask you about the spoils; say: The spoils belong to Allah and the Messenger}, up to His saying {if you are believers}. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided it among them equally at once."
"5023","5363 - Malik ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, he said: al-Ashja'i narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Abi Rabi'ah, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Makhul, from Abu Sallam, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, similar to it. He did not mention Ubadah, except that he said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided it at once among them, and the Qur'an was revealed: {They ask you about the spoils; say: The spoils belong to Allah and the Messenger}. A group said this verse was revealed regarding other than this meaning."
"5024","5364 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us, from Ata regarding His saying: {They ask you about the spoils; say: The spoils belong to Allah and the Messenger}. He said: Whatever strays from the polytheists to the Muslims without fighting, of beast and the like, is an extra grant to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: The proof of the soundness of this interpretation is what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding Abu Bakrah."
"5025","5365 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Whoever went out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Ta'if, he freed him. Abu Bakrah was among them, so he is a freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5026","5366 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn al-Khalil al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir informed us, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) freed on the day of Ta'if whoever came out to him of the slaves of Ta'if. Among those freed that day were Abu Bakrah and others, so they were freedmen of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5027","5367 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, from al-Fadl ibn Muhalhal, from al-Mughirah, from al-Shibak, from al-Sha'bi, from a man of Thaqif who said: We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to return Abu Bakrah to us, but he refused us, saying: He is the freedman of Allah and the freedman of His Messenger. Do you not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) freed Abu Bakrah and those Ta'if slaves who descended to him, a freeing by which they became his freedmen? That proves their ownership had become due to him before the freeing, to the exclusion of others with him of the Muslims, and that when they are taken without fighting, as had no horses nor camels been urged over it, that belongs to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the exclusion of others with him of the Muslims. A group said the interpretation of this verse was meant with a meaning other than these two meanings."
"5028","5368 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: On the day of Badr the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever does such and such gets such and such. So the young men went, and elders sat under the banners. When the spoil came, the young men came seeking their extra grant, and the elders said: Do not prefer yourselves over us, for we were under the banners, and had you been routed we would have been support for you. So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {They ask you about the spoils}, and he recited until he reached: {As your Lord brought you out of your house with truth, though a party of the believers were averse}. Saying: Obey in this matter, as you saw the outcome of my command when you went out averse. So he divided among them equally. Do you not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided it all among them, as Allah the Exalted revealed: {They ask you about the spoils; say: The spoils belong to Allah and the Messenger}. What Allah attributed to Himself was by way of obligation, and what He attributed to His Messenger by way of ownership. Another aspect was also reported regarding that."
"5029","5369 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, from his father who said: Four verses were revealed regarding me: I gained a sword on the day of Badr, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, grant it to me. He said: Put it where you took it from. Then I said: O Messenger, grant it to me. He said: Put it where you took it from. I said: O Messenger of Allah, grant it to me. He said: Put it where you took it from; would you make him like one with no need - or he said: or he is made like one with no need; the doubt is from Ibn Marzuq. He said: And was revealed: {They ask you about the spoils}, to the end of the verse. Abu Ja'far said: In all these reports which permitted spoils, they were only made in the beginning of their permitting for Allah and the Messenger. What Allah, glory be to Him, attributed of them to Himself was not upon spending something of them in Allah's right, spent therein specifically, not allowed to pass to another, and spent specifically as a share to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so divided over two shares spent in two ways; rather they were all made disposable in one way, namely made for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who did not prefer himself with them over his companions, nor single out some to the exclusion of others, but included them all and equalized among them therein, and did not take out a fifth for Allah, because the verse of the fifth is the fay of fays, and the verse of spoils had not then been revealed to him. In what we mentioned is what proves that when the verse of spoils was revealed - over whose interpretation occurred the disagreement we mentioned - what Allah the Exalted attributed of spoils to Himself does not entail a share for Allah therein, that share being other than the share of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein. Rather it was from Him, the Mighty and Majestic, as His imposing division upon the aspects we mentioned. Thereby the saying of one who went to the view spoil is divided into six shares is void. Then we returned to the saying of one who went to the view it is divided into four shares, to what they argued therein from the report of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, which we reported at the start of this book, though it is an interrupted report, the like of which is not established, except that a group of the people of knowledge of reports say it is sound, and that Ali ibn Abi Talhah, though he had not seen Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, only took that from Mujahid and Ikrimah, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"5030","5370 - Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Fahm narrated to us, he said: I heard Ahmad ibn Hanbal say: Had a man journeyed to Egypt and returned from it with the book of interpretation of Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, I would not deem his journey wasted. We found what was attributed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the verse of spoils was ownership, not otherwise. In this was a decisive proof sparing us arguing with other than it against the people of this view. But we add in arguing against them, saying: We found Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, attributed to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something of fay in other than the two verses whose mention we advanced at the start of this chapter, and that was upon ownership from Him of what He attributed to him thereof, the Mighty and Majestic, saying: {Whatever Allah bestowed upon His Messenger of them, you urged over it neither horses nor camels}."
"5031","5371 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Abu Umayyah narrated to us, they both said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Malik ibn Aws al-Nadri who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, sent to me saying: The household people of your tribe have come to Madinah, and we have ordered a grant for them, so divide it among them. While I was thus, his chamberlain Yarfa came to him saying: Here are Uthman, Abd al-Rahman, Sa'd, al-Zubayr and Talhah seeking permission upon you. He said: Admit them. Then we waited a while, and he said: Here are al-Abbas and Ali seeking permission upon you. He said: Admit them both. Al-Abbas entered and said: O Commander of the Faithful, judge between me and this man - both then disputing over what Allah bestowed upon His Messenger of the properties of Banu al-Nadir. The people said: Judge between them, O Commander of the Faithful, and relieve each from the other. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I adjure you by Allah by Whose permission the heavens and earth stand: Do you know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We are not inherited; what we leave is charity? They said: He said that. Then he said the like to them both, and they said: Yes. He said: I shall inform you about this fay: Allah singled out His Prophet with something He gave none other, saying: {Whatever Allah bestowed upon His Messenger of them, you urged over it neither horses nor camels}. By Allah, he did not take it apart from you nor prefer himself with it over you; he divided it among you and spread it among you, until this property remained of it. He would spend from it upon his family a year's provision, then gather the rest as the collective property of Allah. Do you not see that His saying, the Mighty and Majestic, {Whatever Allah bestowed upon His Messenger of them}, is upon a fay which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) owned to the exclusion of others, not upon an opening of speech entailing ownership for him. Likewise what He attributed to him in the verse of fay and the verse of spoil, whose mention we advanced at the start of this book, is upon ownership from Him, not upon an opening of speech entailing no ownership for him. What we mentioned establishes that fay and the fifth of spoils in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were spent in five ways, neither more nor fewer."
"5032","5372 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to me, narrating to me from Abu Ubayd, from Sa'id ibn Ufayr, from Abdullah ibn Lahi'ah, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ja'far, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I saw spoils divided into five parts, then lots cast over them; whatever fell to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) belongs to him, not taken apart."
"5033","5373 - Then Yahya ibn Uthman narrated it to me, he said: My father and Sa'id ibn Ufayr narrated to us, then he mentioned it with its chain and text from them both."
"5034","5374 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, then he mentioned with his chain similar to it. Except that he said: What fell to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) belongs to him, and he divides the rest among them. That was also reported from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar and from Ata ibn Abi Rabah."
"5035","5375 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Musa ibn Abi A'ishah who said: I heard Yahya ibn al-Jazzar say: The share of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is a fifth of the fifth."
"5036","5376 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Ata who said: The fifth of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and the fifth of the Messenger are one."
"5037","5377 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from al-Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam was employed over charities, and he took Abu Rafi' along. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him. He said: O Abu Rafi', charity is forbidden to Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, and the freedman of a people is of themselves."
"5038","5378 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they both said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abi Rafi', freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a man of Banu Makhzum over charity, and he said to Abu Rafi': Accompany me that we may gain of it. He said: Until I seek permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. He said: The family of Muhammad may not take charity, and the freedman of a people is of themselves."
"5039","5379 - Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Warqa ibn Umar narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib who said: I entered upon Umm Kulthum daughter of Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, and she said: A freedman of ours called Hurmuz or Kaysan informed that he passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So he called me, saying: O Abu so-and-so, we are a household forbidden to eat charity, and the freedman of a people is of themselves, so do not eat charity. Since the charity forbidden to Banu Hashim included their freedmen, while their freedmen did not enter with them into the share of the near kin by agreement of the Muslims, that establishes the corruption of one who said: It was made for the near kin in the verse of fay and the verse of the fifth of spoil as replacement for the charity forbidden them. This saying is also corrupt from another angle: we saw that had charity been lawful to Banu Hashim as to all Muslims, it would have been forbidden to their rich as it is forbidden to the rich of all other Muslims. We saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) included all Banu Hashim in the share of the near kin, among them al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, who was wealthy in both pre-Islam and Islam. Do you not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hastened from him the zakah of his property for two years? Since his wealth did not prevent him from the share of the near kin, while that wealth would have prevented him from charity before Allah forbade it to Banu Hashim, that proves the share of the near kin was not made for one to whom it is made as replacement for the charity forbidden him. As for those who went to the view the near kin in the two verses we mentioned at the start of this book are Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib specifically, they argued with what Jubayr ibn Mut'im reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding that."
"5040","5380 - Ali ibn Shaybah and Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar al-Baghdadiyyan narrated to us, they both said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Jubayr ibn Mut'im who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided the share of the near kin therewith, he gave Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib, and gave Banu Umayyah nothing. I and Uthman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, these Banu Hashim Allah favored through you; what of us and Banu al-Muttalib, when we and they in lineage are one thing? He said: Banu al-Muttalib did not separate from me in pre-Islam nor Islam. They said: When we saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) include in his giving what he was commanded to give his near kin - Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib - and deprive those above them, giving them nothing, that proves those above them are not of his near kin. This saying, in our view, is also corrupt, for we saw him deprive Banu Umayyah and Banu Nawfal, giving them nothing, though they are kin - how could they not be kin when their position to him is like that of Banu al-Muttalib? Since Banu Umayyah and Banu Nawfal did not leave the kinship of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by his leaving giving them, likewise those above them of the rest of Quraysh clans do not leave his kinship by his leaving giving them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also gave of the share of the near kin one who is neither of Banu Hashim nor Banu al-Muttalib, but of Quraysh, meeting him at a father more distant than the father at which Banu Umayyah and Banu Nawfal meet him, namely al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam."
"5041","5381 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi informed me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from his grandfather, that he used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the year of Khaybar, struck for al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam four shares: a share for al-Zubayr, a share for the near kin - for Safiyyah daughter of Abd al-Muttalib, mother of al-Zubayr - and two shares for the horse."
"5042","5382 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Dawud al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam on the day of Khaybar four shares: a share for him with the Muslims, two shares for the horse, and a share for the near kin."
"5043","5383 - al-Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father who said: Al-Zubayr would be struck in the sheep - i.e. spoil - four shares: two shares for his horse, and a share for the near kin. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, for his kinship to him, of the share of the near kin - while al-Zubayr is neither of Banu Hashim nor Banu al-Muttalib, and he made him therein like Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib - that proves the near kin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) are Banu Hashim, Banu al-Muttalib, and others of his kin besides them. If one says: Al-Zubayr, though not of Banu Hashim, his mother is of them - Safiyyah daughter of Abd al-Muttalib ibn Hashim - and thereby the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him what he gave, so he stood with him by his place through his mother in place of others of Banu Hashim - it is said to him: Had what you described been as you mentioned, he would have given others besides Banu Hashim whose mother is of Banu Hashim. There were in his presence, besides Banu Hashim, those whose mothers were Hashimi, closer in womb kinship to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through the mother than al-Zubayr: among them Umamah daughter of Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi', whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) deprived, giving her nothing of the share of the near kin when he deprived Banu Umayyah - she being of Banu Umayyah - and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not give her for her Hashimi mother, Zaynab daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with her. He also deprived Ja'dah ibn Hubayrah al-Makhzumi, giving him nothing, though his mother Umm Hani daughter of Abu Talib ibn Abd al-Muttalib ibn Hashim, and did not give him for his mother though she was of Banu Hashim. That proves the reason the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam what he gave of the share of the near kin was not kinship through his mother, but another reason. What we mentioned establishes that the near kin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) are Banu Hashim, Banu al-Muttalib, and others who are kin to him besides Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commanded His Messenger in other than this verse: {Warn your nearest kin}, yet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not single out Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib for warning, but warned of his people one more distant in womb than Banu Umayyah and Banu Nawfal."
"5044","5384 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Isbahani narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Abd al-Quddus narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Abbad ibn Abdullah who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When was revealed {Warn your nearest kin}, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Ali, gather Banu Hashim for me - they being forty men, or forty less a man - then he mentioned the hadith. Abu Ja'far, may Allah be pleased with him, said: In this hadith he aimed the warning at Banu Hashim specifically."
"5045","5385 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Isbahani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abd al-Ghaffar ibn al-Qasim, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, similar to it. Except that he said: Gather Banu al-Muttalib for me."
"5046","5386 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Taymi narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq and Zuhayr ibn Amr, they both said: When was revealed {Warn your nearest kin}, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to a stony part of a mountain, ascended its top, then said: O sons of Abd Manaf, I am a warner. In this hadith is his including Banu Abd Manaf, with one closer to him than them of his kin."
"5047","5387 - Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad and Hassan ibn Ghalib narrated to us, they both said: Dimam ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Ibn Wardan, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: O sons of Hashim, O sons of Qusayy, O sons of Abd Manaf, I am the warner, death is the raider, and the Hour is the appointment. In this hadith is that he called Banu Qusayy, with one closer to him than them."
"5048","5388 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid and Affan narrated to us, from Abu Awanah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When was revealed {Warn your nearest kin}, the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and called: O sons of Ka'b ibn Lu'ayy, save yourselves from the Fire; O sons of Abd Manaf, save yourselves from the Fire; O sons of Hashim, save yourselves from the Fire; O sons of Abd al-Muttalib, save yourselves from the Fire; O Fatimah daughter of Muhammad, save yourself from the Fire, for I own nothing for you from Allah, except that you have a womb tie which I shall keep moist with its wetting. In this hadith is that he warned Banu Ka'b ibn Lu'ayy with one closer to him than them. In the hadith also is that he made them all possessors of womb ties."
"5049","5389 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: When was revealed {Warn your nearest kin}, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascended al-Safa and called: O sons of Adi, O sons of so-and-so, to the clans of Quraysh, until they gathered. A man unable to come out would send a messenger to see. Abu Lahab and Quraysh came and gathered. He said: If I informed you horsemen in the valley intend to raid you, would you believe me? They said: Yes; we have tried naught of you but truthfulness. He said: I am a warner to you before a severe punishment. In this hadith is that he called all the clans of Quraysh."
"5050","5390 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, he said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said when was revealed upon him {Warn your nearest kin}: O company of Quraysh, buy yourselves from Allah; I avail you nothing from Allah. O sons of Abd Manaf, buy yourselves from Allah; I avail you nothing from Allah. O Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, I avail you nothing from Allah. O Safiyyah, aunt of the Messenger of Allah, I avail you nothing from Allah. O Fatimah daughter of the Messenger of Allah, I avail you nothing from Allah."
"5051","5391 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab who said: Sa'id and Abu Salamah informed me, that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned similar to it. Except that he said: O Safiyyah, O Fatimah. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commanded him to warn his nearest kin, warned Quraysh, far and near, that proves they are all his kin. Had it not been so, he would have aimed his warning at his kin among them, leaving one not kin to him unwarned, as he did not warn one joined to him by a father other than Quraysh. If one says: He only gathered all Quraysh and warned them because Allah commanded him to warn his nearest kin, and he has no kin closer than Quraysh, so he called all Quraysh since in its entirety it was his kin-tribe, the nearest of tribes to him - it is said to him: Had it been as you mentioned, he would have said: Warn your near kin; but He, the Mighty and Majestic, did not say so to him, rather said to him: {Warn your nearest kin}. So He informed him that every member of this tribe is of his nearest. Thereby the saying of one who made the near kin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) specifically Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib is void. In what we mentioned after this proof we argued with is what spares us arguing for the saying of one who said the near kin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) are all Quraysh. It was reported from Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, on the interpretation of Allah's saying, the Mighty and Majestic: {Say: I ask you no wage except affection in kinship}, what also indicates this meaning."
"5052","5392 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them both, regarding His saying, the Mighty and Majestic: {Say: I ask you no wage except affection in kinship}. He said: That they maintain my kinship and not belie me. This is addressed to all Quraysh, proving all Quraysh are his kin. It was also reported regarding that from Ikrimah what indicates this meaning as well."
"5053","5393 - Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Bajali narrated to us, he said: I asked Ikrimah about Allah's saying, the Mighty and Majestic: {Say: I ask you no wage except affection in kinship}. He said: The kinships of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were of all the clans of Quraysh, and they were the severest people to him in harm. So Allah the Exalted revealed regarding them: {Say: I ask you no wage except affection in kinship}."
"5054","5394 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Nusayr narrated to us, from Umar ibn Farrukh, from Habib ibn al-Zubayr who said: A man came to Ikrimah saying: O Abu Abdullah, Allah's saying, the Mighty and Majestic: {Say: I ask you no wage except affection in kinship}. He said: Are you a Saba'i? He said: I am not a Saba'i, but I wish to know. He said: If you wish to know, there was no quarter of Quraysh's quarters but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had lineage among them. Quraysh used to maintain their wombs before him, but when the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and called them to Islam, they cut him off, withheld from him and deprived him. So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: {Say: I ask you no wage except affection in kinship}, that you maintain me for what you used to maintain your kinship with before me. It was also reported from Mujahid regarding that what indicates this meaning."
"5055","5395 - Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Warqa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid regarding His saying: {Say: I ask you no wage except affection in kinship}, that you follow me, believe me, and maintain my womb tie. In what we reported from Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Ikrimah, and from Mujahid, on the interpretation of this verse, is what proves all Quraysh are kin to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). That agreed with what we mentioned on the interpretation of Allah's saying, the Mighty and Majestic: {Warn your nearest kin}. Except that from al-Hasan was reported on the interpretation of this verse an aspect opposing this aspect."
"5056","5396 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, from Hushaym, from Mansur ibn Zadham, from al-Hasan regarding His saying: {Say: I ask you no wage except affection in kinship}. He said: Drawing near to Allah through righteous action. As for one who went to the view Quraysh are of the near kin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that of the near kin are also whoever touches him by a womb through his mothers to the furthest of every father, for each mother of his mothers of the tribe she is of, he argued with analogy, saying: I saw a man's lineage through his father and mother differing, yet differing lineage from them did not prevent his being a son to them both. Then we saw him having kinship to each of them: by his place from his father kinship to his father's kin, and by his place from his mother kinship to his mother's kin. Do you not see he inherits his paternal brothers and maternal brothers, and his paternal and maternal brothers inherit him, though the inheritance of one group differs from the other, and that difference does not prevent kinship. Since his mother's kin became kin to him as his father's kin became kin to him, what his father's kin deserve for their kinship to him, his mother's kin deserve the like for their kinship to him. The people of knowledge spoke on the like, regarding a man who bequeaths a third of his property to the kin of so-and-so: they said sayings we shall explain, and explain each proponent's doctrine leading to his saying, in this book of ours, Allah willing. Abu Hanifah, may Allah's mercy be upon him, said: It is every unmarriageable womb kin of so-and-so, the testator to his kin, by what he bequeathed them, from his father's side and his mother's side, except that he begins therein with one whose kinship to him is from his father's side over one whose kinship is from his mother's side. Its explanation is that he has an uncle and maternal uncle: his uncle's kinship to him from his father's side is like his maternal uncle's from his mother's side, so he begins therein with his uncle over his maternal uncle, making the bequest to him. Zufar ibn al-Hudhayl used to say: The bequest is to everyone near to him from his father's side or mother's side, to the exclusion of one farther among them; equal therein are one of unmarriageable womb to the testator to his kin and one not. Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them both, said: The bequest therein is to everyone joined to him and so-and-so by one father, since the emigration, from his father's side or mother's side. They equalized therein near and far, unmarriageable womb and otherwise. They did not prefer therein one whose womb is from the father's side over one whose womb is from the mother's. Others went therein to the view the bequest, by what we described, is to everyone joined to him and the testator to his kin by his third father down to below that. Others went to the view the bequest is to everyone joined to him and so-and-so, the testator to his kin, by his fourth father down to below that. Others went to the view the bequest, in what we mentioned, is to everyone joined to him and so-and-so, the testator to his kin, by one father in Islam or pre-Islam, of those tracing by fathers or mothers to him, either through a father or a mother, up to meeting him whereby inheritances are established and testimonies stand. As for what Abu Hanifah, may Allah's mercy be upon him, went to of what we mentioned in this section, it is corrupt in our view, for when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided the share of the near kin, he gave Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib, most of them not unmarriageable womb kin. It was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he commanded Abu Talhah to place something of his property which he brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for Allah and His Messenger. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to place it among his poor kin, so Abu Talhah gave it to Ubayy ibn Ka'b and Hassan ibn Thabit. Hassan meets him at his third father, Ubayy at his seventh, and neither is unmarriageable womb to him. Reports came with that. Among them is what..."
"5057","5397 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: al-Majishun narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When this verse was revealed: {You will not attain righteousness until you spend of what you love}, Abu Talhah came, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon the pulpit. He said: The house of Abu Ja'far and the house next to it were the palace of Hudaylah, gardens. The palace of Hudaylah were gardens of Abu Talhah, wherein a well which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would enter, drinking its water and eating its fruit. Abu Talhah came to him, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon the pulpit, and said: Allah says: {You will not attain righteousness until you spend of what you love}; the dearest of my property to me is this well, so it is for Allah and His Messenger; I hope its righteousness and store. Place it, O Messenger of Allah, where Allah shows you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Excellent, O Abu Talhah; a profitable property. We have accepted it from you and returned it to you; place it among the nearest. He said: So Abu Talhah gave charity to his womb kin, among them Ubayy ibn Ka'b and Hassan ibn Thabit. He said: Hassan sold his share to Mu'awiyah. It was said to him: Hassan sells the charity of Abu Talhah. He said: I do not sell a sa' for a sa' of dirhams."
"5058","5398 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him, who said: When this verse was revealed: {You will not attain righteousness until you spend of what you love} - he said: or he said: {Who is it that will lend Allah a good loan} - Abu Talhah came saying: O Messenger of Allah, my wall at such-and-such place: had I been able to conceal it I would not have announced it. He said: Place it among your poor kin and your poor family."
"5059","5399 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Thumamah who said: Anas said: Abu Talhah had a land which he made for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Place it among your poor kin. So he placed it for Hassan and Ubayy. My father said: From Thumamah, from Anas, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him: And they were nearer to him than me."
"5060","5400 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, that he heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, say: Abu Talhah was the richest of the Ansar in Madinah in property of palms. The dearest of his property to him was a walled garden, Hudaylah, facing the mosque. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would enter it and drink of good water therein. Anas said: When this verse was revealed: {You will not attain righteousness until you spend of what you love}, Abu Talhah stood to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O Messenger of Allah, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says in His Book: {You will not attain righteousness until you spend of what you love}, and the dearest property to me is the walled garden; it is a charity hoping its righteousness and store with Allah, so place it, O Messenger of Allah, where you wish. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Excellent; that is a profitable property; excellent, that is a profitable property. I have heard what you said therein, and I deem you place it among the nearest. Abu Talhah said: I shall do, O Messenger of Allah. So Abu Talhah divided it among his relatives and cousins. Abu Ja'far said: This Abu Talhah, may Allah be pleased with him, placed it in Ubayy and Hassan, though he and Ubayy meet at his seventh father, for Abu Talhah's name is Zayd ibn Sahl ibn al-Aswad ibn Haram ibn Amr ibn Zayd Manat ibn Adi ibn Amr ibn Malik ibn al-Najjar. And Hassan is ibn Thabit ibn al-Mundhir ibn Haram ibn Amr ibn Zayd Manat ibn Adi ibn Amr ibn Malik ibn al-Najjar, and neither is unmarriageable womb to him. That proves the corruption of one who claimed kinship is only one whose womb is unmarriageable. As for what Zufar ibn al-Hudhayl went to of what we related from him in this section, it is also corrupt, for we saw when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib what he gave of the share of the near kin, he equalized between one near in womb to him and one far in womb among them to him, all being his kin. Had the near veiled the far, he would not have given a far one with a near, for Allah only commanded him to give his kin, and he would not oppose what He commanded him. And this Abu Talhah combined in his giving Ubayy ibn Ka'b and Hassan ibn Thabit, one nearer to him than the other if both are his kin. He was not thereby opposing what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him, just as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in giving Banu al-Muttalib with Banu Hashim did not oppose Allah's command in giving whom He commanded him to give of his kin. As for what those went to who said a man's kin is everyone joined to him by his fourth father down of his fathers, it is also corrupt, for its people also hold giving of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the share of the near kin to Banu al-Muttalib - they being the sons of his fourth father - while he did not give the sons of his fifth father, nor the sons of any of his fathers above that. We saw him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) deprive Banu Umayyah and Banu Nawfal, giving them nothing, not because they are not of his kin. Likewise it is possible, when he deprived those above them, that that was from him not because they are not his kin. And this Abu Talhah gave what Allah and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to give, to his poor kin, some of the sons of his seventh father. Abu Talhah, may Allah be pleased with him, was not thereby opposing what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him, nor did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) deny what he did thereof. As for what went to the view a man's kin is everyone joined to him by his third father down, they said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided the share of the near kin, he gave all Banu Hashim - they being the sons of his third father - so they were his kin to him, and he gave Banu al-Muttalib what he gave because they were his allies; had he given them because they were his kin, he would have given those like them in kinship of Banu Umayyah and Banu Nawfal. This saying, in our view, is corrupt, for had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) given Banu al-Muttalib by alliance not kinship, he would have given all his allies - Khuza'ah were his allies, and Amr ibn Salim al-Khuza'i pleaded to him by that alliance."
"5061","5401 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made truce with the people of Makkah, Khuza'ah being allies of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in pre-Islam, and Banu Bakr being allies of Quraysh, Khuza'ah entered the peace of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Banu Bakr entered the peace of Quraysh. Afterwards there was fighting between Khuza'ah and Bakr, and Quraysh supplied them with weapons and food and shaded them, and Banu Bakr prevailed over Khuza'ah and killed among them. The delegate of Khuza'ah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed of what the people did, called him to help, and recited poetry therein. Hammad said: This poetry is partly from Ayyub, partly from Zayd ibn Hazim, and mostly from Muhammad ibn Ishaq."
"5062","5402 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri and others, similar to it. Except that he mentioned the pleader to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this poetry was Amr ibn Salim. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not include Khuza'ah in the share of the near kin, for the alliance between him and them, it is impossible that giving Banu al-Muttalib was for alliance. Had their giving also been for alliance, he would have given the freedmen of Banu Hashim, but he gave them nothing. As for what Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah's mercy be upon them both, went to of what we mentioned from them, it is the best of all these sayings in our view, for we see people in our time ascribed to al-Abbas, likewise the family of Ali, Ja'far, Aqil, al-Zubayr and Talhah: the children of all of them are ascribed only to their highest father, so it is said: Banu al-Abbas, Banu Ali, and Banu those we mentioned, until that gathers them, and they have become through their fathers distinct like different tribes. If one says: We saw when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided the share of the near kin, he only placed it in those joined to him by a pre-Islamic father, so the sons of that father were of his kin; likewise those to whom Abu Talhah gave what he gave of those we mentioned, only a pre-Islamic father joins them and him - then why did you say a man's kin is whoever is joined to him by his furthest fathers in Islam? It is said to him: We mentioned above in this book of ours that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave kin and withheld kin, and all whom he gave and all whom he withheld of those he did not give were in position to him, and the position of the one he gave joins him and them in one tribe to which they are ascribed, until they are all called the Qurashis, and they are not ascribed beyond Quraysh so it is said: the Kinanis. So the people of the tribe all became sons of one father and one kinship, distinct from others, not ascribed to him. Likewise every father arising in Islam that becomes a clan or tribe to which his children are ascribed in Islam: he and his children are all ascribed to one tribe that preceded Islam, so they are all of that tribe's people. This is the best saying in this chapter in our view, and Allah we ask for success."
"5063","5403 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Thabit ibn Ya'qub narrated to me, from Dawud ibn Sa'id ibn Abi al-Zubayr, from Malik ibn Anas, may Allah's mercy be upon him, from his uncle Abu Suhayl ibn Malik, who said: This is the letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz on fay and spoil. To proceed: Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, sent down the Quran upon Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as insights and mercy to a believing people. He legislated therein the religion, brightened thereby the path, diversified thereby speech, and clarified what is sought of what attains His pleasure, and what is avoided of His prohibitions and angers. Then He permitted His lawful which He widened, and forbade His unlawful, making it shunned and its people hated. Of what He mercifully granted this nation and widened upon them is what He permitted of spoil, extending it and not forbidding it to them, as He had tried therewith the people of prophethood and scripture before them. Of that was what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) transferred to a private group apart from people, of what he gained of the properties of Banu Qurayzah and al-Nadir, when Allah says at that time: {Whatever Allah bestowed upon His Messenger of them, you urged over it neither horses nor camels, but Allah gives His messengers mastery over whom He wills, and Allah is over all things Powerful}. Those properties were exclusively for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); no fifth nor spoil share was due therein, for Allah and His Messenger to administer its affair. He chose the needy therewith, the foremost, upon what He inspires him thereof and permits him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not harm therewith, nor choose it for himself nor his relatives, nor single out any of them therewith by obligation nor shares; rather he preferred, with its widest and most, the people of truth and precedence among the Emigrants: {who were expelled from their homes and properties, seeking bounty from Allah and pleasure, and helping Allah and His Messenger; those are the truthful}. Allah apportioned groups thereof among the needy of the Helpers. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept a group thereof for his emergency and right and what befalls him, missing nothing thereof, nor preferring himself therewith, nor intending anyone after him be given it; he made it a charity not inherited, with none having a coveted share therein in this world, despising it and preferring what is with Allah. This is what no horses nor camels were urged over. And of the extra grants by which Allah favored His Messenger, making none therein like what He made for him of spoil - over which those differed who differed - is Allah's saying, the Mighty and Majestic: {Whatever Allah bestowed upon His Messenger of the townspeople belongs to Allah and to the Messenger and to the near kin and orphans and the poor and the wayfarer, that it not be a circulation among the rich of you}. Then He said: {Whatever the Messenger gives you, take it; whatever he forbids you, refrain; fear Allah, Allah is severe in punishment}. As for His saying belongs to Allah: Allah, blessed and exalted, is free of the world, its people and all therein, and all that is His; but He says: place it in His path which He commanded. His saying and to the Messenger: the Messenger had no share in spoil except like the share of the generality of Muslims; but He says: to the Messenger belong its division, acting therewith and judgment therein. As for His saying and to the near kin: ignorants among people thought the kin of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) have an imposed share of spoil, cut off from them and not given them. Had it been so, He would have clarified it as He clarified the obligations of inheritances: the half, quarter, sixth and eighth; nor would richness with one of them or poverty reduce their share thereof, as that does not cut the heirs' share of their portions. Rather the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted them therein something of spoil: estates, captives, livestock, goods and cash. But there was in none of that a known obligation nor a precedent followed, until Allah took His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except that he apportioned among them a portion on the day of Khaybar, not including thereby all of them that day, nor singling out a near one to the exclusion of another needier. He gave that day one with no kinship, when they complained to him of need, and of what was from them beside him of their people, and what reached their allies thereof; he did not prefer them over them for their kinship. Had the near kin a right, as those thought, his maternal uncles would be kin, and his father's and grandfather's maternal uncles, and everyone touching him by a womb; for that is all kinship. As had that been as they thought, Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, would have given them it after fay widened and multiplied. And Abu al-Hasan - meaning Ali, may Allah be pleased with him - when he ruled what he ruled, with none objecting to him therein, would he not have taught them thereof a matter to act upon therein, known after him. Had that been as they claimed, Allah the Exalted would not have said: {that it not be a circulation among the rich of you}, for among the kin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were those rich and in ease when the Quran descended and after that. Had that share been valid for him and them, it would have been a circulation; indeed it would have been an inheritance to his kin, none ever allowed to cut or break it. Rather He says: to the near kin, by their right and kinship in need. The binding right is like the Muslims' right, in poverty and need; when rich, no right for him. The orphan in his orphanhood - even if the orphan inherited from his heir, no right for him. The wayfarer in his travel and state - even if of great wealth, eased upon - no right for him therein; that right is returned to the needy. Allah sent those He sent, and mentioned the near orphan and the dusty poor; all of them thus. Neither the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nor the righteous predecessors would leave a right Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, imposed for the kin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and stand for them with Allah's right therein, as He said: {Establish prayer and give zakah}, and the rulings of the Quran. They carried out thereupon gifts of gifts placed in people's fays; some given of those gifts are not upon Islam, yet they carried that out for them. Whoever claims other than this is a forger speaking upon Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, His Messenger and the righteous believers, of those who followed other than truth. As for the saying of one who says on the fifth: Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, imposed known obligations wherein is the right of whom He named - then the fifth in this matter is like spoil. Allah gave His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) captives; he took people thereof and left his daughter, though she had shown him her hands from the millstone's place; he entrusted her to Allah's remembrance and glorification. She claimed a right for his kinship. Had this fifth and fay been as the proponent of this saying thinks, it would have been injustice upon the Muslims and appropriation of what Allah bestowed upon them; division thereof would not have been suspended among claimants by kinship, lineage and inheritance; shares of male kin and slave-mothers would have entered therein. One versed in religion sees that not agreeing with Allah's saying, the Mighty and Majestic, to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {Say: Whatever wage I asked you is yours}, and {I ask you no wage for it, nor am I of the pretenders}, and the prophets' saying to their peoples like that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would neither claim what is not his, nor leave a share nor portion for himself nor another; Allah chose it for them and favored them therein, nor would deprive them of it. The women of Banu Sa'd ibn Bakr asked him ransom and the Muslims' freeing of their captives, after they had been fay; he ransomed and released them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said while asking of their cattle a tree with his cloak, thinking they pulled it from him: Had the number of Tihamah's trees been livestock, I would have divided it among you; I am no more entitled to it than you by a hair taken from a camel's hump, except the fifth, for it is returned among you. In this is clarification of the fay places the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) directed therein, by Allah's ruling and just decree. Whoever turns from this or deviates therein, naming the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by other than what his Lord named him, is thereby a forger, liar, distorting Allah's saying, the Mighty and Majestic, from its places, persisting thereon - and whoever follows him thereon - in denial, to what the misguided of the two scriptures became who claim upon their prophets. Abu Ja'far said: Others said: Allah only made the fifth's affair to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to place it among his kin he deems, rich or poor, with whom He commanded he give of the fifth besides them shown in the fifth's verse; therefore He commanded him in the fay verse too. When they differed therein this difference we described, we had to look therein to extract a sound saying from these sayings. We considered the saying of one who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave of his kin whom he gave, giving by an obligatory right for them - though Allah did not mention them in the spoils verse nor the fay verse. We found this saying corrupt, for we saw him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) give kin and withhold kin. Had what Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, attributed to them in the spoils verse and fay verse been by way of obligation for them, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have deprived any of them; he would have included them all with what Allah made for them, not being in anything thereof outside what Allah commanded him therein. Do you not see that had a man bequeathed to the kin of so-and-so a third of his property, specified and known, the executor of his will may not place the third in some kin to the exclusion of the rest until he includes them all with the third he bequeaths them, equalizing among them therein; if he does otherwise therein, he opposes what he was commanded. Far be it from Allah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) be in anything of his act toward what Allah commanded him opposing, abandoning His ruling. Since what he gave of what he spent among his near kin did not include all his kin, it is impossible thereby that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, made for his kin (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what He withheld from them; for had a specific thing been made for his kin, they would have been like the kin of so-and-so bequeathed a third of property, whose executor may neither withhold some nor favor one over one. Thereby this saying is void. Then we considered the saying of those who said: No right became due to the kin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the fay verse nor the spoils verse; rather He stressed his affair by Allah mentioning them - i.e. they are given for their kinship, poverty and need. We found this saying corrupt, for had that been as they said, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have given the rich of Banu Hashim - among them al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah's pleasure be upon them both; he gave him with them, though wealthy in pre-Islam and Islam, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hastened from him ... The near kin's share is not for poverty, but for another meaning. Had he given them for poverty, what he gave them would have been of the way of charity, and charity is forbidden them."
"5064","5404 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Burayd ibn Abi Maryam, from Abu al-Hawra al-Sa'di who said: I said to al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both: What do you remember of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: I remember I took a date of charity dates and put it in my mouth, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it out and cast it among the dates. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, what harm to you in this date for this boy? He said: We, the family of Muhammad, charity is not lawful to us."
"5065","5405 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they both said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Thabit ibn Umarah, from Rabi'ah ibn Shayban who said: I said to al-Hasan, then he mentioned similar to it, except that he said at its end: Nor to any of his household."
"5066","5406 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad and Sa'id, sons of Zayd, narrated to us, from Abu Jahdam Musa ibn Salim, from Abdullah ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: We entered upon Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled us out with nothing to the exclusion of people except three: perfecting ablution, not eating charity, and not breeding donkeys upon horses."
"5067","5407 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us. (h). Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Shababah ibn Sawwar narrated to us. Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us. 5408 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, from Abu Hurayrah who said: al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, took a date of charity dates and put it in his mouth. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Kakh, kakh; cast it, cast it. Do you not know we do not eat charity."
"5068","5409 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they both said: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, from Bahz ibn Hakim, from his father, from his grandfather who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say regarding grazing camels: In every forty a she-camel in its third year; whoever gives it seeking reward gets its reward, and whoever withholds it, I shall take it from him and half his camels, a firm decree of our Lord's decrees; none of it is lawful to any of us."
"5069","5410 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam ibn Marwan al-Darir narrated to us. (h). 5411 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, they both said: Ma'ruf ibn Wasil al-Sa'di narrated to us, he said: I heard Hafsah in the year ninety - Ibn Abi Dawud said in his hadith: daughter of Talq - say: Rashid ibn Malik Abu Umayr narrated to us, he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a tray of dates was brought. He said: Charity or gift? He said: Rather charity. He said: So he placed it before the people, with al-Hasan before him. The boy took a date and put it in his mouth, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) inserted his finger, gently with him, took it out and cast it away, then said: We, the family of Muhammad, do not eat charity."
"5070","5412 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hakim al-Awdi narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abdullah ibn Isa, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from his father who said: I entered with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the charity house, and al-Hasan took a date and he took it out of his mouth, saying: We, a household, charity is not lawful to us."
"5071","5413 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Isbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us. Then he mentioned with his chain similar to it. Except that he said: We, a household, charity is not lawful to us, without doubt. Abu Ja'far, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Do you not see that the charity lawful to the rest of the poor besides Banu Hashim by way of poverty is not lawful to Banu Hashim as it is lawful to others. Likewise fay and spoil: had what they are given thereof been upon the aspect of poverty, it would not have been lawful to them. As for what the people of this saying argued for their saying, of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding Fatimah with glorification, when she asked him to give her a servant upon captives coming to him, entrusting her then with what he commanded her of glorification and not giving her any of the captives - he mentioned therein what..."
"5072","5414 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam who said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrating from Ali, that Fatimah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them both, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complaining to him the millstone's trace in her hands. It had reached her the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had received captives, so she came asking him for a servant, but she did not meet him; A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with them both, met her, and she informed her the story. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, she informed him thereof. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us after we had taken our beds, and we went to rise. He said: Shall I not guide you to better than what you asked? You shall magnify Allah thirty-four times, glorify Allah thirty-three times, and praise thirty-three times, when you take your beds; it is better for you than a servant."
"5073","5415 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said to Fatimah one day: Allah has brought your father abundance of slaves, so seek a servant from him. She came to him and mentioned that to him. He said: By Allah, I shall not give you, leaving the people of al-Suffah folding their stomachs, finding nothing to spend upon them; rather I shall sell them and spend upon them. Shall I not guide you to better than what you asked, taught me by Jibril, Allah's blessings upon him? Magnify ten times at the end of every prayer, praise ten times, glorify ten times; and when you go to your bed. Then he mentioned like what he mentioned in the hadith of Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb. Abu Ja'far said: If one says: Do you not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not give her a servant of the captives, though had she therein a right by what Allah mentioned of the near kin in the spoils verse and fay verse, he would not have withheld that from her and preferred others over her. Do you not see him say: By Allah, I shall not give you, leaving the people of al-Suffah folding their stomachs, finding nothing to spend upon them. It is said to him: His withholding her may be because she was not with him a kinswoman, but nearer than kinship; for a child may not be said to be his father's kin - kinship is only beyond the child. Do you not see Allah's saying, the Mighty and Majestic, in His Book: {Say: Whatever good you spend is for parents and nearest}; He made parents other than nearest. Just as parents are excluded from their child's kinship, likewise their child is excluded from their kinship. Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah's mercy be upon him, said regarding a man who bequeaths a third of his property to the kin of so-and-so: His parents and child do not enter therein, for they are nearer than kinship. It is possible the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not give Fatimah what she asked for this meaning. If one says: It was also reported from him regarding other than Fatimah of Banu Hashim like this as well - then he mentioned what..."
"5074","5416 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, he said: Ayyash ibn Uqbah narrated to me, he said: al-Fadl ibn al-Hasan ibn Amr narrated to me, from Ibn Umm al-Hakam, that his mother narrated to him she went, she and her sister, until they entered upon Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her. They all went out and came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he had come from one of his raids, with slaves with him. They asked him to give them servants. He said: The orphans of Badr's people preceded you."
"5075","5417 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb dictated to us, from Ayyash ibn Uqbah al-Hadrami, that al-Fadl ibn al-Hasan ibn Amr ibn Umayyah narrated to him, that Ibn Umm al-Hakam, or Duba'ah, daughters of al-Zubayr ibn Abd al-Muttalib, narrated to him from one of them, that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gained captives. I and my sister and Fatimah daughter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went, complained to him of our state, and asked him to give us something of the captives. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The orphans of Badr preceded you. But I shall guide you to what is better for you: you shall magnify Allah after every prayer thirty-three takbirs, thirty-three tasbihs, thirty-three tahmids, and there is no god but Allah alone without partner; to Him belongs dominion and praise, and He is over all things Powerful, once. Ayyash said: And they are the paternal cousins of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5076","5418 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. Except that he said: (And I do not know what the man's name is, nor his father's name?) It was said to him: This is not a proof for you against one who makes obligatory the share of the near relatives; because he only makes it obligatory for one whom the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw fit to favor with it. So it is possible that he favored with it his near relative from among the orphans of the people of Badr, and from among the weak who had become, due to their weakness, from the people of al-Suffah. So when the saying of one who held that the share of the near relatives is one, for all of them together, on the view that they in his opinion are Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib exclusively, not extended to others, was negated, and the saying of one who said: the right of the near relatives is a fifth from the spoils of war, and from fay by their poverty and need, by what we argued against each of the two sayings, the other saying was established, which is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had the right to single out with it whom he wished of them, and to deprive whom he wished of them. If one says: What is your proof for that? It is said to him: We have mentioned of the proofs for that in what preceded in this book what suffices us from repeating it here, besides that we add further clarification on it also."
"5077","5419 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, he said: Juwayriyah ibn Asma narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from al-Zuhri, that Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn Nawfal ibn al-Harith narrated to him, that Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith narrated to him, he said: Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith and al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib gathered and said: (If only we sent these two boys, mine and al-Fadl ibn Abbas, over the charity, so they discharge what the people discharge and obtain what the people obtain). He said: While they were about that, Ali ibn Abi Talib came and stood over them, and that was mentioned to him, so Ali said: (Do not do it, for by Allah he will not do it). They said: Nothing prevents you from this except jealousy toward us; by Allah you attained marriage kinship with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and we were not jealous of you. Ali said: (I am Abu Hasan, send them). So they went and he lay down; when he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr we raced him to the chamber and stood at it until he came, and he took our ears and said: (Bring out what you conceal). Then he entered and we entered upon him, and he was on that day with Zaynab bint Jahsh, and we left the speech to one another. Then one of us spoke and said: O Messenger of Allah, you are the most dutiful and most joining of people, and we have reached marriage age, and we have come to you to appoint us over some of the charities, so we deliver to you as they deliver and obtain as they obtain. He was silent until we wanted to speak to him, and Zaynab kept signaling to us from behind the curtain not to speak to him. He said: (Charity is not befitting for the family of Muhammad; it is only the dirt of people. Call Mahmiyah to me - and he was over the fifth - and Nawfal ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib). So they came to him. He said to Mahmiyah: (Marry this boy, your daughter) to al-Fadl ibn Abbas, so he married him. And he said to Nawfal ibn al-Harith: (Marry this boy), so he married me. Then he said to Mahmiyah: (Pay the dower for them from the fifth, such and such). Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Mahmiyah to pay the dower for them from the fifth, and he did not divide the fifth after that according to the number of Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib, so the amount for each one of them would be known. That indicates that what Allah named for the near relatives in the two verses we mentioned at the beginning of this book of ours is not for particular people by their kinship. If it were so, equality among them in it would have been obligatory, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have kept it in Mahmiyah's hand apart from his family until he placed it among them, just as he did not keep the four fifths of the spoils from their people nor appoint a keeper over it apart from them. In the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointing over the fifth from the spoils one who guards it until he places it where the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) orders him, his placing it therein is proof that its ruling is to him as to whom he sees among his near relatives. Had the near relatives a specific right, no share of which may be diverted from each one's lot to another, even if kin, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have withheld a right for al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, nor for Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith, nor from others, until he delivers to each one his right; and al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas and Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabi'ah would not have needed that something Allah made for them by the verse mentioning them be paid as dower for them. In the absence of what we mentioned is sound proof and standing argument that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed among his near relatives in whom he placed it, and what he could divert from them to like relatives, none of them was worthier of it than another, except whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw to place it in; by that he is worthier than whom he saw to deprive of it. In that is also another proof, which is:"
"5078","5420 - Fahd ibn Sulayman ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Budayl ibn Maysarah, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from a man of Bulqayn, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in Wadi al-Qura, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, for whom is the booty? He said: For Allah is a share, and for these are four shares. I said: Is anyone more entitled to anything of the booty than anyone? He said: No, even an arrow one of you takes from his side, he is not more entitled to it than his brother."
"5079","5421 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from a man of Bulqayn, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5080","5422 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, he said: I used to sit with Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he said: The delegation of Abd al-Qays, when they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Who are the people? Or: Who is the delegation? They said: Rabi'ah. He said: Welcome to the people - or: to the delegation - not disgraced nor regretful. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we cannot come to you except in the sacred month, so order us with a comprehensive decisive matter by which we inform those behind us and enter Paradise. He said: Do you know what faith in Allah alone is? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: Testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, establishment of prayer, giving of zakat, fasting of Ramadan, and that you give the fifth from the booty."
"5081","5423 - Rabi al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The delegation of Abd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So it is known that he attributed the fifth from the spoils to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and did not attribute to Him four fifths of it, and that what is besides it from it is for people without particular individuals, which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) places among them as he sees. Had it been for the known-numbered near relatives it would not have been so. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to take the fifth to place it where he saw fit to place it, and divide what remained after it into shares. That indicates that what he divided into shares is for particular people, none may withhold it from them, and that what he takes and does not divide until he puts his judgment into it is that which is not for particular people, and it is referred to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he places it where he sees fit."
"5082","5424 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Hilal al-Rasibi narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The delegation of Abd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Between us and you is this tribe of Mudar, and we cannot come to you except in the sacred month, so order us with a matter we take and convey to those after us. He said: I command you with four and forbid you from four: testimony that there is no god but Allah, that you establish prayer, give zakat, and give Allah's share from the spoils and the select share; and I forbid you from hantam, dubba, naqir and muzaffat (vessels for wine)."
"5083","5425 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took his sword Dhu al-Faqar as an extra share on the day of Badr."
"5084","5426 - Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, he said: al-Ashja'i narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mutarrif, he said: I asked al-Sha'bi about the share of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): is it like the share of one of the Muslims, and the select share was chosen by him, if he wished a slave, if he wished a maid, if he wished a horse."
"5085","5427 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took as extra his sword Dhu al-Faqar on the day, and it is the one about which he saw the vision on the day of Uhud."
"5086","5428 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad informed me, from Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi, from Ibn Shihab, from Malik ibn Aws, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him, said in what he argues with: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had three select properties: Banu al-Nadir, Khaybar and Fadak. As for Banu al-Nadir, it was reserved for his emergencies; as for Fadak, it was reserved for wayfarers; as for Khaybar, he split it into three parts: he divided a part among the Muslims, reserved a part for maintenance, and what remained beyond his family he returned to the poor of the Muhajirun, may Allah be pleased with them."
"5087","5429 - Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: al-Jurayri informed us, from Abu al-Ala, he said: While I was with Mutarrif at the top of al-Mirbad in the camel market, a Bedouin came to us with a piece of leather or a piece of sack, al-Jurayri doubted, and said: Is there among you one who reads? I said: I read. He said: Here, read it, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote it for us. And in it was: From Muhammad the Prophet to Banu Zuhayr ibn Uqaysh - a clan of Ukl - they testified that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and separated from the polytheists, and acknowledged the fifth in their spoils and the share of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his select share, for they are secure with Allah's security. Someone said to him: Did you hear anything from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) you narrate to us? He said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes that the rancor of the chest leaves him, let him fast the month of patience and three days of every month. A man said: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Do you think I lie about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I will not narrate to you a hadith today, and he took it and left. Abu Jafar said: They all agreed that this share is not for the caliph after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that he is not in it like the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Since the caliph does not succeed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what was his of what Allah singled him out with apart from the other fighters with him, his relatives are more deserving not to succeed the relatives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what was theirs in his lifetime from fay and booty. Thus the saying that the share of the near relatives after the Prophet's death is for the caliph's relatives is void. Then we returned to what people said besides this of the sayings mentioned in this section. As for one who singles out Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib apart from other relatives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and makes the share for them exclusively, we mentioned the corruption of his saying before in this book, so it sufficed from repeating it here. Likewise one who makes it for the poor of the Prophet's relatives apart from their rich, making them like other poor Muslims, we mentioned the corruption of his saying before. There remains the saying of those who say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had the right to place it where he saw fit among his relatives, and none of them deserved anything until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave it to him; he could select from the booty for himself what he saw. That was cut off with his death, not obligatory for anyone after his death."
"5088","5430 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, he said: My uncle Juwayriyah ibn Asma narrated to me, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Yazid ibn Hurmuz, he narrated that Najdah, chief of al-Yamamah, wrote to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, asking him about the share of the near relatives. So Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, wrote to him: (It is ours, and Umar ibn al-Khattab invited us to marry our unmarried from it, and pay our debtor from it, but refused to hand it all to us, and we hold it is ours)."
"5089","5431 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Qays narrating, from Yazid ibn Hurmuz, he said: Najdah wrote to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, asking him about the share of the near relatives whom Allah mentioned and obligated for them. He wrote to him, and I am witness: (We held they are the relatives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but our people refused that to us). It was said to him: We do not deny we were opposed in what we held of what we mentioned, but Abdullah ibn Abbas held that the share of the near relatives is established, and that they are Banu Hashim, in the Prophet's life and after his death, and he reported that his people refused that to him, among them Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and those who followed him, may Allah be pleased with them. On that, one like those mentioned has his saying opposed to Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"5090","5432 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Bishr al-Khathami, from Ibn Humamah, he said: A jar with silver from Dayr Harb fell upon me, so I brought it to Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: (Divide it into five fifths, take four and bring a fifth). When I turned he said: (Are there poor needy in your area?) I said: Yes. He said: (Take it and divide it among them). Do you not see that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, ordered him to divide the fifth from buried treasure among the poor of his area, and did not obligate him to pay anything of it to any of the near relatives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This is contrary to what Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, held on it."
"5091","5433 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Azhar ibn Sa'd al-Samman narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, he said: Umayr ibn Ishaq narrated to me, he said: Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn Umayyah narrated to me - O Allah, or he narrated to the people while I was among them - he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to me, he said: Umar sent to me at noon, so I came to him; when I reached the door I heard severe weeping, so I said: (To Allah we belong and to Him we return), Umar the Commander of the Faithful has been seized. I entered until I came and my hand fell on him, and I said: No harm upon you, O Commander of the Faithful. He said: Did what you saw please you? I said: Yes. He said: The family of al-Khattab is insignificant to Allah; had we been honored to Him He would have taken me to my companion before me. Then he said: Sit, let us think. So we wrote those deserving in Allah's path, and wrote the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and those below that, and those deserving in Allah's path got four thousand, and the Mothers of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with them, and those below that got a thousand, until we distributed the wealth. Do you not see that Umar and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf equated those deserving and the rank after them, and did not include the near relatives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for their kinship, as they included entitlement by its entitlement."
"5092","5434 - Yazid ibn Sinan also narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Raja al-Hashimi narrated to us, he said: Abu Mashar narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar ibn Abdullah, freedman of Ghufrah, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, took charge, wealth came to him from Bahrain, and he said: (Whoever had a promise upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), let him come to me and take). Jabir ibn Abdullah came and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) promised me when wealth from Bahrain came he would give me thus and thus and thus three times, filling his two hands. He said: Take with your hand. He took with his hand and found it five hundred, and said: Count to it a thousand. Then he gave whoever the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had promised something, then divided the rest among people, and each got ten dirhams. The next year much more wealth came, and he divided it, and each got twenty dirhams, and surplus remained. He said: O people, surplus remains, and you have servants who treat for you and work for you; if you wish we give them a portion, so he gave them five dirhams each. It was said: O caliph of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), if only you favored the Muhajirun and Ansar by their merit. He said: Their reward is upon Allah; this is spoils, and equality in spoils is better than preference. When Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, died and Umar succeeded, conquests opened and more wealth came, and he said: Abu Bakr had an opinion in this wealth and I have another; Abu Bakr saw to divide equally, and I see to favor the Muhajirun and Ansar, and not make one who fought the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) like one who fought with him. So he favored them: he made for those who witnessed Badr five thousand, and for one with Islam like theirs but who did not witness Badr four thousand, and for people according to their Islam and ranks. He fixed for the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) twelve thousand each, except Safiyyah and Juwayriyah, for whom he fixed six thousand each, but they refused to take. He said: I fixed for you by migration. They said: You fixed for them for their place with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we have the like of their place, so Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saw that and made them equal. He fixed for al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib twelve thousand for his kinship to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and fixed for himself five thousand, and for Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, five thousand, sometimes adding something, and fixed for al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, five thousand each, joining them to their father for their kinship to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and fixed for Usamah ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, four thousand. When he came to Madinah he خطب, and said in his sermon: Abu Bakr held in this wealth an opinion: he held to divide among them equally, and I hold to favor the Muhajirun and Ansar by their merit; if I live this year I shall return to Abu Bakr's opinion, for it is better than mine. Do you not see that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, when he divided, equaled among all people, and did not advance the near relatives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over others, nor make for them a share in that wealth distinguishing them from people. That is proof he held they have after the Messenger of Allah's death (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) no right in fay wealth, besides what they take as one takes who is not of the near relatives. Then this Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when the matter came to him and he held preference among people by ranks, did not make for the near relatives a share distinguishing them over people; rather he made them and all people equal, and preferred among them by ranks, apart from what they deserve by kinship, had its people a standing share. That indicates what we held of the lifting of the share of the near relatives after the Messenger of Allah's death (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), by a hadith narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5093","5435 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Malik ibn Aws, he said: I was sitting with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when Ali and al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, came disputing. Al-Abbas said: (O Commander of the Faithful, judge between me and this liar, sinner). Hammad said: I euphemize the speech. He said: By Allah I will judge between you. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died and Abu Bakr took charge of his charity, he was strong over it and rendered trust in it, and this one claimed he betrayed and sinned, and a word Ayyub said; and Allah knows he neither betrayed nor sinned. 5436 - Hammad said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Malik and others, from al-Zuhri, that he said: (He was in it rightly guided, following truth), then he returned to Ayyub's hadith. When Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, died, I took charge of it after him, and was strong over it and rendered trust, and this one claims I betrayed and sinned. In Amr's hadith from al-Zuhri: (I was in it rightly guided, following truth). Then he returned to Ikrimah's hadith. Then they came to me and said: Hand to us the charity of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I handed it to them. This one said to this one: Give me my share from my brother's son, and this one said to this one: Give me my share from my wife from her father, though he knew that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is not inherited; what he leaves is charity. In Amr's hadith from al-Zuhri: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: We are not inherited; what we leave is charity. Then he returned to Ikrimah's hadith. Then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, recited: {Alms are only for the poor and needy and workers over them} the verse. So this is for these. Then he recited: {And know that whatever you gain, a fifth of it is for Allah and the Messenger and the near relatives} to the end of the verse. Then he said: And this is for these. In Amr's hadith from al-Zuhri he said: {Whatever Allah bestowed upon His Messenger from them, you did not urge over it horse nor camel} to the end of the verse. It was exclusive to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what Muslims did not urge over with horse nor camel; he took from it his provision and his family's provision, and made the rest for his family. Then he returned to Ayyub's hadith. Then he recited: {Whatever Allah bestowed upon His Messenger from the people of the towns is for Allah and the Messenger and the near relatives} to the end, then: {For the poor Muhajirun who were expelled from their homes and wealth} until {they are the truthful}; these are the Muhajirun. Then he read: {Those who settled the home and faith before them} until {they are the successful}; these are the Ansar. Then he read: {Those who came after them} until {Kind, Merciful}. This verse encompassed the Muslims; none has a right except what you own of your slaves. If I live, Allah willing, none will remain without his right. What he did not have with him for them - how is other than that imagined of him in what Malik ibn Aws narrated from him? Rather the meaning of what Malik ibn Aws narrated from him in this hadith, of his saying: (So this is for these), i.e., it is for them upon the meaning Allah made it for them at the time He sent down the verse upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning them, and upon like what He meant of what He made for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein of the share He attributed to him. That share was not جاری for him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his life and after his death uninterrupted to the Day of Resurrection; rather it was جاری for him in his life, cut off from him by his death. Likewise what He attributed therein to his near relatives is likewise obligatory for them in his life, which he, peace be upon him, places in whom he wills of them, lifted by his death. Just as Umar's saying: so this is for these, does not necessitate the permanence of the Messenger of Allah's share (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the time he said what he said, likewise his saying: so it is for these, does not necessitate permanence of the share of the near relatives to the time he said what he said. A sound lasting counter: that this hadith of Malik ibn Aws from Umar contradicts the hadith of Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, on the share of the near relatives."
"5094","5437 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Kalbi, from Abu Salih, from Umm Hani, that Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: (O Abu Bakr, who inherits you when you die?) He said: My children and family. She said: (Why do you inherit the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) apart from me?) He said: O daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), your father did not bequeath a house nor gold nor slave. She said: (Nor Allah's share which He made for us, and our select property in your hand?) He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: It is only a sustenance Allah fed me; when I die it is among the Muslims."
"5095","5438 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Saib, from Abu Salih, from Umm Hani, that Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, said to Abu Bakr: (Who inherits you when you die?) He said: My children and family. She said: (Why do you inherit the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) apart from us?) He said: O daughter of the Messenger of Allah, your father did not bequeath a house, nor wealth, nor slave, nor gold, nor silver. She said: (Fadak which Allah made for us, and our select property in your hand for us). He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: It is only a sustenance Allah fed me; when I die it is among the Muslims. Do you not see that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that what he gave his relatives was only from a sustenance Allah fed him and owned him in his life, and cut it from his relatives by his death. We mentioned at the beginning of this book from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with them, that he said: People differed after the Messenger of Allah's death; one said: the share of the near relatives is for the caliph's relatives, and one said: the Prophet's share is for the caliph after him, then their opinion agreed to make these two shares for horses and equipment in Allah's path, in Abu Bakr's rule, may Allah be pleased with him. When they agreed after differing, their consensus was proof. In what they agreed is nullification of the share of the near relatives from spoils and fay after the Messenger of Allah's death. If one says: As for what you narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, it was only in what he held, following Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from dislike to claim opposition to them."
"5096","5439 - And he mentioned on that what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: I asked Abu Jafar, I said: Do you see Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, when he ruled Iraq and what he ruled of people's affairs, how did he do with the share of the near relatives? He said: He followed with it - by Allah - the path of Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. I said: How, while you say what you say? He said: By Allah, his family issued only from his opinion. I said: What prevented him? He said: He disliked - by Allah - to be accused of opposing Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. It was said to him: This Muhammad ibn Ali interpreted about Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, in his leaving opposition to Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, while he in reality holds contrary to what they held. That is not permissible - in our view - for Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, nor imagined of one like him; how is it imagined of him when he opposed Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, in things, and opposed Umar alone in other things? Among them: what he held of permissibility of selling ummahat al-awlad after Umar's prohibition of selling them, and of that what he held of equality among people in stipends, while Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, preferred among them according to their precedence. Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, knew Allah better than to run something on what the truth in his view is contrary to it; rather he ran the matter of the share of the near relatives on what he saw as truth and justice, and did not oppose Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, in it. Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, used to oppose Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, in their lives in things they held contrary to what he held, and he did not see blame upon him in that, nor did they prevent him from it, nor take him to account for it; how then would this be possible for him in a state when the imam is other than him, then he spits upon it in a state when he himself is the imam? This - in our view - is impossible."
"5097","5440 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Isa ibn Asim, from Zadham, he said: We were with Ali and we mentioned option (khiyar), and he said: As for the Commander of the Faithful Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he asked me about it, and I said: If she chooses her husband it is one and she is more entitled to it, and if she chooses herself it is one irrevocable. Umar said: (Not so, but if she chooses herself it is one and he is more entitled to her, and if she chooses her husband there is nothing). I could only follow the Commander of the Faithful. When the matter came to me, I knew I am questioned about private parts, so I took what I held. One of his companions said: An opinion you held, the Commander of the Faithful followed you on it, is dearer to me than an opinion you were alone in. He said: By Allah, Zayd ibn Thabit sent to me, and he opposed me and him, and said: (If she chooses her husband it is one and he is more entitled to her, and if she chooses herself it is three, not lawful for him until she marries another husband). Do you not see that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, reported in this hadith that when the matter came to him and he knew he is questioned about the private part he took what he held, and he did not see following Umar in what he holds contrary, may Allah be pleased with them both. Likewise also when the matter came to him it is impossible - with his knowledge of Allah, and with his knowledge he is questioned about wealth - that he permits it to one he sees not of its people, and withholds from it its people. Rather the saying with him on the share of the near relatives was like the saying with Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, so he ran the matter on that, not on other than it. As for Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah's mercy be upon them, the well-known from them on the share of the near relatives is that it was lifted with the Prophet's death (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that the fifth from spoils and all fay are divided into three shares: for orphans, needy and wayfarer."
"5098","5441 - Likewise Muhammad ibn al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi al-Lului narrated to me, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim informed us, from Abu Hanifah. And thus is known from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan in all narrated from him on that of his opinion, and of what he related from Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf, may Allah's mercy be upon them both."
"5099","5442 - As for the companions of dictation, Jafar ibn Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Yusuf dictated to us in Ramadan in the year 181, he said on His saying: {And know that whatever you gain, a fifth of it is for Allah and the Messenger and the near relatives and orphans and needy and wayfarer}: This in what reached us - and Allah knows best - is in what is taken from the armies of polytheists of spoils, and the fifth from it, on what Allah named in His Book; four fifths of it among the troops who took it, for the horse a share, for the man a share, on what came of hadiths and reports. Abu Hanifah, may Allah's mercy be upon him, said: For the man a share, for the horse a share, and the fifth is divided into five shares: Allah's and the Messenger's fifth is one, and the fifth of the near relatives; for each category Allah named in this verse a fifth of the fifth. In this narration is establishment of the share of the near relatives. They said: And Abu Yusuf dictated to us in a question (Abu Hanifah said: If the imam appears over a land of polytheist lands he has choice; he does what he sees best and good for Muslims: if he sees to make fifth the land and goods and divide four fifths among the troops who conquered with him, he does, and divides the fifth into three shares: for poor, needy and wayfarer. If he sees to leave the lands and leave their people in them, and make them dhimmah, and place upon them and their land kharaj, as Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, did with al-Sawad, all that is his). Abu Jafar said: In this narration is fall of the share of the near relatives, and this saying is the well-known from them. What both narrations agreed on on fay and on the fifth of booty is that when both are secured, the fifth of spoils is placed where it must be placed of what we mentioned. As for fay, begun from it is repairing bridges, building mosques, salaries of judges, salaries of troops, gifts of delegations, then what remains after that is placed in the like of what the fifth of spoils is placed in equally. These are the aspects of fay and fifths of spoils that ran in the Messenger of Allah's time (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he died. What must be followed in them after his death (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until the Day of Resurrection, we have clarified and explained to the utmost we owned, and Allah we ask for success."
"5100","5443 - As for Sufyan al-Thawri, Malik ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, he said: al-Ashjai narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us: The Prophet's share (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the fifth is a fifth of the fifth, and the rest is for these classes Allah named, and the four fifths for one who fought for it."
"5101","5444 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made truce with the people of Makkah, Khuzaah were allies of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Jahiliyyah, and Banu Bakr were allies of Quraysh. Khuzaah entered the truce of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Banu Bakr entered Quraysh's truce. Then fighting occurred between Khuzaah and Banu Bakr, and Quraysh supplied them with weapons and food, shaded over them, and Banu Bakr prevailed over Khuzaah and killed among them. Quraysh feared they were over a people who had broken, so they said to Abu Sufyan: Go to Muhammad, renew the alliance, make peace among people, and that there is not in a people who shaded over a people and supplied them with weapons and food what would make them breakers. Abu Sufyan went and traveled until he came to Madinah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Abu Sufyan has come to you, and he will return pleased without need. He came to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: O Abu Bakr, renew the alliance and make peace among people or among your people. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The matter is to Allah and His Messenger, and he had said in what he said to him that there is not in a people who shaded over a people and supplied them with weapons and food what would make them breakers. He said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The matter is to Allah and His Messenger. Then he came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and mentioned to him like what he mentioned to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Did you break? Whatever of it was new, Allah wore it out, and whatever of it was strong - or he said: firm - Allah cut it. Abu Sufyan said: I never saw like today a witness of ten. Then he came to Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said to her: O Fatimah, is there for you in a matter you lead the women of your people in, then mentioned to her like what he said to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, then said to her: So renew the alliance and make peace among people. She, may Allah be pleased with her, said: It is only to Allah and His Messenger. Then he came to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and said to him like what he said to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I never saw like today a more joining man; you are the chief of people, so renew the alliance and make peace among people. Abu Sufyan struck one leg over the other and said: You have taken between people some from some. Then he went until he came, and: By Allah you brought us neither war to beware, nor peace to be secure; return, return. With a mature camel. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had sent al-Zubayr from upper Makkah, and Khalid ibn al-Walid from lower Makkah. He said: He said to them: This is al-Zubayr from upper Makkah, this is Khalid from lower Makkah; and Khalid, and what is Khalid; and Khuza'ah with mutilated noses. Then he said: Whoever lays down his weapon is secure, whoever locks his door is secure, whoever enters Abu Sufyan's house is secure. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came; they shot something of arrows. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prevailed over them, and secured people except Khuza'ah against Banu Bakr. He mentioned four: Miqyas ibn Dubabah, Abdullah ibn Abi Sarh, Ibn Khatal, and Marah, freedwoman of Banu Hashim. Hammad said: Sabarah in Ayyub's hadith, or in another's hadith. He said: Khuza'ah fought them till midday. Allah revealed: {Will you not fight a people who broke their oaths and intended to expel the Messenger}, to His saying: {And He heals the breasts of a believing people}. Khuza'ah said: {And removes the rage of their hearts, and Allah turns to whom He wills}."
"5102","5446 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Ishaq say: Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihab al-Zuhri and others narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had made peace with Quraysh in the year of Hudaybiyah on that whoever loves to enter the covenant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his pact enters it, and whoever loves to enter Quraysh's covenant and pact enters it. Khuzaah and Banu Kab and others with them hastened and said: We are in the covenant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his pact. Banu Bakr hastened and said: We are in Quraysh's covenant and pact. Quraysh stood upon fulfilling that a year and part of a year, then Banu Bakr aggressed against Khuzaah, over what they have at lower Makkah. Al-Zubayr said to him: Attack them by night in it, and they hit a man of them, and the people exceeded and fought, and Quraysh reinforced Banu Bakr with weapons, and those of Quraysh who fought with arrows secretly fought with them, until they drove Khuzaah to the Haram. The leader of Banu Bakr that day was Nawfal ibn Muawiyah. When they reached the Haram, Banu Bakr said: O Nawfal, your god, your god, we have entered the Haram. He said a great word: You have no god today, O Banu Bakr; take your revenge. Khuzaah had hit before Islam three men, while they were deviating: Duwayb, Kulthum and Sulayman ibn al-Aswad ibn Zurayq ibn Yamar. By my life, O Banu Bakr, you steal in the Haram, will you not take your revenge in it? He said: They had hit a man of them on the night they attacked them by night at al-Watir, with him a man of his people called Munabbih, a single man; he and Tamim went out. Munabbih said: O Tamim, save yourself; as for me, by Allah I am dead, whether they kill me or not. Tamim left and Munabbih was caught and killed, and Tamim escaped. When he entered Makkah he joined the house of Budayl ibn Warqa and the house of Rafi, a freedman of theirs. Amr ibn Salim went out until he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he stood while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting in the mosque, and Amr said verses. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Banu Kab have been helped. Then Budayl ibn Warqa went out with a group of Khuzaah until they came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah, and informed him of what befell them, having returned. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had said: As if with Abu Sufyan having come to increase the covenant and increase the term. Then he mentioned like what is in Ayyub's hadith. And for his Companions. The others said: How is what you mentioned as you described, when you narrated that Abu Sufyan came as envoy to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah after what was between Banu Bakr and Khuza'ah of fighting, and after what was from Quraysh to Banu Bakr of aid, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knew his place, yet did not seize him nor touch him. That indicates he was with him in his security as before, not exiting it by what Banu Bakr did in fighting Khuza'ah, nor by what Quraysh did aiding Banu Bakr with food, weapons and shading, not breaking his security by the truce between him and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor exiting him from it. Of the argument against them for the others is that the Messenger of Allah's leaving Abu Sufyan (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not because the truce between the Messenger of Allah and Makkah's people stood; rather he left him because he was an envoy to him from Makkah's people, seeking a second truce, other than the first truce, due to the first truce's breach. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not touch him with killing nor other; because of the sunna of messengers is that they are not killed."
"5103","5447 - Then there was narrated from him on that what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated to us, he said: Abu Wail narrated to me, he said: Ibn Muayr al-Sa'di narrated to us, he said: I went out racing a horse of mine at al-Shajar, and passed a mosque of Banu Hanifah's mosques, and heard them testifying that Musaylimah is the Messenger of Allah. I returned to Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, and mentioned their matter to him. He sent the police and they seized them, and they were brought to him; they repented and returned from what they said, and said: We will not return, so he freed them. He brought forward a man of them called Abdullah ibn al-Nawwahah and struck his neck. People said: You seized people in one matter, freed some and killed some. He said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Ibn al-Nawwahah and a man with him called Ibn Hujr ibn Uthal came as two envoys from Musaylimah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Do you testify I am the Messenger of Allah? They said: Do you testify that Musaylimah is the Messenger of Allah? He said: I believe in Allah and His Messenger; had I been a killer of envoys I would have killed you both; therefore I killed this one."
"5104","5448 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, that al-Hasan ibn Ali ibn Abi Rafi narrated to him, that Abu Rafi informed him that he came with a letter from Quraysh to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: When I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Islam was cast into my heart, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah I will never return to them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not betray the covenant nor detain envoys, but return; if what is in your heart now remains, return. He said: I returned, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and became Muslim. Bukayr said: And he informed me that Abu Rafi was a Copt."
"5105","5449 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: Sa'd ibn Tariq narrated to me, from Salamah ibn Nuaym, from his father, he said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Musaylimah's messenger came with his letter, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says to them: And do you two say like what he says? They said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that envoys are not killed, I would have struck your necks. The proof of the people of Makkah's exit from the truce by what was between Banu Bakr and Khuzaah, and by Quraysh's aid to Banu Bakr in that, is Abu Sufyan's request to renew the alliance and confirm the truce when the people of Makkah asked him that. Had the truce not been broken, they would have had no need to that, and Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, Umar ibn al-Khattab, Ali and Fatimah daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when Abu Sufyan asked them what he asked of that, would have said: What need have you and the people of Makkah to that? You are all in truce and security, needing with them nothing else. Then this Amr ibn Salim, one of Khuzaah, implores the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what we mentioned of his imploring him, in the hadith of Ikrimah and al-Zuhri, and asks him in that for help, and says in what he implores him of that verses; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) does not deny that upon him. Then Amr ibn Salim disclosed to him the meaning by which Quraysh's breach was, what they had covenanted him upon, and agreed with him by saying verses; and he did not mention in that anyone besides Quraysh from Banu Nufathah nor from others. Then Hassan ibn Thabit composed in the poetry we mentioned from him, in Ikrimah's hadith, the meaning Amr ibn Salim mentioned in the poetry by which he implored the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). In that is proof that the men of Banu Kab were hit by Quraysh's breach by which they exited their covenant in the belly of Makkah. Do you not see him say verses. Then he mentioned what we clarified of who caused that, Quraysh and its men, and said: Suhayl ibn Amr, he was one of those the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contracted the truce with. As for what was mentioned to you: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he conquered it, did not divide wealth, nor enslave anyone, nor take land as booty, how does he enslave one he favored in his blood and wealth. As for the land of Makkah, people differed on the Prophet's leaving dealing with it. Whoever holds he conquered it by force said verses. Anyone, nor took land as booty; how does he enslave one he favored in his blood and wealth. As for Makkah's land, people differed on the Prophet's leaving dealing with it (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Whoever holds he conquered it by force said: He left it as favor upon them, like his favor upon them in their blood and all their wealth. Among them Abu Yusuf; because he held Makkah's land ownerships run as on other lands. Some said: Makkah's land was not of what spoils fell upon; because Makkah's land in their view has no ownerships. Among them Abu Hanifah and Sufyan al-Thawri, may Allah have mercy on them. We mentioned in this chapter the reports each party narrated who held what Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on them, held, in the Book of Sales, of the explanation of differing reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on rulings; that sufficed from repeating it here. Then speech returned to what establishes Makkah was opened by force. If you say: The two hadiths of al-Zuhri and Ikrimah we mentioned are interrupted. It is said to you: There was narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, a hadith indicating what we narrated."
"5106","5450 - Fahd ibn Sulayman ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to me, he said: al-Zuhri said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out for a journey and left when ten of Ramadan had passed; he fasted and people fasted with him until when he was at al-Kadid he broke fast. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went on until he camped at Marr al-Zahran with ten thousand Muslims, and Sulaym and Muzaynah heard. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) camped at Marr al-Zahran, news had been blinded to Quraysh; no news of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to them, nor did they know what he would do. That night Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, Hakim ibn Hizam and Budayl ibn Warqa went out looking whether they find good or hear it. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) camped at Marr al-Zahran, al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I said: O morning of Quraysh, if the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enters Makkah by force before they come to him seeking security, it is destruction of Quraysh to the end of time. He said: I sat on the white mule of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and went out on it until I entered al-Arak, meeting some woodcutters, or a milkman, or one in need coming to them, to inform them of the Messenger of Allah's place so they go out to him. He said: While I direct him and seek what I went out for, I heard the speech of Abu Sufyan and Budayl arguing, Abu Sufyan saying: I never saw like tonight fires nor army. Budayl said: By Allah this is Khuzaah, war has excited them. Abu Sufyan said: Khuzaah by Allah are too low for these to be their fires. I recognized Abu Sufyan's voice and said: O Abu Hanzalah. He recognized my voice and said: Abu al-Fadl? I said: Yes. He said: What is with you, may my father and mother be ransom for you? I said: Woe to you, this by Allah is the Messenger of Allah among people; O morning of Quraysh, by Allah if the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enters Makkah by force before they come seeking security, it is destruction of Quraysh to the end of time. He said: What is the trick, may my father and mother be ransom? I said: By Allah nothing except that you ride behind this beast and I bring you to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); for by Allah if he gets you he will strike your neck. He rode behind the mule and his two companions returned. Every time I passed a fire of the Muslims they said: Who is this? And when they saw the mule of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and me upon it they said: The uncle of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon his mule, until I passed the fire of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: Who is this? He stood to me, and when he saw Abu Sufyan on the back he said: Abu Sufyan, enemy of Allah! Praise to Allah who enabled you without covenant nor pact. Then he went out running to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I spurred the mule and preceded him, and I preceded what the two hasten to. I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Umar entered and said: O Messenger of Allah, this is Abu Sufyan; Allah has enabled him without covenant nor pact. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have granted him protection. When Umar persisted I said: Slowly, O Umar; by Allah had he been from Banu Adi ibn Kab you would not say this. He said: Slowly, O Abbas; by Allah your becoming Muslim that day was dearer to me than al-Khattab becoming Muslim had he become Muslim. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take him, O Abbas, to your lodging; when morning comes bring him to me. In the morning I brought him; when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him he said: Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan, has not the time come you know there is no god but Allah? He said: May my father and mother be ransom, how forbearing, generous, joining you are; by Allah I thought had there been with Allah another god it would have sufficed me. He said: Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan, has not the time come you know I am Allah's Messenger? He said: May my father and mother be ransom, as for this there is still something in myself. Al-Abbas said: I said: Woe to you, become Muslim and testify there is no god but Allah and Muhammad is Allah's Messenger before your neck is struck. So he became Muslim. Of the speech we mentioned from them. That indicates that were it not al-Abbas's protection, may Allah be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have prevented Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in what he wanted of killing Abu Sufyan. What exit from truce is absent? What breach of it is clearer than this? Then Abu Sufyan when he entered Makkah after that called at highest voice with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made for him: (Whoever enters Abu Sufyan's house is secure, whoever locks his door is secure). Quraysh did not say to him: What need have we to enter your house and lock our doors while we are in security, sufficed from seeking security by other? Rather they knew their exit from the first security and breach of the truce between them and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that when addressed with this speech they are not secure unless they do what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made them secure by doing, of entering Abu Sufyan's house or locking their doors. Then there was narrated from Umm Hani daughter of Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with her, what indicates the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah while it is an abode of war, not an abode of security."
"5107","5451 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: Said ibn Abi Hind narrated to me, from Abu Murrah, freedman of Aqil ibn Abi Talib, that Umm Hani daughter of Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with her, said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) camped at upper Makkah, two men of my in-laws from Banu Makhzum fled to me, and she was married to Hubayrah ibn Abi Wahb al-Makhzumi. My brother Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, entered upon me and said: I will kill them. I locked my house upon them, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at upper Makkah and found him bathing in a bowl in which was trace of dough, Fatimah his daughter, may Allah be pleased with her, screening him with a garment. When he bathed he took his garment and wrapped with it, then prayed (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eight rakahs of forenoon, then turned to me and said: Welcome, O Umm Hani, what brought you? I informed him the news of the two men and Ali's news, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: We have granted protection to whom you granted, and secured whom you secured."
"5108","5452 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Said al-Maqburi, may Allah be pleased with him, from Abu Murrah, freedman of Aqil, from Fakhitah Umm Hani, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bathed on the day of Makkah's conquest, then prayed eight rakahs in one garment differing between its ends. She said: I said: I granted protection to my two brothers-in-law of the polytheists, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, slips upon them to kill them. She said: He said: He had no right to that; we have granted whom you granted, and secured whom you secured. Do you not see that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, wanted to kill the two Makhzumis for Makkah? Had they been in security he would not have sought that from them. Umm Hani, may Allah be pleased with her, secured them so their blood becomes forbidden to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, by that. She did not say to him: (You have no way to killing them; because they and all Makkah are in truce and security). Then Umm Hani, may Allah be pleased with her, informed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what was from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and of her granting these two Makhzumis protection. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: We have granted whom you granted, and secured whom you secured, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not blame Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, in his wanting to kill them before Umm Hani's protection. That indicates that were it not her protection, killing them would have been sound; and it is impossible he may kill them while standing security and prior truce for them. This is the Messenger of Allah's entry (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) into Makkah; what is clearer than this? Then Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated in this chapter what is clearer than this."
"5109","5453 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah informed us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abdullah ibn Rabah, he said: We came as delegation to Muawiyah, among us Abu Hurayrah. He said: Shall I not inform you of a hadith of your hadith, O company of Ansar? Then he mentioned the conquest of Makkah and said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came when he arrived at Makkah, and sent al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam over one flank, and Khalid ibn al-Walid over the other flank, and Abu Ubaydah over the unarmored; they took the belly of the valley while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in a squadron. He looked and saw me and said: O Abu Hurayrah. I said: O Prophet of Allah. He said: Call the Ansar for me; none comes to me except an Ansari. He called them until they surrounded him, while Quraysh had gathered their rabble and followers and said: These advance; if they have something we are with them, and if they are hit we are given what we asked. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar, may Allah be pleased with them, when they surrounded him: Look at Quraysh's rabble and followers. Then he struck one hand over the other: Mow them mowing until you meet me at al-Safa. They went; none of us wished to kill whom he wished except he killed, and none of them faced us. Abu Sufyan said: O Messenger of Allah, the green of Quraysh is permitted, and no Quraysh after today. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever locks his door is secure, and whoever enters Abu Sufyan's house is secure. People locked their doors. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came until he reached the Stone, touched it, then circumambulated the House. He came upon an idol beside the House they worship, in whose hand is a bow, holding the bowstring. When he came upon the idol he kept stabbing its eyes and saying: {Truth has come and falsehood vanished; falsehood is ever vanishing}. Until when he finished his circumambulation he came to al-Safa, climbed it until he saw the House, raised his hands and praised Allah and supplicated as Allah willed, the Ansar, may Allah be pleased with them, beneath him. The Ansar said to one another: As for the man, desire for his kin and pity for his clan has seized him. Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Revelation came to him with it, and when it came it was not hidden from us; none raises his head to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until revelation ends. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O company of Ansar, did you say: As for the man, desire for his kin seized him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O company of Ansar, did you say: As for the man, desire for his kin seized him and pity for his clan? They said: Had it been mentioned. He said: Nay, I am Allah's servant and Messenger; I migrated to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and to you; life is your life, death is your death. They came weeping to him saying: By Allah we said what we said only jealously for Allah and His Messenger. He said: Allah and His Messenger believe you and excuse you. This Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports Quraysh at the Messenger of Allah's entry (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) into Makkah gathered their rabble and followers and said: These advance; if they have something we are with them, if they are hit we are given what we asked. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood upon that from them and said to the Ansar: Look at Quraysh's rabble and followers. Then he said with one hand over the other: Mow them mowing until you meet me at al-Safa. None of us wished to kill whom he wished except he killed, and none of them faced us. So is this an entry upon security? Then was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after that favor upon them and pardon. There was narrated from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in this hadith an addition over what is in Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah's hadith."
"5110","5454 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Sallam ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Thabit al-Bunani narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Rabah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - when he marched to Makkah to conquer it - sent Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam and Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with them. When he sent them the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: Call the Ansar. He called: O company of Ansar, answer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They came as usual. Then he said: Take this road, and none shall overlook except you kill him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) marched and Allah opened upon them, of killing that day four. He said: Then the chiefs of Quraysh of the polytheists entered the Kabah thinking the sword will not be lifted from them. Then he circumambulated and prayed two rakahs, then came to the Kabah and took its doorposts and said: What do you say and what do you think? They said: We say: A brother and son of uncle, forbearing, merciful. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I say as Yusuf said: {No blame upon you today; Allah forgives you and He is Most Merciful of merciful}. He said: They went out as if spread from graves and entered Islam. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out from the door toward al-Safa, خطب while the Ansar below him. The Ansar said to one another: The man is seized by pity for his people and desire for his kin. Revelation came down upon him, and he said: O company of Ansar, did you say: Pity for his people seized him and desire for his kin; what prophet am I then? Nay by Allah I am Allah's Messenger truly; life is your life, death is your death. They said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we said only fearing you leave us, miserly with you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are truthful with Allah and His Messenger. By Allah none of them remained except he bowed his neck with tears. Do you not see that Quraysh after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah thought the sword will not be lifted from them. Do you think they feared that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he had secured them before? This by Allah is not feared from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); rather they knew to him is killing them if he wills, and to him favoring them if he wills, and that Allah had made him prevail over them."
"5111","5455 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Harith ibn al-Barsa, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Makkah's conquest say: Makkah will not be raided after this day ever. Abu Sufyan said: The interpretation of this hadith; because they will never disbelieve, so they will not be raided for disbelief; this is only with his entering it an entry of raiding. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No Qurashi will be killed after this day in custody."
"5112","5456 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Abdullah ibn Muti said: I heard Muti say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Makkah's conquest say: No Qurashi will be killed in custody after this day to the Day of Resurrection. He said: That indicates Quraysh's blood was only forbidden after that day for what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his forbidding it upon them that day. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) خطب that day a sermon clarifying Makkah's ruling before his entering it, its ruling at his entering, and its ruling after that."
"5113","5457 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah made Makkah sacred the day Allah created the heavens and earth, the sun and moon, and placed it between these two mountains; then it was not lawful for anyone before me, and was lawful for me only an hour of daytime; its grass is not cut, its trees not lopped, its game not startled, its lost property not lifted except by its announcer. Al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: (Except idhkhir)."
"5114","5458 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhib, he said: Said al-Maqburi narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Shurayh al-Kabi say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah made Makkah sacred and people did not make it sacred; whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not shed blood in it, nor lop trees in it. If one permits himself saying: It was made lawful for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then Allah made it lawful for me and did not make it lawful for people; it was made lawful for me only an hour."
"5115","5459 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi narrated to me, from Abu Shurayh al-Khuzai, he said: When Amr ibn Said sent the expedition to Makkah to fight Ibn al-Zubayr, Abu Shurayh al-Khuzai came to him and spoke to him with what he heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he went out to his people's club and sat; I went to him and sat with him, and he narrated of what he narrated to Amr ibn Said, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and of what Amr ibn Said answered him. He said: I said to him: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he conquered Makkah; the morrow of conquest day, Khuzaah aggressed against a man of Hudhayl and killed him in Makkah while he was a polytheist. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood خطب among us and said: O people, Allah made Makkah sacred the day He created heavens and earth; it is sacred to the Day of Resurrection. It is not lawful for one believing in Allah and the Last Day to shed blood in it, nor lop trees in it. It was not lawful for anyone before me, nor lawful for anyone after me, and was lawful for me only this hour in anger; then it returned as sacred. If one says to you: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made it lawful, say: Allah made it lawful for His Messenger and did not make it lawful for you. O company of Khuzaah, withhold your hands; you killed a slain I will pay blood-money for. Whoever is killed after this stand of mine is with the better of two views: if he likes, the blood of his killer; if he likes, his blood-money. He said: Leave, O shaykh; we know its sanctity better than you; it does not prevent a bloodshedder, nor one violating sanctity, nor one casting off obedience. I said: I was witness and you were absent; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered our witness to convey to our absent; I have conveyed to you."
"5116","5460 - Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Said al-Maqburi, that he said: I heard Abu Shurayh al-Khuzai say to Amr ibn Said while he was on the pulpit when he cut an expedition to Makkah to fight Ibn al-Zubayr: O this one, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Makkah is sacred; Allah made it sacred and people did not make it sacred. Allah only made fighting in it lawful for me an hour of daytime, and perhaps after me are men who deem fighting in it lawful; whoever of them does that, say: Allah made it lawful for His Messenger and did not make it lawful for you, and let the witness convey to the absent. Were it not that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let the witness convey to the absent, I would not have narrated this hadith to you. Amr said: You are an old man who doted, and I intended you. He said: By Allah we shall speak truth even if you tighten our necks."
"5117","5461 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, from Shuayb ibn al-Layth, from his father, from Abu Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Shurayh al-Khuzai, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with like the meaning of Fahd's hadith before this hadith."
"5118","5462 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood over al-Hajun then said: By Allah you are the best of Allah's land and most beloved of Allah's land to Allah. It was not lawful for anyone before me, nor lawful for anyone after me, and was made lawful for me only an hour of daytime; after this hour of it it is forbidden to the Day of Resurrection."
"5119","5463 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal and Abu Salamah narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"5120","5464 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, he said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, he said: When Allah opened upon His Messenger Makkah, Hudhayl killed a man of Banu Layth for a slain of theirs in Jahiliyyah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and said: Allah withheld the Elephant from Makkah's people, and empowered over them His Messenger and the believers. It was not lawful for anyone before me, nor lawful for anyone after me; it was made lawful for me only an hour of daytime, and this hour of mine is forbidden: its trees not lopped, its thorns not cut, its fallen not picked except for its announcer."
"5121","5465 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. Except that he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, withheld the Elephant from Makkah's people, and said: Its stray is not picked except for an announcer. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reported in this sermon of his that Allah made Makkah lawful for him an hour of daytime, then it returned forbidden to the Day of Resurrection. Had he no need to fight in that hour, it would have been in that hour, and in what before and after it, upon one meaning, and its ruling in all those times one ruling. If one says: He was only permitted to display weapons in it, nothing else. It is said to him: What need has he to display weapons if he cannot fight anyone in it with them? This is impossible in our view; displaying weapons in it is not permissible unless fighting with them is permitted to him. Al-Layth ibn Sad clarified in his hadith we narrated from him in this section, from Abu Said al-Maqburi, this meaning, saying in it: Allah only made fighting in it lawful for me an hour of daytime. Is it permissible for him to make lawful for him fighting one he is in truce and security with? This is not permissible. Then his entering it (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was an entry of a warrior, not an entry of one secure; because he entered it with the helmet on his head."
"5122","5466 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah in the year of conquest with the helmet on his head. When he removed it a man came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, this Ibn Khatal is clinging to the curtains of the Kabah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Kill him. Malik said: Ibn Shihab said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not that day in ihram."
"5123","5467 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, and did not say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not that day in ihram. And it was said: He entered it with a black turban upon him."
"5124","5468 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Ammar al-Duhni, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered on the day of Makkah's conquest with a black turban upon him."
"5125","5469 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Ammar al-Duhni, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5126","5470 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered with a black turban upon him."
"5127","5471 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hakim al-Awdi narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Ammar al-Duhni, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: Had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at his entering it been not a warrior, he would not have entered it. This Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, one who narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Allah's making Makkah lawful for him as we narrated from him in this section, forbade people to enter the Haram without ihram."
"5128","5472 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad, from Qays, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: None enters Makkah except in ihram."
"5129","5473 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Haytham ibn al-Jahm narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ata said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: (There is no umrah upon the Makkan except that he goes out of the Haram, then does not enter it except in ihram). It was said to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: If a man goes out of Makkah nearby? He said: Yes, he fulfills his need and makes with its fulfillment an umrah."
"5130","5474 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik informed us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to say: (No trader nor need-seeker enters Makkah except in ihram). That what we mentioned indicates that Allah's making it lawful for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was only for his need to fight from it, not for other than that."
"5131","5475 - If one says: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had secured all people except six persons, and he mentioned on that what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Suddi claimed, from Musab ibn Sad, from his father, he said: On the day of Makkah's conquest the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) secured people except four men and two women, and said: Kill them even if you find them clinging to the curtains of the Kabah: Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl, Abdullah ibn Khatal, Miqyas ibn Dubabah and Abdullah ibn Sad ibn Abi Sarh. As for Abdullah ibn Khatal, he was brought while clinging to the curtains of the Kabah; Said ibn Hurayth and Ammar ibn Yasir raced to him; Said preceded Ammar, being the stronger of the two men, and killed him. As for Miqyas ibn Dubabah, people caught him in the market and killed him. As for Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl, he rode the sea and a violent wind befell them; the ship's people said to the ship's folk: Be sincere, for your gods avail you nothing here. Ikrimah said: By Allah if nothing saves me at sea except sincerity, nothing saves me on land but it. O Allah, upon You is a covenant from me: if You save me from what I am in, I shall come to Muhammad, then place my hand in his hand; I shall find him pardoning, generous. So he became Muslim. As for Abdullah ibn Abi Sarh, he hid with Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called people to allegiance, he brought him until he stood him over the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, take allegiance from Abdullah. He raised his head and looked at him three times, each refusing; he took his allegiance after three. Then he turned to his companions and said: Was there not among you a right-minded man who stands to this one when he saw me withhold my hand from his allegiance and kills him. They said: We did not know, O Messenger of Allah, what was in yourself; why did you not gesture to us with your eye. He said: It is not befitting a prophet to have treacherous eyes."
"5132","5476 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. It was said to him: This was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after Allah made him prevail over them. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he first made peace, these six persons had entered that truce of his, and their blood became lawful after that by causes occurring from them after the truce. Likewise Abu Sufyan was also in the truce. Then Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, brought him to him: (O Messenger of Allah, this is Abu Sufyan; Allah has enabled him without contract nor pact. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not deny that upon him until al-Abbas after that granted him protection by sparing his blood for his protection). Likewise Hubayrah ibn Abi Wahb al-Makhzumi and his two cousins who after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah joined Umm Hani daughter of Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with her, and Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, wanted to kill them. They had entered the first truce, then their blood became lawful after that by the causes from them until Umm Hani, may Allah be pleased with her, granted them protection, so their blood became forbidden by that. Likewise whoever did not enter Abu Sufyan's house on the day of Makkah's conquest, nor locked his door upon himself, had entered the first truce without stipulation upon him in it to enter Abu Sufyan's house, nor to lock his own door upon himself; then his blood became lawful after the first truce by the causes from him after that."
"5133","5477 - It is indicated by what Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Mansur al-Tusi narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Shubah narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Safar, from al-Sha'bi, from Abdullah ibn Muti ibn al-Aswad, from his father - whose name was al-As and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named him Muti - he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - when he ordered killing these men in Makkah - say: Makkah will not be raided after today ever, nor will a man of Quraysh be killed in custody after the year. This indicates that its raiding that year was contrary to after it of years, and in that what indicated that there was no security for its people that year; because one in security is not raided. And his saying: (No man of Quraysh will be killed in custody after that year) for that. In what we narrated and mentioned of reports and disclosed of proofs is what establishes proof in disclosing what we differed in, and clarifying Makkah's conquest that it is by force. And Allah is success."
"5134","And there was narrated on Makkah's matter what prevents it being a truce: 5478 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us. (H). 5479 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Urwah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, from his father, he said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) manifested Islam and the people of Makkah became Muslim, before prayer was obligated, until he would recite the prostration verse and prostrate and they prostrate; some could not prostrate from crowding and narrow place for many people, until the chiefs of Quraysh came: al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah, Abu Jahl and others; they were at Taif in their lands, and said: Do you leave your fathers' religion? So they disbelieved. Abu Jafar, may Allah have mercy on him, said: In this hadith is that the Islam of Makkah's people had preceded, and that they disbelieved after that. How is it permissible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) secures apostate people after power over them? This is not permissible for him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Muslims all agreed that the apostate is separated from food except what sustains himself, and separated from spacious living and disposal in Allah's land until he returns to Allah's religion, or refuses that and Allah's ruling proceeds upon him, and that had he asked the imam to secure him to reside apostate secure in the abode of Islam, the imam does not answer him to that nor give him what he asked. In establishment of what we mentioned of Muslims' consensus on what we described is sound proof and decisive argument that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not secure Makkah's people after power over them and prevailing over them. Allah knows best the right."
"5135","In the name of Allah, Most Gracious, Most Merciful. 16 - Book of Sales. 1 - Chapter: Sale of barley for wheat with excess. 5480 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala al-Sadafi narrated to me, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Abu al-Nadr narrated to him that Busr ibn Said narrated to him, from Mamar ibn Abdullah that he sent a slave of his with a sa of wheat (it is al-hintah) and said to him: Sell it then buy barley with it. The slave went and took a sa and increase of part of a sa. When Mamar came he informed him, and Mamar said to him: Why did you do? Go, return it, and take only like for like, for I used to hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Food for food, like for like. Our food that day was barley. It was said to him: It is not like it. He said: I fear it resembles it (that it is like it). Abu Jafar said: People went to this hadith and followed it, and said: Sale of wheat for barley is not permissible except like for like. Others opposed them in that and said: No harm in selling wheat for barley with excess, two likes for a like or more. Of the proof for them against the first party in the hadith they argued with against them is that Mamar reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he heard him say: Food for food, like for like. Then Mamar said: Our food that day was barley. It is possible the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant by his saying Mamar related from him the food that was their food that day, so that is upon barley for barley; there is nothing in this hadith of mention of sale of wheat for barley of what was mentioned in it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); rather it is mentioned from Mamar, of his opinion, and of his interpreting what he heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Do you not see that it was said to him: It is not like it, i.e., not of its kind, and he did not deny that to one who said it; his answer to him was (I fear it resembles it), as if he feared the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he heard him say, which is what we mentioned in his hadith, is upon all foods, so he avoided that and kept clear of it, for the doubt in his heart from it. When it was negated that this hadith has proof for either party against its fellow, we looked whether in other than it is what indicates to us the ruling of that how it is."
"5136","5481 - We considered that, and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah informed us, from Qatadah, from Muslim ibn Yasar, from Abu al-Ashath, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit that he stood and said: (O people, you have innovated sales, I do not know what they are? Gold for gold, weight for weight, its ingot and coined, silver for silver, weight for weight, its ingot and coined; no harm in selling gold for silver, silver the more of them, hand to hand; deferred is not sound. Wheat for wheat, mudd for mudd, hand to hand; barley for barley, mudd for mudd, hand to hand; no harm in selling barley for wheat, barley the more of them, hand to hand; deferred is not valid. Dates for dates, until he counted salt, like for like; whoever increases or seeks increase has taken usury). Abu Jafar said: This Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, opposed Mamar ibn Abdullah in what he held, on what we mentioned from him in the first hadith. This speech was narrated from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5137","5482 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi narrated to us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Muslim ibn Yasar and another man, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell gold for gold, nor silver for silver, nor wheat for wheat, nor barley for barley, nor dates for dates, nor salt for salt except equal for equal, like for like, hand to hand; but sell gold for silver, silver for gold, wheat for barley, barley for wheat, dates for salt, salt for dates, hand to hand, however you wish. He said: One of them omitted: dates for salt, and the other added (whoever increases or is increased has taken usury)."
"5138","5483 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Mualla ibn Asad informed us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ayyub, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"5139","5484 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, from his father, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Yusuf, from Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from Ayyub ibn Abi Tamimah, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Ibn Yasar, from Abu al-Ashath, he said: I heard Ubadah ibn al-Samit say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade - or the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not trade gold for gold, nor silver for silver except weight for weight, nor dates for dates, nor wheat for wheat, nor barley for barley, nor salt for salt except equal for equal, like for like; whoever increases or is increased has taken usury. But sell gold for silver, wheat for barley, dates for salt, hand to hand, however you wish."
"5140","5485 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Khalil, from Muslim al-Makki, from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade gold to be sold for gold, its ingot and coined, except weight for weight; silver for silver, its ingot and coined, except like for like. He mentioned barley for barley, dates for dates, salt for salt measure for measure; whoever increases or is increased has taken usury. No harm in selling barley for wheat, hand to hand, barley the more of them."
"5141","5486 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ashath, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5142","5487 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Muslim ibn Yasar, and he mentioned another who narrated to them, or they narrated: The house gathered Ubadah ibn al-Samit and Muawiyah together, in a church or synagogue. Ubadah narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell gold for gold, nor silver for silver, nor wheat for wheat, nor barley for barley, nor dates for dates, nor salt for salt, except equal for equal, like for like. One of them said, the other did not. Ubadah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us to sell gold for silver, wheat for barley, barley for wheat, hand to hand, however we wish. Abu Jafar said: In these reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is permission to sell barley for wheat two likes for a like. The saying is established by reports. Then we sought its ruling from wheat how much it is? Some said: It is half a sa for each needy; some said: It is a mudd for each needy. Those who made it from wheat half a sa made it from barley a sa; and those who made it from wheat a mudd made it from barley two mudds. We mentioned that with its isnads from them elsewhere. That establishes they are two different kinds; had they been one kind, what suffices from one would suffice from the other. If one says: Barley was increased over what was made from wheat, for wheat's dearness and barley's abundance. The answer: We saw what is given of good wheat and bad in oath expiation equal, likewise barley. Do you not see one obligated to expiate an oath who gives each needy half a mudd equaling half a sa, that does not suffice for half a sa nor a mudd. Since that is so, and barley is rendered in oath expiations double what is rendered of wheat, that establishes it is a kind other than wheat. That establishes no harm selling it for wheat two likes for a like and more."
"5143","2 - Chapter: Sale of fresh dates for dry dates. 5488 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik and Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, freedman of al-Aswad ibn Sufyan: Zayd Abu Ayyash informed him that he asked Sad about sult for white wheat. Sad said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked about fresh dates for dry dates. He said: Does fresh decrease when it dries? They said: Yes. He said: Then no, and he disliked it."
"5144","5489 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Zayd Abu Ayyash, from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, and he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: People went to this hadith, followed it and made it a basis, and forbade by it sale of fresh dates for dry dates. Among them: Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah's mercy be upon them. Others opposed them, making fresh and dry one kind, permitting sale of each for its fellow like for like, disliking it deferred. We considered this hadith their opponent argued with against them: did anything enter it?"
"5145","5490 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abdullah ibn Yazid that Zayd Abu Ayyash informed him, from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sale of fresh dates for dry dates deferred. This was the basis of the hadith with mention of deferment; Yahya ibn Abi Kathir added it over Malik ibn Anas, so he is worthier. Others besides Abdullah ibn Yazid also narrated this hadith like what Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated."
"5146","5491 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed us, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah, he narrated from Imran ibn Abi Anas that a freedman of Banu Makhzum narrated to him that Sad ibn Abi Waqqas was asked about a man lending a man fresh dates for dry dates to a term? Sad said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us from this. This Imran ibn Abi Anas, an early known man, narrated this hadith as Yahya narrated. In correcting meanings of reports, Abdullah ibn Yazid's hadith - since differed from him in it - should be lifted, and this hadith of Imran established. So this prohibition in this hadith of Sad is only for the cause of deferment, not other. This is this chapter's path, by correcting reports. As for its aspect by reasoning: We saw them not differ on sale of fresh for fresh like for like, that it is permissible; likewise dry for dry like for like, though in one is moisture not in the other, all decreases when kept, differing decrease, and dries. They did not look to that at drying time to nullify sale, but looked to its state at sale time and acted on that, not observing what it turns to after of drying and decrease. By reasoning it should likewise be fresh for dry: looked to at sale time, not to what it turns to of change and drying. This is Abu Hanifah's saying, may Allah be pleased with him, and it is reasoning in our view."
"5147","3 - Chapter: Meeting caravans. 5492 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, he said: Simak informed me, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not meet the market, and let none of you sell for another."
"5148","5493 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, he said: Simak narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not meet the market."
"5149","5494 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Numayr informed us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade goods to be met until they enter markets."
"5150","5495 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah informed us, he said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"5151","5496 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad informed us, he said: Sakhr ibn Juwayriyah informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not meet sales."
"5152","5497 - Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh informed us, from Uqayl, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade goods to be met until they are brought down, i.e., taken to markets."
"5153","5498 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Asad informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Muslim al-Khayyat, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade riders to be met."
"5154","5499 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Salih ibn Dinar, from his father, from Abu Said that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not meet anything of sale until it comes to your market."
"5155","5500 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Adi ibn Thabit, he said: I heard Abu Hazim narrating from Abu Hurayrah, he said: We were forbidden, or forbade meeting."
"5156","5501 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not meet riders."
"5157","5502 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abi Layla, from a man of the Prophet's companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not meet produce. Abu Jafar said: People argued with these reports and said: Whoever meets something before its market entry then buys it, his purchase is void. Others opposed them and said: Every city where meeting harms its people, meeting in it is disliked, purchase valid; every city where meeting does not harm its people, no harm meeting in it."
"5158","5503 - They argued on that with what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah informed us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: We used to meet riders and buy food from them in bulk; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us to sell it until we move it from its place, or transport it."
"5159","5504 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ghalib narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that they used to buy food from riders in the Messenger of Allah's time (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he sends over them one who prevents them selling it where they bought it, until they convey it to where food is sold. In these reports is permission of meeting, and in the first, prohibition of it. Worthier for us to make that without contradiction. What was forbidden of meeting is for the harm in that to other than the meeters residing in markets. What was permitted of meeting is that with no harm to market residents. This is these reports' aspect - in our view - and Allah knows best."
"5160","5505 - They argued on permitting purchase with forbidden meeting with what Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not meet produce; whoever meets it and buys something from it is with option when it comes to market."
"5161","5506 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not meet produce, and a townsman shall not sell for a Bedouin, and the seller has the option when he enters the market. In this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he forbade meeting produce, then made for the seller in that the option, when he enters the market. Option is only in a valid sale; had it been void, its seller and buyer would be forced to rescind it, and neither would have refusal of that. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made option in that for the seller, its validity is established, though with forbidden meeting. If one says: You do not make option for the met seller, as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made for him in this hadith. Our answer, with Allah's success, is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is established from him that he said: The two sellers have the option, so long as they have not separated. Reports are successive from him with that, and we shall mention them in their place in this book, Allah willing. So we knew thereby that when they separate, they have no option. If one says: You made for one who buys what he has not seen the option of seeing, until he sees it and approves, so why deny that the option of meeting is likewise? It is said to him: The option of seeing we did not obligate by analogy; rather we found the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affirmed it, judged with it and agreed upon it, and did not differ in it. Difference in that only came from those after them. So we made that outside the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The two sellers have the option until they separate. We knew the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not mean that, by their consensus on its exit from it, as we knew by their consensus on permitting salam, that it is outside the Prophet's prohibition (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of selling what is not with you."
"5162","5507 - If one says: Did you narrate from the Prophet's Companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the option of seeing anything? It is said to him: Yes. Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Muhammad ibn Shadhan narrated to us, they said: Hilal ibn Yahya ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Rabah ibn Abi Maruf al-Makki, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Alqamah ibn Waqqas al-Laythi, he said: Talhah ibn Ubaydullah bought property from Uthman ibn Affan. It was said to Uthman: You have been cheated - the property was at Kufah, now the property of Talhah's family there - Uthman said: I have the option, for I sold what I did not see. Talhah said: To me is the option, for I bought what I did not see. They arbitrated between them Jubayr ibn Mutim, and he judged the option is for Talhah, no option for Uthman. Reports on that came successive, though most are interrupted; it is interrupted, not opposed by connected. In this also is another proof: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made in Abu Hurayrah's hadith for the met seller the option, in what he sold when he enters markets, and learned prices. We wanted to look: did anything oppose that or not? We considered that."
"5163","5508 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Hafs al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas, he said: We were forbidden that a townsman sell for a Bedouin, even if he is his father or brother."
"5164","5509 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Muhammad, from Anas, he said: We were forbidden that a townsman sell for a Bedouin."
"5165","5510 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Muslim al-Khayyat, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A townsman shall not sell for a Bedouin."
"5166","5511 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Sakhr ibn Juwayriyah informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5167","5512 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, and added (nor buy for him)."
"5168","5513 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Salih ibn Dinar, from his father, from Abu Said al-Khudri, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: A townsman shall not sell for a Bedouin."
"5169","5514 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us. (H). 5515 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5170","5516 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5171","5517 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: I heard al-Numan ibn Rashid narrating from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5172","5518 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salih ibn Nabhan, freedman of al-Tawamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5173","5519 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Adi ibn Thabit, he said: I heard Abu Hazim narrating from Abu Hurayrah, he said: He forbade, or it was forbidden, that the Muhajir sell for the Bedouin."
"5174","5520 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abi Layla, from a man of the Prophet's Companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he forbade the townsman to sell for the Bedouin."
"5175","5521 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salih, freedman of al-Tawamah, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a townsman to buy for a Bedouin."
"5176","We looked into the cause for which he forbade (the townsman to sell) for the Bedouin, what it is? And 5522 Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, he said: I heard Jabir say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A townsman shall not sell for a Bedouin; leave people, Allah provides some of them from some."
"5177","5523 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ata, from Hakim ibn Abi Yazid, that he came to him for a need. He said: He narrated to me from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave people, let some of them obtain from some; when one of you seeks counsel from his brother, let him counsel him. So we knew thereby that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade the townsman to sell for the Bedouin because the townsman knows market prices and exacts upon townsmen, so they have no profit in that. When the Bedouin sells them in his ignorance of market prices, townsmen profit over him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered to leave townsmen and Bedouins in sales, and prevented townsmen entering upon them in that. Since that is so, and permission of meeting with no harm is established by the reports we mentioned, the met one's purchase from them becomes a townsman's purchase from a Bedouin; it enters the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Leave people, Allah provides some from some. It is void there is option for the seller in that; had he option in it, the buyer would have no profit in that, nor would the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) order a townsman to intercept him, nor to undertake sale for the Bedouin from him, for he would have option to rescind that sale, or return to him its price to the prices in sales of townsmen among themselves. In the Prophet's preventing townsmen from that (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is permission for townsmen to seek the Bedouins' ignorance in sale from them, and purchase from them. This is Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad's saying, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"5178","4 - Chapter: Option of the two sellers until they separate. 5524 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us. (H). 5525 - Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us. (H). 5526 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal informed us, he said: Sufyan informed us. (H). 5527 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad informed us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us. They all said, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every two sellers, there is no sale between them until they separate, or it is a sale of option."
"5179","5528 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two sellers have the option, so long as they have not separated. He said: (Or one of them says to his fellow: Choose). Perhaps he said: (Or it is a sale of option)."
"5180","5529 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every two sellers have the option, so long as they have not separated, or it is a sale of option."
"5181","5530 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Khalil, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Hakim ibn Hizam, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The two sellers have the option until they separate, or so long as they have not separated; if they are truthful and clarify, their sale is blessed for them; if they lie and conceal, the blessing of their sale is effaced."
"5182","5531 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from Abu al-Wadi, from Abu Barzah, that they disputed to him about (a man who sold a slave girl, and the seller slept with her; in the morning he said: I do not approve her). Abu Barzah said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two sellers have the option so long as they have not separated, and they were in a hair tent."
"5183","5532 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb informed us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Jamil ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Wadi, he said: We camped a camp; a companion of ours sold a horse to a man, and we stayed in our camp (our day and night). The morrow, the man stood to saddle his horse; his fellow said to him: You sold me. They disputed to Abu Barzah. He said: If you wish I judge between you by the Messenger of Allah's judgment (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The two sellers have the option so long as they have not separated. I do not see you separated."
"5184","5533 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammam informed us, from Qatadah, from Salih (Abu al-Khalil), from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Hakim ibn Hizam, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two sellers have the option until they separate, or so long as they have not separated; if they are truthful and clarify, their sale is blessed for them; if they lie and conceal, perhaps a separation turns between them, and the blessing of their sale is effaced. 5534 - Hammam said: I heard Abu al-Tayyah say: I heard this hadith from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Hakim ibn Hizam, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the like of this."
"5185","5535 - Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr, Hashim ibn al-Qasim, narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Utbah informed us, from Abu Kathir al-Ghubari, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The two sellers have the option so long as they have not separated, or it is a sale of option."
"5186","5536 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan narrated to us, from Samurah ibn Jundub, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two sellers have the option, so long as they have not separated, and each takes what he approves of the sale. Abu Jafar said: People differed in interpreting the Messenger of Allah's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The two sellers have the option so long as they have not separated. Some said: This is upon separation by words: when the seller says (I sold to you), the buyer says (I accepted), they have separated and their option is cut. They said: What they had of option is what the seller had to nullify his saying to the buyer: (I sold you this slave for a thousand dirhams) before the buyer's acceptance. When the buyer accepts, he and the seller have separated, and option is cut. They said: This is as Allah mentioned on divorce: {If they separate, Allah enriches each from His bounty}. The husband when he says to the woman (I divorced you for such and such), and the woman says (I accepted), she is separated, and they separate by that speech, though not separated by bodies. Likewise when a man says to a man: (I sold you this slave of mine for a thousand dirhams), and the buyer says (I accepted), they have separated by that speech, though not separated by bodies. Among those who said this and interpreted this interpretation, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah's mercy be upon him. Isa ibn Aban said: The separation that cuts the mentioned option in these reports is separation by bodies: when a man says to a man: (I sold you this slave of mine for a thousand dirhams), the addressed has to accept, so long as he has not parted his fellow; when they part, he has after that no acceptance. He said: Were it not this hadith came, we would not know what cuts what the addressed has of accepting the address his fellow addressed him with, obligating him sale. When this hadith came, we knew that parting of their bodies after address cuts the addressee's acceptance of that address and sale he obligated for him. When this hadith came, we knew that the parting of their bodies after address cuts acceptance, until the addressed returns to his fellow after parting and the sale is renewed between them. A sale. This is a sound counter. As for what they mentioned from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, of his act they inferred the Messenger of Allah's intent (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on separation: that bears - in our view - what they said, and bears other. It is possible Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, found ambiguous that separation he heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what it is? It bore - with him - separation by bodies, upon what this party's people mentioned. It bore - with him - separation by bodies upon what this party's people mentioned which Isa held. It bore - with him - separation by words, upon what others held. No proof attended him indicating one is worthier than others; so he parted his seller by body, by precaution. It is also possible he did that because some people hold sale is completed only by that, while he holds sale is completed without it. He wanted sale completed in his saying and his opponent's saying, so his seller has no rescinding sale upon him, in his saying, nor in his opponent's saying. There was narrated from him what indicates his opinion on separation was contrary to what those held who held sale is completed by it."
"5187","5537 - That is that Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to me, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Hamzah ibn Abdullah, that Abdullah ibn Umar said: Whatever the bargain reaches alive is from the buyer's wealth."
"5188","5538 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. Abu Jafar said: This Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, held on what the bargain reaches alive, if it perishes after it, that it is from the buyer's wealth. That indicates he held sale is completed by words before the separation after that, and sale transfers by those words from the seller's ownership to the buyer's ownership, until it perishes from his wealth if it perishes. What we mentioned is more indicative of Ibn Umar's way, may Allah be pleased with them both, on the separation he heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), than what they mentioned. As for what they mentioned from Abu Barzah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), there is no proof for them in it either - in our view - because that hadith is only in what Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, from Jamil ibn Murrah, that a man sold his fellow a horse, and they spent night in a camp; in the morning the man stood to saddle his horse and said to him: (You sold me). Abu Barzah said: (If you wish I judge between you by the Messenger of Allah's judgment (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two sellers have the option until they separate. I do not see you separated). In this hadith is what indicates they had separated by bodies, because in it the man stood to saddle his horse, so he moved thereby from place to place. Abu Barzah did not observe that, and said: (I do not see you separated), i.e., since you are disputing, one claiming sale, the other denying it, you have not separated the separation by which sale is completed, contrary to what you separated by bodies. Then after this, we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates the sold is owned by the buyer by speech without parting by bodies. That is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he takes possession of it. That was proof that when he takes possession, selling it is lawful for him."
"5189","5539 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed me. (H). 5540 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to me, from Musa ibn Ward, that Said ibn al-Musayyab said: I heard Uthman ibn Affan خطب on the pulpit saying (: I used to buy dates and sell them with profit of measures. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: When you buy, measure; when you sell, measure). Whoever buys food by measure and sells it before measuring it, his sale is not permissible; when he buys it, measures and takes possession, then parts his seller, all agreed he needs after separation no re-measuring. Measuring it after sale before parting was opposed to measuring it before sale. That indicates that when he measures it a measuring that makes selling it lawful for him, that measuring was from him while owner of it. When he measures it a measuring that does not make selling it lawful, he measured while not owner of it. That establishes, by what we mentioned, the buyer's ownership in sale by his buying it, before a parting after that. This is this chapter's aspect, by reports. As for by reasoning: We saw wealth owned by contracts, in bodies, wealth, benefits and private parts. What is owned of private parts is marriage; it is completed by contract, not by parting after it. What benefits are owned by is leases; it is owned by contract, not by parting after contract. By reasoning it should likewise be owned wealth, by all contracts, of sales and others, owned by words, not by parting after them, by analogy and reasoning on what we mentioned. This is Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad's saying, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"5190","5 - Chapter: Sale of musarrah. 5541 - Abu Bakrah, Bakkar ibn Qutaybah, narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin and Khilas ibn Amr, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever buys a musarrah ewe or musarrah milch camel and milks it is with the better of two views: between choosing it, and returning it, and a vessel of food."
"5191","5542 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: I heard Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say:"
"5192","5543 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad, i.e., Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever buys a musarrah has the option: if he wishes he returns it and a sa of dates. Thus in Muhammad ibn Ziyad's hadith. In Ayyub's hadith: and a sa of food, no samra (white wheat)."
"5193","5544 - Rabi al-Jizi and Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Maslamah narrated to us. (H). 5545 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi informed me. (H). 5546 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us. They said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from Musa ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys a musarrah ewe, let him turn with it, let him milk it; if he approves its milking he keeps it, else he returns it, and returns with it a sa of dates."
"5194","5547 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed me, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5195","5548 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sad and Ikrimah, from Abu Hurayrah that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys a musarrah ewe or musarrah milch camel, not knowing it is musarrah, if he wishes he returns it and with it a sa of dates, if he wishes he keeps it."
"5196","5549 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih informed us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah, that Abu Ishaq narrated to him, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever buys a musarrah ewe, let him turn with it, let him milk it; if he approves its milking he keeps it, else he returns it and returns with it a sa of dates. Abu Jafar said: These reports were narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as we mentioned, and no time was mentioned in them for the buyer's option. It was narrated from him that he made the option for him in that three days."
"5197","5550 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us with that, he said: Abdullah ibn Jafar al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he forbade sale of the ewe while muhaffalah; when he sells it, its owner has the option three days; if he dislikes it, he returns it and returns with it a sa of dates."
"5198","5551 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me that Suhayl ibn Abi Salih informed him, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys a musarrah ewe is with option in it three days; if he wishes he keeps it, if he wishes he returns it, and returns with it a sa of dates."
"5199","5552 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Asad informed us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, Hisham ibn Urwah and Habib, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Except that he said: He returns it and a sa of food, no samra. Abu Jafar said: People went to that the musarrah ewe when a man buys it and milks it, and does not approve its milking, between him and three days, he has the option: if he wishes he keeps it, if he wishes he returns it, and returns with it a sa of dates. They argued with these reports. Among them Ibn Abi Layla, except that he said: He returns it and returns with it the value of a sa of dates. Abu Yusuf also said this saying in some of his dictations, though not well-known from him. All that was opposed by others, who said: The buyer has no returning it by defect; rather he reverts upon the seller for the defect's deficiency. Among them Abu Hanifah and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah's mercy be upon them both. They held that what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that, of what we mentioned in this chapter, is abrogated. This speech was narrated from them in summary, then differed from them after on what abrogated that. Muhammad ibn Shuja said - in what Ibn Abi Imran informed me from him - His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) abrogated it: The two sellers have the option so long as they have not separated. We mentioned that with its isnads before in this book. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut option by separation, that establishes no option for anyone after it except one the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) excepted in this hadith by his saying: Except a sale of option. Abu Jafar said: This interpretation, in my view, is corrupt; because the option made in musarrah is an option of defect, and defect option is not cut by separation. Do you not see that a man had he bought a slave, taken possession, and separated, then saw a defect in him after that, he has returning him by defect. Who milks her after the three days has milked her after knowing her defect; that is approval from him of her. For this cause I mentioned, the described interpretation's corruption is obligatory. Isa ibn Aban said: What was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the ruling on musarrah, in the first reports, was at the time penalties in sins were taken by wealth. Of that what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on zakat that: Whoever pays it willingly has its reward; else we take it from him and half his wealth, a resolve of our Lord's resolves, Mighty and Majestic. Of that what was narrated from him in Amr ibn Shuayb's hadith on one stealing unguarded fruit: he is beaten lashes and fined double it. We mentioned that with its isnads in the chapter of a man's intercourse with his wife's slave girl; that sufficed from repeating it here. He said: Since the ruling in early Islam was so until Allah abrogated usury, taken things were singled to their likes, if they have likes, and to their value, if they have no likes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had forbidden tasriyah, and was narrated from him on that."
"5200","5553 - al-Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: al-Masudi narrated to us, from Jabir al-Jufi, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Abdullah, he said: I testify over the truthful, believed Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Sale of muhaffalah is deceit, and deceit of a Muslim is not lawful. Whoever does that and sells what he made sell it contrary to what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered and entering what he forbade, his punishment in that was to make the milk milked in the three days for the buyer with a sa of dates, though it may equal many sas. Then punishments in wealth for sins were abrogated, and things returned to what we mentioned. Since that is so, and returning the musarrah itself is obligatory, while milk has left it, we knew that milk the buyer took from it, some was in its udder at sale time, so it is in the sold's ruling; some occurred in its udder in the buyer's ownership after sale; that is for the buyer. Since returning milk in full to the seller is not possible, since some is with what he did not own its sale, and milk cannot all be made for the buyer if he owned some from the seller by his selling him the ewe he returned to him by defect, while his owning it to him was with part of the price the sale occurred with; it is not permissible he return the ewe with all price while that milk is safe for him without price. Since that is so, the buyer was prevented from returning it, and reverts upon his seller for its defect's deficiency. Isa said: (This is the aspect of musarrah sale's ruling). Abu Jafar said: What Isa said of this bears other than what he said. I saw in that an aspect likelier in my view to abrogate this hadith than that aspect Isa held. That is that the musarrah's milk the buyer milked from it, in the three days he milked it in, some was in the seller's ownership before purchase, and some occurred in the buyer's ownership after purchase; except that he milked it time after time. What was in the seller's hand of that was sold; when it necessitates nullifying sale in the ewe. And what occurred in the buyer's hand of that, he only owned it by the sale also; its ruling is the ewe's ruling, because it is from its body - this upon our way. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had made for the musarrah's buyer after returning her, all her milk he had milked from her, for the sa of dates he obligated upon him to return with the ewe. That milk then had perished, or some perished. The buyer had owned milk as debt, for a sa of dates as debt. That entered sale of debt for debt. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after that forbade sale of debt for debt."
"5201","5554 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Asim narrated to us. Abu Bakrah said in his hadith: Musa ibn Ubaydah informed us; Ibn Marzuq said in his hadith from Musa ibn Ubaydah al-Zaydi, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sale of debt for debt, meaning (debt for debt). That abrogated what preceded from him, of narrated on musarrah, whose ruling is debt's ruling. It is said to one who holds acting with narrated on musarrah, of what we mentioned at this chapter's beginning: It was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Revenue is by liability. Scholars acted with that."
"5202","5555 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhib. (H). 5556 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Makhlad ibn Khufaf, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Revenue is by liability."
"5203","5557 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: al-Zanji ibn Khalid narrated to us, I heard him say: Hisham ibn Urwah claimed to us, from his father, from Aishah, she said: A man bought a slave and used him, then saw a defect in him, and disputed him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he returned him by defect. He said: O Messenger of Allah, he used him. He said to him: Produce is by liability."
"5204","5558 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Mutarrif ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: al-Zanji ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5205","5559 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Salamah al-Majishun informed us, he said: Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. Scholars received this report with acceptance. You claimed that a man had he bought a ewe and milked it, then found in it a defect other than tahfil, he returns it and milk is his. Likewise had in milk's place a child it bore, he returns her upon the seller, and the child is his; that in your view is of the revenue the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made for the buyer by liability. The sa you obligate upon the musarrah's buyer, when he returns her upon the seller by tasriyah, is either compensation for all the milk he milked from her, some in her udder at sale time, and some occurred in her udder after sale, or compensation for the milk in her udder at sale time exclusively. If it is compensation for both, you have thereby broken your principle making the child and milk for the buyer after return by defect, because you made their ruling the ruling of revenue the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made for the buyer by liability. If that sa is compensation for what was in her udder at sale time exclusively, the rest safe for the buyer because it is of revenue, you have made for the seller a sa as debt for milk as debt; this is not permissible in your saying, nor in another's. On whichever of the two aspects this meaning is in your view, you thereby leave a principle of your principles. You were with saying this ruling in musarrah is abrogated worthier than another; because you make milk in revenue's ruling, and another does not make it so."
"5206","6 - Chapter: Sale of fruits before they ripen. 5560 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Zurah Wahbullah ibn Rashid narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, he said: Nafi narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid sale of fruit and its purchase, until its soundness appears."
"5207","5561 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us. (H). 5562 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me. They both said: from Ibn Shihab. (H). 5563 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Do not sell fruit until its soundness appears."
"5208","5564 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Do not sell fruit until its soundness appears."
"5209","5565 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja, i.e., al-Ghudani, informed us, he said: Shubah informed us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, and added: When he was asked about its soundness, he said: Until its blight goes."
"5210","5566 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed us, from Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Suraqah, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he forbade sale of fruits until blight goes. He said: I said: When is that, O Abu Abd al-Rahman? He said: Rising of Pleiades."
"5211","5567 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sale of fruit until its soundness appears."
"5212","5568 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Salim ibn Hayyan, he said: Said ibn Mina narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sale of fruits until they tanqih. It was said to Jabir: What is tanqih? He said: They redden and yellow, and are eaten from."
"5213","5569 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman and Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Maslamah ibn Qanab narrated to us, he said: Kharijah ibn Abdullah ibn Sulayman ibn Zayd ibn Thabit narrated to us, from Abu al-Rijal, from his mother Amrah, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sale of fruits until they escape blight."
"5214","5570 - Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Baghandi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sale of fruit until its soundness appears."
"5215","5571 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus ibn al-Qasim al-Yamami narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sale of muhaqalah, muzabanah, mukhadarah, mulamasah and munabadhah. Umar said: My father explained to me mukhadarah, he said: Nothing of palm fruit should be bought until it ripens, reddens or yellows."
"5216","5572 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad Abu Bakr al-Sayrafi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sale of fruit until it blossoms, grapes until they blacken, and grain until it hardens."
"5217","5573 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sale of palms until they blossom. I said to Anas: What is their blossoming? He said: They redden and yellow. Have you seen, if Allah withholds the fruit, with what does one of you deem his brother's wealth lawful?"
"5218","5574 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr informed us, he said: Humayd informed us, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sale of palm fruit until it blossoms. It was said to him: What is its blossoming? He said: It reddens or yellows."
"5219","5575 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not trade fruits until they blossom. We said: O Messenger of Allah, what is their blossoming? He said: They redden or yellow. Have you seen, if Allah withholds the fruit, with what does one of you deem his brother's wealth lawful?"
"5220","5576 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Said and Abu Salamah narrated to me, that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell fruit until its soundness appears. Abu Jafar said: People went to these reports and claimed fruits may not be sold on palm tops until they redden or yellow. Others opposed them and said: All these reports in our view are established, sound in coming; we take them, not leaving them. But their interpretation in our view is other than what the first party interpreted them upon. That is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sale of fruits until their soundness appears; that bears to be upon what the first party interpreted, and bears that he meant sale of fruits before they exist, so the seller sells what is not with him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade him that, in his forbidding sale of years."
"5221","5577 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Humayd al-Araj, from Sulayman ibn Atiq, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of years. Yunus said: Sufyan said to us: It is the sale of fruits before their soundness appears."
"5222","5578 - Rabi al-Jizi and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Kahmas ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah ibn Jundub, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of years."
"5223","5579 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata and Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of fruit until it becomes edible."
"5224","5580 - Muhammad Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5225","5581 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Abu al-Walid narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about the sale of date-palms, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of date-palms until we eat from them, or until they are eaten from."
"5226","5582 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Shubah informed us, from Amr ibn Murrah, he said: I heard Abu al-Bakhtari al-Tai saying: I asked Ibn Abbas about salam (advance payment), and I said: We leave things for which we find no prohibition in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic. He said: We do that; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of date-palms until they are eaten from."
"5227","5583 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Mufaddal ibn Faddalah narrated to me, from Khalid, that he heard Ata ibn Abi Rabah being asked about a man who sells the fruit of his land, whether fresh dates or grapes, advancing payment for it before it ripens. He said: It is not proper. Ibn al-Zubayr sold the fruit of land of his for three years, and Jabir ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari heard of that and went out to the mosque. He said among the people: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prevented us from selling fruit until it ripens."
"5228","5584 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: I asked Ibn Umar about advance payment for fruit, and he said: Umar forbade the sale of fruit until it becomes sound. These reports we have mentioned indicate what the fruits forbidden to be sold before their soundness appears are: they are those sold before they exist, as advance-payment goods. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that until they exist and are safe from blight; only then is salam (advance payment) for them permissible. Do you not see that when Abu al-Bakhtari asked Ibn Umar about salam for date-palms, his answer was what he mentioned in his hadith about forbidding the sale of fruits until they are eaten. That shows the prohibition in the reports presented at the beginning of this chapter concerns the sale of fruits before they become fruits. Do you not see the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Have you considered, if Allah withholds the fruit, for what does one of you take the wealth of his brother? That can only refer to withholding of a fruit which one had no right to come into being. What is in these reports is the prohibition of salam for fruits out of their season; these reports indicate the prohibition of that. As for the sale of fruits on their trees after they have appeared, that in our view is permissible and sound. The proof for that is what has come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5229","5585 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Salim ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Ibn Umar, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever sells date-palms after they have been pollinated, its fruit belongs to the one who sold it unless the buyer stipulates otherwise; and whoever sells a slave, his wealth belongs to the one who sold him unless the buyer stipulates otherwise."
"5230","5586 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to me, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever buys a slave and does not stipulate his wealth, he has nothing of it; and whoever buys date-palms after their pollination and does not stipulate the fruit, he has nothing of it."
"5231","5587 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun say: Hammad ibn Salamah informed me, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid al-Makhzumi, from Ibn Umar, that a man bought date-palms whose owner had already pollinated them, and he disputed with him before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled against him that the fruit belongs to its owner who pollinated it, unless the buyer stipulates otherwise. Abu Jafar said: In these reports the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned the fruit of the date-palms to their seller unless its buyer stipulates it, in which case it belongs to him by his stipulation, and by that he is a buyer of it. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has here permitted the sale of fruit on the tops of date-palms before its soundness appears. That indicates that the prohibited meaning in the first reports is other than this meaning. If one says: what was permitted is the sale of fruit in these reports because it is sold along with something else, and the permissibility of selling it with something else does not prove that selling it alone is likewise, for we have seen things that enter with something else in sales but may not be sold separately alone; among them are roads and courtyards, which enter into the sale of houses but may not be sold separately. Our answer to that, and with Allah is success, is that roads and courtyards enter into the sale even if not stipulated, while fruit does not enter into the sale of date-palms unless stipulated. So what enters into the sale of something else without stipulation is what may not be sold alone. And what does not enter into the sale of something else except by stipulation is what, when stipulated, is sold; so it could not be sold along with something else unless its sale alone is permissible. Do you not see that if a man sold a house containing furnishings, those furnishings do not enter into the sale, but if its buyer stipulated them in his purchase of the house, they become his by his stipulation. And if what was in the house were wine or swine and he stipulated it in the sale, the sale would be void. So nothing enters into his purchase of the house by his stipulation except what he may lawfully buy. And if he bought it alone, and the fruit we mentioned, its stipulation with the date-palms is permissible for him, that could only be because its sale alone is permissible. Do you not see that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in this hadith, joining it with his mention of date-palms: Whoever sells a slave who has wealth, his wealth belongs to the seller unless the buyer stipulates it. So he assigned the wealth to the seller when the buyer does not stipulate it, and assigned it to the buyer by his stipulation of it; and that wealth, had it been wine or swine, the sale of the slave would have been void if he stipulated it therein. Rather, one may only stipulate along with the slave, of his wealth, what may be sold alone; as for what may not be sold alone, its stipulation in his sale is not permissible, because by that it becomes sold, and the sale of that thing is not sound. That is also a sound proof for what we mentioned about the fruit that enters into the sale of date-palms by stipulation, that it is the fruits whose sale separately, apart from the sale of the date-palms, is permissible. By that, what we mentioned is established, and this is the saying of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on them both. Muhammad ibn al-Hasan held that the prohibition mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the beginning of this chapter is the sale of fruit on condition that it be left on the tops of the date-palms until it reaches maturity and ripeness, and until it is harvested, while the sale occurred before maturity, so the buyer has bought apparent fruit along with what the sellers date-palms will add to it after that until it is harvested; that is void. He said: as for when the sale occurs after its size has fully matured and its growth has ceased, there is no harm in buying it and stipulating leaving it until its harvest and cutting. He said: the prohibition of that occurred only because of stipulating leaving it due to the increase. He said: in that is proof that there is no harm in that stipulation in buying it after the increase has ceased. Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated this to me from his father, from Muhammad. And the interpretation of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf on this is better in our view, and Allah knows best. Reflection also supports it, for if the sale of the fruits occurs after their maturity, on condition they be left until harvest, then the date-palms here are hired out for the fruits to remain in them until the time of their cutting from them, and that, had it been separate, would not be permissible; so when it is with something else, it is likewise. Some people said: the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the sale of fruits until their soundness appears was not from him as a prohibition of that, but was as counsel to them for that, because of their frequent disputing before him about it, and they narrated that from Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5232","5588 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Abu Zuraah Wahb Allah informed us, from Yunus ibn Yazid, he said: Abu al-Zinad said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr used to narrate from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah al-Ansari, that he informed him that Zayd ibn Thabit used to say: People in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to trade in fruits. Then when people harvested and the time of their demanding payment came, the buyer would say: the fruit was struck by rot and mildew, it was struck by muraq, or it was struck by qusham, blights they would argue with; and qusham is something that strikes it so that it does not become ripe. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, when disputes about that became numerous before him: Do not trade until the soundness of the fruit appears, as counsel he counseled, because of their many disputes. What we mentioned shows that what we narrated at the beginning of this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his forbidding the sale of fruits until their soundness appears, was only in this sense, not otherwise."
"5233","Chapter 7: Al-Araya. 5589 - Ismail ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris informed us, from Sufyan, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of fruit for fruit."
"5234","5590 - Abd Allah said: And Zayd ibn Thabit narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave concession for al-araya."
"5235","5591 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us. (Ha). 5592 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he forbade muzabanah."
"5236","5593 - Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: And Zayd ibn Thabit informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave concession for al-araya."
"5237","5594 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave concession for al-araya."
"5238","5595 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us with this chain, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muhaqalah and muzabanah, and gave concession for al-araya."
"5239","5596 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit narrated to me, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave concession for the sale of al-araya, for dates, whether dry or fresh."
"5240","5597 - Ismail ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ismail al-Shaybani, he said: I sold what was on the tops of my date-palms for one hundred wasqs, and if there is more it is for them, and if there is less it is upon them. So I asked Ibn Umar about that and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of fruit for dates, except that he gave concession for al-araya."
"5241","5598 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata and Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of fruit until it becomes edible, and said: Nothing of it is to be sold except for dirhams and dinars, except al-araya, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave concession for them."
"5242","5599 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muzabanah, except that he gave concession for the sale of al-araya."
"5243","5600 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Zubayr and Said ibn Mina, from Jabir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muhaqalah, muzabanah and mukhabarah. One of them said: and muawamah, and the other said: and the sale of years; and he forbade thunya (exception) and gave concession for al-araya."
"5244","5601 - Ismail ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Yahya ibn Said, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of fruit for dates, except that he gave concession for the ariyyah to be sold for its estimated amount of dates, its people eating it fresh."
"5245","5602 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the people of their house, among them Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of fruit for dates, and said: That is riba, that is muzabanah. Except that he gave concession for the sale of the ariyyah, one or two date-palms which the household takes for its estimated amount in dates, eating them fresh."
"5246","5603 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi and Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, they said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from the freedman of Ibn Abi Ahmad, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave concession for the sale of al-araya in five wasqs or in less than five wasqs; Dawud doubts whether five or less than five."
"5247","5604 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Tamimi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Wasi ibn Habban, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave concession for the ariyyah in one wasq, two wasqs, three and four, and said: For every ten qinws, one qinw is placed in the mosque for the poor."
"5248","5605 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq informed us, and he mentioned with his chain the same, except that he said: then he said: one wasq, two wasqs, three and four, and he did not mention his saying: for every ten. Abu Jafar said: These reports have come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and are successive concerning the concession for the sale of al-araya, and the people of knowledge all accepted them and did not differ about the soundness of their coming, but disputed their interpretation. Some people said: al-araya is when a man owns one or two date-palms in the midst of abundant date-palms belonging to another man. They said: the people of Madinah, when it was the time of fruits, would go out with their families to their gardens, and the owner of the one or two date-palms would come with his family, harming the owners of the abundant date-palms. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave concession to the owner of the abundant date-palms to give the owner of the one or two date-palms the estimated amount of what he has in dates, so that he and his family would leave him, and all the dates of the garden would remain purely for the owner of the abundant date-palms, he and his family being in it. This saying has been narrated from Malik ibn Anas, may Allah have mercy on him. Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, used to say, as I heard Ahmad ibn Abi Imran mention that he heard it from Muhammad ibn Samaah, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah, he said: its meaning in our view is that a man grants a man the fruit of a date-palm of his, but does not hand it over to him until it occurs to him (to withhold it); so he was given concession to withhold that and to give him its estimated amount in dates in its place. This interpretation was more fitting and worthier than what Malik said, for the ariyyah is only a gift. Do you not see how the one who praised the Ansar praised them, when he says: in years of blights; meaning they used to grant them as ariyyah in the blight years. Had the ariyyah been as Malik held, they would not have been praised for it, since they would have given as they give; but the ariyyah is other than what he said. If one says: you have mentioned in the hadith of Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of fruit for dates and gave concession for al-araya, so al-araya in this hadith is also a sale of fruit for dates. It is said to him: there is nothing of that in the hadith; in it is only the mention of the concession for al-araya along with the mention of forbidding the sale of fruit for dates, and a thing may be joined with a thing while their rulings differ. If one says: the fixing at five wasqs is mentioned in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him, and in his mentioning that is what negates that what is more than that has its ruling. It is said to him: there is nothing in it negating anything of what I mentioned; that would only be so had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The ariyyah is only in five wasqs or in less than five wasqs. Since the hadith only states that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave concession for the sale of al-araya in five wasqs or in less than five wasqs, that may mean that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave concession in that to people concerning an ariyyah of theirs of this measure. So Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, transmitted that and informed of the concession in what it was in, and that does not negate that that concession applies to what is more than that. If one says: in the hadith of Umar and Jabir, may Allah be pleased with them both: except that he gave concession for al-araya, so that became excepted from the sale of fruit for dates. Thus it is established that it is a sale of fruit for dates. It is said to him: it may be that he meant thereby the one granted the ariyyah, so he gave him concession to take dates in place of dates on the tops of the date-palms; for he is thereby in the position of a seller, and that is lawful for him, so the exception is for this reason. And in the hadith of Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah: except that he gave concession for the sale of the ariyyah for its estimated amount in dates, its people eating it fresh. He mentioned a people for the ariyyah and made them eat it fresh; that can only be while those to whom it returned, for the replacement taken from them, owned it. That confirms the saying of Abu Hanifah. If one says: had the interpretation of these reports been what Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, held, there would be no meaning to the mention of concession in them. It is said to him: rather it has a sound meaning, but there is disagreement about what it is. Isa ibn Aban said: the meaning of the concession in that is that properties are all not owned by replacement except by one who owned them; a man may not sell what he does not own for its replacement and thereby own that replacement. He only owns that replacement when he owns it by sound ownership of the thing it is a replacement for. He said: the one granted the ariyyah had not owned the ariyyah, for he had not taken possession of it, yet the dates he takes in place of it have been made lawful for him in this hadith, though they are a replacement for fresh dates he had not owned. He said: this is what was meant by the concession. Another said: the concession is that when a man grants a man something of his fruit and has promised to hand it over to him so that the one handed over owns it by taking possession of it, and it is upon a man in his religion to fulfill his promise even if he is not compelled to it by ruling, then concession was given to the granter to withhold what he granted by giving the grantee its estimated amount in dates in place of it, without being sinful nor in the position of one who breaks an appointment; this is the place of the concession. This interpretation we mentioned from Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, is worthier than what the sense of this hadith was carried upon, for reports have come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) successively forbidding the sale of fruit for dates. Among them is what we have mentioned at the beginning of this. And among them is what has:"
"5249","5606 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Said and Abu Salamah narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not trade fruit for dates. 5607 - Ibn Shihab said: And Salim ibn Abd Allah narrated to me, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same alike."
"5250","5608 - Yazid and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5251","5609 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Ibn Umar asked about a man who bought fruit for one hundred farqs, to be measured out to him. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade this, meaning muzabanah."
"5252","5610 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of date-palm fruit for dates by measure, and raisins for grapes by measure, and grain for wheat by measure."
"5253","5611 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, that Ibn Umar was asked about a man who sold the fruit of his land to a man for one hundred farqs. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade this, and it is muzabanah."
"5254","5612 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Zuraah Wahb Allah ibn Rashid narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, he said: Nafi narrated to me that Abd Allah ibn Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muzabanah. He said: Muzabanah is when a man buys or sells his garden for dates by measure, or his vineyard for raisins by measure, or sells grain by measure for something of food."
"5255","5613 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muhaqalah and muzabanah."
"5256","5614 - Ismail ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, and he added that a man sell grain for one hundred farqs of wheat; and muzabanah is to sell fruit on the tops of date-palms for one hundred farqs."
"5257","5615 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Taifi informed us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mukhabarah, muzabanah and muhaqalah."
"5258","5616 - Abu Bakrah, Bakkar ibn Qutaybah, narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Sad ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, he said: Umar ibn Abi Salamah narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muhaqalah and muzabanah. He said: Muhaqalah is partnership in grain, and muzabanah is dates for dates on the date-palms. These reports have successively come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding the sale of measured fruit for fruit on the tops of date-palms. If the interpretation of al-araya is carried upon what Abu Hanifah held, the prohibition remains general and nothing of it is voided. If it is carried upon what Malik held, there is excluded from it what he interpreted the ariyyah upon, so nothing should be excluded from an agreed-upon hadith except by a hadith agreed upon in its interpretation, or by another agreed-upon indication. It has also been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what we have mentioned in other than this place, concerning forbidding the sale of fresh dates for dry dates. If we carry the meaning of the ariyyah upon what Malik said, what is narrated about it contradicts what is narrated about forbidding the sale of fresh dates for dry dates. If we carry it upon what Abu Hanifah said, their meanings agree and do not contradict. It is more proper for us in directing the senses and meanings of the reports to direct them to what contains no contradiction nor opposition of one sunnah by another. Thus there is established, by what we mentioned concerning the meaning of al-araya, what Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, held, and Allah is the guardian of success. It has also been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Lighten the charities, for in wealth there is the ariyyah and the bequest."
"5259","5617 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us of that, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, he said: I heard Qays ibn Sad narrating from Makhul al-Shami, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of that. That shows that the ariyyah is only something the owners of wealth grant ownership of to people in their lifetimes, as they grant ownership of bequests after their deaths. And another proof that the meaning of the ariyyah is as Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, said, not as his opponent said."
"5260","5618 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub and Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the seller and the buyer from muzabanah."
"5261","5619 - He said: And Zayd ibn Thabit said: He gave concession for al-araya, for one or two date-palms granted as a gift to a man, so he sells them for their estimated amount in dates. This is Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, one of those who narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the concession for the ariyyah, and he has informed that it is a gift, and Allah knows best."
"5262","Chapter 8: A man buys fruit, takes possession of it, then a calamity strikes it. 5620 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Humayd al-Araj, from Sulayman ibn Atiq, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of years and commanded the remission of calamities."
"5263","5621 - Ismail ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5264","5622 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Humayd al-Araj, from Sulayman ibn Atiq, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the remission of the calamity. Abu Jafar said: Some people held that the meaning of these calamities which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded to remit is fruits a man buys and takes possession of, then a calamity strikes them in his hand and destroys a third of them or more. They said: that voids its price from the buyer. They said: whatever strikes them and destroys less than a third of them is gone from the wealth of the buyer, and nothing of its price, little or much, is voided from him. They said: this is like the other hadith narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5265","5623 - They mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, that Abu al-Zubayr informed him, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you sell fruit to your brother and a calamity strikes it, it is not lawful for you to take anything from him; for what would you take the wealth of your brother without right."
"5266","5624 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, and he mentioned with his chain the same. They said: this hadith has clarified the meaning we mentioned. Others opposed them in that and said: whatever of that is destroyed, little or much, after the buyer takes possession of it, is gone from the wealth of the buyer. And whatever is destroyed in the hand of the seller, before the buyer takes possession of it, its price is voided from the buyer. They said: these reports narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which you mentioned are accepted and sound as they came. We do not reject anything of that given the soundness of its channel, but we oppose the interpretation the people of the first saying put upon them, and we say: the meaning of the calamities mentioned in them is the calamities by which people are struck, overtaking them in the kharaj lands whose kharaj belongs to the Muslims, so that kharaj is remitted from them, as an obligatory binding duty, for in that is benefit for the Muslims and strengthening for them in cultivating their lands; but as for sold goods, no. This is the interpretation of the hadith of Jabir at the beginning of this chapter according to them. As for the second hadith of Jabir, its meaning is other than this meaning, for it mentions the sale but does not mention possession. That, according to us, concerns sales that are struck while in the hands of their sellers, before the buyer takes possession of them, so it is not lawful for the sellers to take their prices, for they would take them without right. This is the interpretation of this hadith according to them. As for what the buyers took possession of and came into their hands, that is like all other sales which their buyers take possession of, then blights occur in them in their hands. Just as other than fruits is gone from the wealth of those who bought them, not from the wealth of their sellers, so too are fruits. This is reflection, and it is worthiest of what this hadith is carried upon; for there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what has:"
"5267","5625 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me. (Ha). 5626 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf informed us. (Ha). 5627 - And Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us. (Ha). 5628 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Saylahini narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us; they all said: from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, from Iyad ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: A man was struck in fruits he had bought, and his debt became great, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give charity to him. So charity was given to him, but that did not reach the settlement of his debt. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take what you find, and you have nothing but that. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not void the debt of the creditors, among whom were its sellers, nor return it upon the sellers for the price, if they had taken possession of that from him, it is established that calamities occurring in the hand of the buyer do not cancel anything of the price owed by him to the seller. If one says: fruits do not resemble other sales because they hang on the tops of date-palms, the hand of one who bought them reaching them only by cutting them off, while other things are not so. So what is possessed without a fresh cutting is what is gone from the wealth of the buyer, and what is not possessed except by a fresh cutting is what is gone from the wealth of the seller. It is said to him: this speech is corrupt in two ways: as for one of them, we have seen these fruits, when sold on the tops of date-palms and destroyed entirely, or something of them destroyed in the hands of their sellers, that is gone from their wealth, not from the wealth of the buyers; so the destruction of little and much of them in that is alike, for they had not taken possession of them. Then when they take possession of them and less than a third of them is destroyed, it is agreed that it is gone from the wealth of the buyer, for it was destroyed after his taking possession of it. Since the destruction of little and much of it in the hand of the seller was alike, and its little, when destroyed in the hand of the buyer, was gone from his wealth, the destruction of its much is likewise. And the buyer, by the seller vacating between him and the fruit of the date-palms, is one who has taken possession of it even if he did not cut it; this is one aspect. And another aspect: we have seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of food until it is taken possession of, and the Muslims agreed upon that, and fruits were included in that by their agreement; and they agreed that had its buyer sold it while in the hand of its seller, his sale would be void, but had he sold it after the seller vacated between him and it, without cutting it, his sale would be permissible. So he becomes one who has taken possession of it by the seller vacating between him and it, before his cutting it. Thus it is established that the taking possession by the buyer of that hanging on the tops of date-palms is by the seller vacating between him and it and enabling him of it. When he does that with him, it has come into his hand and his liability, and the seller is quit of it. Whatever calamity then occurs in it, destroying all of it or part of it, it is gone from the wealth of the buyer, not from the wealth of the seller. This is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all."
"5268","Chapter 9: What was forbidden to be sold until taken possession of. 5629 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Affan narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he takes possession of it."
"5269","5630 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5270","5631 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5271","5632 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he takes full possession of it."
"5272","5633 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he takes possession of it."
"5273","5634 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Umar, Umar ibn Muhammad, Malik and others informed me: that Nafi narrated to them, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he takes full possession of it."
"5274","5635 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same; Malik said: until he takes possession of it."
"5275","5636 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and others informed me, from al-Mundhir ibn Ubayd al-Madani, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn Umar, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade anyone to sell food he bought by measure until he takes full possession of it."
"5276","5637 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he takes possession of it."
"5277","5638 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from al-Dahhak ibn Uthman, from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he takes full possession of it."
"5278","5639 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, from Abd Allah ibn Ismah al-Jushami, from Hakim ibn Hizam, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Have I not been told, or have you not been informed, that you sell food; so do not sell it until you take full possession of it."
"5279","5640 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, from Safwan ibn Mawhab, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Safi, from Hakim ibn Hizam, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he said: until he takes possession of it."
"5280","5641 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Ata, from Hizam ibn Hakim, from Hakim ibn Hizam, he said: I used to buy food and profit in it before taking possession of it, so I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Do not sell it until you take possession of it. Abu Jafar said: Some people held that whoever buys any food may not sell it until he takes possession of it, while whoever buys other than food may sell it even if he has not taken possession of it, and they argued that with these reports. They said: since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) aimed the prohibition at food, that shows the ruling of other than food in that is contrary to the ruling of food. Others opposed them in that and said: that prohibition fell upon food and other than food, even though what is mentioned in the reports in which that prohibition is mentioned is food. They argued that with what:"
"5281","5642 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from Ubayd ibn Hunayn, from Ibn Umar, he said: I bought oil in the market, and when I had secured it, a man met me and offered me a good profit for it, so I wanted to strike his hand. Then a man took hold of my arm from behind me; I turned to him, and behold it was Zayd ibn Thabit. He said: Do not sell it where you bought it until you bring it to your lodging, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us to sell goods where they are bought until the merchants bring them to their lodgings. When Zayd informed from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that oil had entered into what he had forbidden to be sold before possession, though it is other than the food about which Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, knew from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the prohibition of selling it after buying it until taken possession of, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, acted upon that and wanted to sell the oil before taking possession of it because it is not of food, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, accepted that from him; and what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what we mentioned from him at the beginning of this chapter, of his aiming at food, did not prevent other than food being contrary to food in that. Then Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him, confirmed that, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid us buying goods where they are bought until the merchants bring them to their lodgings. So he gathered in that all goods, including other than food. That shows that nothing bought may be sold except after its buyer takes possession of it, whether food or other than food. And Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said, though he knew from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his aiming with the prohibition of selling what is not possessed at food:"
"5282","5643 - What Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade is the sale of food before it is taken full possession of. Ibn Abbas said by his own view: And I consider everything like it. This is Ibn Abbas, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them both; the aiming of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the prohibition at food did not prevent him from entering other than food into that prohibition. And Ibn Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated the like of that also."
"5283","5644 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, concerning a man who buys goods then sells them before taking possession of them, he said: I dislike it. This is Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him; he equated sold things in that, though he knew from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his aiming with the prohibition of selling therein until possessed at food in particular. That shows the prohibition is upon what we have described above. If one says: how did he aim the prohibition in that at food in particular and not generalize things? It is said to him: we have found the like of this in the Quran: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: Do not kill game while you are in ihram, and whoever of you kills it deliberately; and He obliged upon him the recompense mentioned in the verse. The people of knowledge did not differ about one who kills game by mistake, that upon him is the like of that, and that His mentioning deliberateness does not negate mistake. Likewise His mentioning food in forbidding its sale before possession does not negate other than food. And we have seen salam (advance payment) is permissible for food, while salam is not permissible for goods; and food was broader in sales than other than food, for salam is permissible for food even if not with the one advanced to, and that is not so for other than it. Since food was broader in sales and more permissible, and we saw he forbade its sale until taken possession of, that is even more deserving for what salam is not permissible in, that its sale until taken possession of is not permissible. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) aimed the prohibition at what, when forbidden, his forbidding of it indicates his forbidding of other than it, and his mentioning it sufficed him from mentioning other than it; so that stood in place of a prohibition had he generalized it over all things. Had he aimed the prohibition at other than food, the ruling of food in that would have been obscure to the hearer, who would not know whether it is likewise or not, for he finds salam permissible for food though it is not present at that time, which is not permissible for goods; so he would say: just as food differed from goods in the permissibility of salam therein though not with the one advanced to, which is not so for goods, so likewise it may differ from it in the permissibility of its sale before taken possession of, even if that is not permissible for goods. This is the reason for which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) aimed the prohibition of selling what is not possessed at food in particular. And in that is another proof: the reason for which it was forbidden for the buyer of food to sell it before possessing it is that the profit of what is in anothers liability should not be lawful to him; then when he takes possession of it, it comes into his liability, so its profit becomes lawful to him and he may sell it wherever he wishes. And sold goods: this very reason exists in them, for profit in them before possession is not lawful to their buyer, for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade profit on what is not guaranteed. Just as that included food and other than food, and profit was not wholesome for anyone except with prior liability for what that profit was from, likewise all sold things: what profit therein is wholesome for its seller, its sale is lawful to him; and what profit therein is forbidden to its seller, its sale is forbidden to him. And other reports have also come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding the sale of what is not possessed, in which he aimed at neither food nor other than it."
"5284","5645 - Abu Hazim, Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar Bundar narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, from Aban ibn Yazid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, that Yala ibn Hakim informed him that Yusuf ibn Mahak informed him that Abd Allah ibn Ismah informed him that Hakim ibn Hizam informed him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took my hand and said: When you buy something, do not sell it until you take possession of it."
"5285","5646 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Yala ibn Hakim ibn Hizam narrated to me that his father asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: I buy sales; what of them is lawful for me? He said: When you buy a sale, do not sell it until you take possession of it. Abu Jafar said: By this we take, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. Except that Abu Hanifah said: there is no harm in selling houses and lands before their buyer takes possession of them, for they are not moved nor shifted, while other sales are not so. And reflection on this, according to us, is that goods and all other things in that are alike, upon what we mentioned about food."
"5286","Chapter 10: A sale in which a condition not of it is stipulated. 5647 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah informed us, from al-Shabi, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that he was traveling with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon a camel of his, and it became exhausted. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caught up with him and said: What is the matter with you, O Jabir? He said: My water-carrying camel is exhausted, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do you have anything with you? Then he gave him a stick or a branch and prodded it with it, or he said: struck it, and it went a pace it had never gone. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Sell it to me for an ounce. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, it is your water-carrier. He said: So I sold it for an ounce, and I excepted its carrying me until I arrive to my family. When I arrived I brought him the camel and said: This is your camel, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Perhaps you think that I detained you only to take your camel. O Bilal, give him an ounce from the store, and he said: Go with your camel; both are yours. Abu Jafar said: Some people held that when a man sells a riding animal to a man for a known price, on condition that the seller ride it to a known place, the sale is permissible and the condition is permissible, and they argued that with this hadith of Jabir. Others opposed them in that, then those opposed to them split into two groups: one group said: the sale is permissible and the condition is void; and one group said: the sale is void. We shall explain what both groups held in this chapter, Allah the Exalted willing. Part of the proof of these two groups together against the first group, in the hadith of Jabir we mentioned, is that in it are two meanings showing they have no proof in it. As for one of the two meanings: the bargaining of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, was only upon the camel, and he did not stipulate in that, for Jabir, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him, any riding. Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: So I sold it and excepted its carrying me to my family. The sense of this hadith is that the sale was only upon what the bargaining from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon; then the exception of the riding was after it, so that exception was separate from the sale, for it was only after it. So there is in that no proof showing us how the ruling of the sale would be, had that exception been stipulated in its contract: is it likewise or not? As for the other proof: Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When I arrived at Madinah I brought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the camel and said: This is your camel, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Perhaps you think that I detained you only to take your camel. O Bilal, give him an ounce and take your camel; both are yours. That shows that the first saying was not upon mutual sale. Even if it were established that the stipulation of riding was in its origin, after the establishment of this defect, there would be no proof in this hadith, for what that condition was stipulated in was not a sale. And because the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not owned the camel against Jabir, so Jabirs stipulation of riding was a stipulation in what he owned. So there is in this no evidence for the ruling of that condition, had it occurred in a sale entailing ownership for the buyer, how its ruling would be. And those who voided the condition in that, while permitting the sale, went to the hadith of Barirah."
"5287","5648 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Aishah wanted to buy Barirah to free her, but her people said to her: We sell her to you on condition that her wala (clientage) is ours. She mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Let that not prevent you, for wala belongs only to one who frees."
"5288","5649 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman, that Barirah came seeking Aishahs help. Aishah said to her: If your people wish that I pour out your price to them in one payment and free you, I shall do so. Barirah mentioned that to her people, and they said: No, unless your wala is ours. Malik said: Yahya said: Amrah claimed that Aishah mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Buy her and free her, for wala belongs only to one who frees."
"5289","5650 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, that she wanted to buy Barirah to free her, but her masters stipulated her wala. She mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Buy her and free her, for wala belongs only to one who frees."
"5290","5651 - And Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, that the household of Barirah wanted to sell her while stipulating the wala. She mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Buy her and free her, for wala belongs only to one who frees."
"5291","5652 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Rabiah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, that Barirah came seeking her help in her contract of manumission. Aishah said: If your people wish, I shall buy you and pay them your price in one payment. She went to her people and said that to them, but they refused unless the wala is theirs. She mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Buy her, and what they said does not harm you, for wala belongs only to one who frees. They said: since the people of Barirah wanted to sell her on condition that she be freed and her wala be theirs, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: That does not harm you, for wala belongs only to one who frees; that shows that all such conditions stipulated in sales are likewise, that they are void while the sales stand. Part of the proof against them is that these reports were thus narrated: that she wanted to buy her to free her, but her people refused unless her wala be theirs. And others narrated them contrary to that."
"5292","5653 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Men of knowledge informed me, among them Yunus ibn Yazid and al-Layth, from Ibn Shihab, they narrated to them from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: Barirah came to me and said: O Aishah, I have contracted with my people for nine ounces, one ounce each year; so help me. She had not yet paid anything of her contract. Aishah said to her: Go back to your people; if they wish that I give them that all together and your wala be mine, I shall do so. She went to her people and offered that to them, but they refused and said: If she wishes to count it as charity upon you, let her do so, while your wala is ours. She mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Let that not prevent you from her; buy and free, for wala belongs only to one who frees. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among the people, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: To proceed: what is the matter with people who stipulate conditions not in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic. Every condition not in the Book of Allah is void, even if a hundred conditions. The decree of Allah is more deserving, and the condition of Allah is more binding; for wala belongs only to one who frees. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is other than what is in the first hadiths, for in the first hadiths the people of Barirah wanted to sell her on condition that Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, free her, while her wala be theirs. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let that not prevent you; buy her and free her, for wala belongs only to one who frees. So in this hadith there was permission for sale on condition that the buyer free, and that the wala of the freed be the sellers; then when that occurred, the sale stood, the condition was void, and the wala belonged to the freer. And in the hadith of Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with her, Aishah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with her, said to her: If your people wish that I give them that, meaning the contract sum, in one payment, I shall do so, and your wala be mine. When Barirah offered them that they said: If she wishes to count it as charity upon you, let her do so. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: Let that not prevent you from her; buy her and free her, for wala belongs only to one who frees. So what was in this hadith, of what was from the people of Barirah, of stipulating the wala, was not in a sale, but in Aishahs, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with her, paying the contract sum to them on behalf of Barirah, while they undertook the contract of that manumission, and that payment from Aishah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with her, was not preceded by ownership. Aishah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with her, mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Let that not prevent you from her; meaning: do not turn back, for this reason, from what you intended in her freeing of reward. Buy her and free her, for wala belongs only to one who frees. So the mention of that buying here was an initiation from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not of what had been before that between Aishah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with her, and the people of Barirah in anything. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and preached, saying: What is the matter with peoples who stipulate conditions not in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic. Every condition not in the Book of Allah is void, even if a hundred conditions; as a rebuke from him to Aishah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with her, in her seeking the wala of one whose manumission contract was undertaken by another, by right of his ownership over her. Then he alerted her and taught her by his saying: For wala belongs only to one who frees; meaning: when the contracted slave is freed by payment of the contract sum, his contractor is the one who freed him, so his wala belongs to him. This is a hadith containing the opposite of what is in the other first hadiths, and there is in it no evidence for stipulating the wala in a sale, how its ruling is: must the sale be void thereby or not? If one says: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated it from his father and added something in it. 5654 - We said to him: You spoke truly."
"5293","Ismail ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, that she said: Barirah came to me and said: I have contracted with my people for nine ounces, one ounce each year; so help me. Aishah said to her: If your people wish that I count it out to them, I shall count it out to them, and your wala be mine. So Barirah went to her people and said that to them, but they refused her. She came from her people while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting and said: I have offered that to them but they refused unless the wala is theirs. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard that and asked her; Aishah informed him and he said: Take her and stipulate, for wala belongs only to one who frees. So Aishah did so. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among the people and mentioned the like of what is in the hadith of al-Zuhri."
"5294","5655 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, and he mentioned with his chain the same. In this hadith is the like of what is in the hadith of al-Zuhri: that what was in it of the stipulation from the people of Barirah that the wala be theirs, and the refusal of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, except that the wala be hers, was Aishahs, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with her, paying the contract sum on behalf of Barirah. Al-Zuhri and Hisham agreed upon this and differed in that from the narrators of the first hadiths. And Hisham added over al-Zuhri the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Take her and stipulate, for wala belongs only to one who frees. Thus it is in the hadith of Hisham; and the place of this speech in the hadith of al-Zuhri is: Buy and free, for wala belongs only to one who frees. In this Hisham and al-Zuhri differed. If what is considered in this is precision and memory, to be taken by what its people narrated and to leave what the others narrated, then what al-Zuhri narrated is worthier, for he is more precise, more exact and more retentive than Hisham. If what is considered in that is interpretation, then his saying Take her may mean: buy her, as a man says to his fellow: For how much do I take this slave, meaning thereby: for how much do I buy this slave? And as a man says to a man: Take this slave for a thousand dirhams, meaning thereby sale. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And stipulate, without clarifying what she stipulates. So it may mean: and stipulate what is stipulated in sound sales. So there is in this hadith of Hisham, when its meaning is uncovered, no disagreement with anything of what is in the hadith of al-Zuhri, nor is there in them both any clarification of how the ruling of the sale is when such a condition occurs in it: is it void, or is it permissible?"
"5295","5656 - As for what those who voided the sale by that condition argued with: what Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a sale and a loan, and two conditions in one sale."
"5296","5657 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: A loan and a sale are not lawful, nor two conditions in a sale."
"5297","5658 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"5298","5659 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"5299","5660 - al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade two conditions in a sale, and a loan and a sale."
"5300","5661 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja informed us, he said: Hammam informed us, from Amir al-Ahwal, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5301","5661 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Nafi informed me, from Dawud ibn Qays, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a sale and a loan. They said: sale in itself is a condition; so when another condition is stipulated in it, these are two conditions in a sale; this is the two conditions forbidden according to them, mentioned in this hadith. But they were opposed in that, it being said: the two conditions in a sale is when the sale occurs for a thousand dirhams on the spot or for a hundred dinars in a year, so the sale occurs on the buyer giving whichever of them he wishes; thus the sale is void, for it occurred for an unknown price. Part of the proof for them in that, of what has been narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), is that Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Salamah, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Amr ibn al-Harith narrating from Zaynab, wife of Abd Allah ibn Masud, that she sold Abd Allah a slave-girl and stipulated her service. That was mentioned to Umar and he said: He shall not approach her, and I find in her no recompense."
"5302","5662 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, he said: Nafi narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, he said: No private part is lawful except a private part its owner, if he wishes, sells, if he wishes, gifts, and if he wishes, keeps; no condition in it."
"5303","5663 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he used to dislike a man buying a slave-girl on condition not to sell nor gift. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, voided the sale of Abd Allah, and Abd Allah followed him upon that and did not oppose him in it. Had he held otherwise he could have opposed him, for what was from Umar was not in the manner of a judgment, but was in the manner of a fatwa. And Zaynab, wife of Abd Allah, followed them both upon that, and she has companionship from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them both, followed them upon that, though he knew from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what was of his saying to Aishah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with her, in the affair of Barirah, upon what we narrated from him in this chapter. That shows its meaning, according to him, was contrary to what those who argued with his hadith carried it upon. And we know of no one among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), other than those we mentioned, who held in that other than what Umar held, and those who followed him upon that of those we mentioned in these reports. So this ought to be made a principle and a consensus of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with them, and not to be opposed. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for its aspect by way of reflection: we have seen the agreed-upon principle that sound conditions may be contracted in the sold thing, like the option for a known term for the seller and for the buyer, so the sale upon that is permissible. Likewise prices: terms stipulated by the buyer may be contracted in them, binding when known, and the sale is guaranteed by them. And we have seen that term, had it been void, the sale would be voided by its voidness; the sale would not stand while it is removed, when contracted therein. Since the sale was made guaranteed by these conditions stipulated in its price, in their soundness and voidness, being made permissible by their permissibility and void by their voidness, then when the sale occurred upon the sold thing, being a slave, on condition he serve the seller for a month, the seller has given ownership to the buyer of his slave on the buyer giving ownership of a thousand dirhams and the service of the slave for a month, while the buyer at that time owns neither the service nor the slave, for his ownership of the slave is only after the completion of the sale. So the sale occurs for wealth and for the service of a slave the buyer does not own at the time of his buying with the wealth and with its service. And we have seen that had he bought a slave for the service of a slave-girl he does not own, the sale would be void. So by reflection it should likewise be when contracted for the service of one whose ownership did not precede for him before that contract, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of what is not with you. And since prices were guaranteed by sound and void terms, upon what we mentioned, likewise sold things are also guaranteed by void and sound conditions. Thus it is established that a sale, had it occurred with an unknown condition stipulated in it, the sale is voided by the voidness of that condition, upon what we mentioned. So the saying of one who said: The sale is permissible and the condition is void, and the saying of one who said: The sale is permissible and the condition stands, are negated. And there was in this chapter no saying other than these two sayings, and other than the other saying: The sale is void when there is stipulated in it what is not of it. Since the first two sayings are negated, this other saying is established, and this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them all."
"5304","Chapter 11: Sale of the land of Makkah and its leasing. 5664 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from his father, from Mujahid, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful to sell the houses of Makkah nor to lease them."
"5305","5665 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Umar ibn Said, from Ibn Abi Sulayman, from Alqamah ibn Nadlah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, and Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman (passed away), while the quarters of Makkah were called al-sawaib: whoever needed, dwelt, and whoever was independent, lodged (others)."
"5306","5666 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Umar ibn Said, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to me, from Alqamah ibn Nadlah, he said: The houses were, in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman, not sold, nor hired out, nor called but al-sawaib: whoever needed, dwelt, and whoever was independent, lodged (others). Abu Jafar said: Some people went to these reports and said: it is not permissible to sell the land of Makkah nor to lease it. Among those who said this saying are Abu Hanifah, Muhammad and Sufyan al-Thawri, may Allah have mercy on him. And that has also been narrated from Ata and Mujahid."
"5307","5667 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Qurrah ibn Habib narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Awwam ibn Hawshab, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, that he used to dislike the rents of the houses of Makkah."
"5308","5668 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid, that he said: Makkah is free; it is not lawful to sell its quarters nor to lease its houses. Others opposed them in that and said: there is no harm in selling its land and leasing it, and they made it in that like all other lands."
"5309","5669 - Among those who held this saying is Abu Yusuf. And they argued in that with what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Ali ibn Husayn informed him that Amr ibn Uthman informed him, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, will you stay in your house in Makkah? He said: And has Aqil left us any quarters or houses? Aqil had inherited Abu Talib, he and Talib, while Jafar and Ali did not inherit him, for they were Muslims, while Aqil and Talib were disbelievers. And Umar ibn al-Khattab for that reason used to say: The believer does not inherit the disbeliever."
"5310","5670 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is what shows that the land of Makkah is owned and inherited, for it mentioned therein the inheritance of Aqil and Talib of what Abu Talib left therein of quarters and houses; this is contrary to the first hadith. Since they differed, reflection upon that was needed, to extract from the two sayings a sound saying. Had it gone to the way of choosing chains and diverted the saying to that, the hadith of Ali ibn Husayn would have been the soundest of them in chain. But we need to uncover that by way of reflection. So we considered that and saw the Sacred Mosque, in which all people are alike: it is not permissible for anyone to build a building in it nor to reserve a place of it; and likewise is the ruling of all places in which no ownership falls to anyone, and all people are alike therein. Do you not see that Arafah: had a man wanted to build, in the place where people stand therein, a building, that would not be his. Likewise Mina: had he wanted to build a house therein, he would be prevented from that. And thus came the report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5311","5671 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam ibn Marwan al-Darir al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from his mother, from Aishah, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we not take for you in Mina something to shade you? He said: O Aishah, it is a resting-place for whoever precedes. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not permit them to make for him therein something to shade him, for it is the resting-place of whoever precedes, and all people are alike therein."
"5312","5672 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us. (Ha). 5673 - And Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us; they said: Israil narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from his mother, who used to serve Aishah, Mother of the Believers, and she narrated to him from Aishah the same. He said: And my mother asked, in the place of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, to give it to her. Aishah said to her: I do not make lawful for you nor for anyone of my household to deem lawful this place, meaning Mina. Abu Jafar said: This is the ruling of the places in which people are alike and over which no one has ownership; and we have seen Makkah other than that, for building therein has been permitted."
"5313","And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day he entered it: Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe, and whoever locks his door upon himself is safe. 5674 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us of that, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abd Allah ibn Rabah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Since Makkah is of what doors are locked upon, and of what dwellings are built therein, its description was the description of the places over which ownerships run and in which inheritances occur. If one argues in that with the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: Those who disbelieved and bar from the way of Allah and the Sacred Mosque, which We made for people alike, the resident therein and the visitor. It is said to him: there has been narrated in its interpretation from the predecessors what has:"
"5314","5675 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Muslim, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Alike, the resident therein and the visitor. And he said: Allahs creation therein are alike."
"5315","5676 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, he said: I wanted to observe itikaf, so I asked Said ibn Jubayr while I was in Makkah, and he said: You are observing itikaf. Then he recited: Alike, the resident therein and the visitor."
"5316","5677 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, from Ata, he said: Alike, the resident therein and the visitor. He said: People are alike in the House; no one is more entitled to it than another. By that it is established that he thereby meant only the House or the Sacred Mosque, not the rest of Makkah; and this is the saying of Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him."
"5317","Chapter 12: Price of the dog. 5678 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Abu Masud, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the price of the dog, the dowry of the prostitute, and the fee of the soothsayer."
"5318","5679 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"5319","5680 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr, from Abu Masud, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three are suht, meaning unlawful; then he mentioned the same."
"5320","5681 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Ismail al-Khazzaz narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah ibn Qariz, that al-Saib ibn Yazid narrated to him that Rafi ibn Khadij narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The earning of the cupper is foul, the dowry of the prostitute is foul, and the price of the dog is foul."
"5321","5682 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin and Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Majid ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Khubayb ibn Abi Thabit, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the price of the dog."
"5322","5683 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Karim al-Jazari narrated to us, from Qays ibn Habtar, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The price of the dog is unlawful."
"5323","5684 - Yunus and Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, they said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"5324","5685 - Malik ibn Abd Allah al-Tujibi narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us. (Ha). 5686 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Jafar, that Safwan ibn Sulaym informed him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the price of the dog, even if it hunts."
"5325","5687 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-Amash, he said: Abu Sufyan narrated to me, from Jabir, confirming it once and once doubting about Abu Sufyan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he forbade the price of the dog and the cat."
"5326","5688 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, without doubting."
"5327","5689 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5328","5690 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Maruf ibn Suwayd informed me that Ulayy ibn Rabah narrated to them, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The price of the dog is not lawful."
"5329","5691 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Humayd ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Said ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Sharik ibn Abi Namir, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the price of the dog and the dowry of the prostitute."
"5330","5692 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Rabah narrated to us, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The price of the dog is of suht (unlawful earnings)."
"5331","5693 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Fudayl informed us, from al-Amash, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the price of the dog."
"5332","5694 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us. (Ha). 5695 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah narrated to us, informing me from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5333","5696 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5334","5697 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid informed us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: I asked Jabir about the price of the dog and the cat, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rebuked that. Abu Jafar said: Some people went to forbidding the prices of all dogs, and argued that with these reports. Others opposed them in that and said: there is no harm in the prices of all dogs benefited from. Part of their proof in that, against the people of the first saying, in what they argued with against them, of the reports we mentioned, is that the ruling of dogs had been that they all be killed, and it was not lawful for anyone to keep anything of them; so their sale was then not permissible, nor their price lawful."
"5335","5698 - Of what was narrated in that: what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the killing of all dogs, and he sent throughout the districts of Madinah that they be killed."
"5336","5699 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), raising his voice, commanding the killing of dogs."
"5337","5700 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the killing of dogs."
"5338","5701 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, he said: the son of the daughter of Abu Rafi informed me, from Abu Rafi, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed the spear to Abu Rafi and commanded him to kill all the dogs of Madinah, until the killing led him to a dog of an old woman, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to kill it."
"5339","5702 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us. (Ha). 5703 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, they said: Yaqub ibn Muhammad ibn Tahlah narrated to us, from Abu al-Rijal, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Rafi, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to kill dogs. So I went out killing them; I did not see a dog but I killed it, until I came to such-and-such place, and he named it, and behold therein a dog circling a house. I went to kill it, when a person called me from inside the house: O servant of Allah, what do you want to do? I said: I want to kill this dog. She said: I am a woman in a neglected house, and this dog drives beasts away from me and alerts me to one who comes; so go to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mention that to him. So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and he commanded me to kill it."
"5340","5703 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Hawdhah ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Awf, from al-Hasan, from Abd Allah ibn al-Mughaffal, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that dogs are a nation of nations, I would have commanded their killing; so kill of them every pitch-black one."
"5341","5704 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah, that Jibril, upon him be prayer and peace, promised the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at an hour to come to him in it, but the hour passed and he did not come to him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, and behold Jibril, upon him be peace, at the door. He said: What prevented you from entering the house? He said: There is a dog in the house, and we do not enter a house in which there is a dog, nor an image. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded concerning the dog and it was taken out; then he commanded concerning dogs that they be killed."
"5342","5705 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, that al-Saib ibn Yazid informed him that Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr informed him that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever keeps a dog, a qirat is deducted from his deed every day. Abu Jafar said: This was the ruling of dogs: that they be killed, and it was not lawful to keep them nor to benefit from them. So what benefit from it was unlawful and keeping it unlawful, its price is unlawful."
"5343","5706 - If the forbidding by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the price of the dog was when this was their ruling, then that has been abrogated, and benefiting from dogs was permitted. And there has been narrated in that what Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hanzalah ibn Abi Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I heard Salim ibn Abd Allah say: I heard Ibn Umar say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever acquires a dog, except a hunting dog or a livestock dog, two qirats are deducted from his reward every day."
"5344","5707 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever acquires a dog, except a hunting dog or a livestock dog, two qirats are deducted from his deed every day."
"5345","5708 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5346","5709 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5347","5710 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to me, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, and he mentioned with his chain the same, except that he said: a qirat."
"5348","5711 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5349","5712 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the killing of dogs except a hunting dog or a livestock dog."
"5350","5713 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, he said: Ibn Shihab said: Salim ibn Abd Allah narrated to me, from his father, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, raising his voice, commanding the killing of dogs; and dogs were killed except a hunting dog or a livestock dog."
"5351","5714 - Ibn Shihab said: And Said ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever acquires a dog, not a hunting dog, nor livestock, nor land, two qirats are deducted from his reward in every day."
"5352","5715 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun say: Hammam ibn Yahya informed us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Hakam, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever acquires a dog, other than a field dog or a hunting dog, two qirats are deducted from his deed every day."
"5353","5716 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he said: except a hunting dog or a livestock dog."
"5354","5717 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Bujayr ibn Abi Bujayr, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned dogs and said: Whoever takes a dog that is not a hunting dog or a livestock dog, a qirat is deducted from his reward every day."
"5355","5718 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Salamah and others, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade dogs, and said: Dogs are not to be taken except by a hunter, or one in fear, or an owner of sheep."
"5356","5719 - And Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, he said: Abu Hurayrah narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever keeps a dog, a qirat is deducted from his deed every day, except a field dog or a livestock dog."
"5357","5720 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Layhiah informed me that Abu al-Zubayr informed him that he asked Jabir: Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say anything about dogs? He said: He commanded their killing, then he permitted groups."
"5358","5721 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Amir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, from Mutarrif, from Abd Allah ibn al-Mughaffal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the killing of dogs, then said: What have I to do with dogs? Then he permitted the hunting dog, and another dog which Said forgot."
"5359","5722 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, he said: al-Saib ibn Yazid informed me that Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr al-Shanai informed him that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever acquires a dog that avails him nothing for udder nor crop, a qirat is deducted from his deed every day. He said: al-Saib said to Sufyan: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, by the Lord of the Qiblah."
"5360","5723 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"5361","5724 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar informed us, he said: Yazid ibn Khusayfah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same, except that he did not mention the saying of al-Saib to Sufyan: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Abu Jafar said: When permission was established after prohibition, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, permitted in His Book what He permitted by His saying: And what you have taught of hunting animals as trainers; we considered the ruling of what is benefited from: is its sale permissible and its price lawful or not? So we saw the domestic donkey, whose eating was forbidden, while its earning and benefiting from it were permitted; so its sale, since this was its ruling, was lawful, and its price lawful. And it would follow by reflection likewise for dogs: when benefiting from them was permitted, their sale became lawful and consuming their price. And what was narrated of the unlawfulness of their prices was at the time of the unlawfulness of benefiting from them; and what was narrated of permitting benefiting from them is proof of the lawfulness of their prices. This is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all."
"5362","5725 - And Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Aban ibn Salih, from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, from Salma Umm Rafi, from Abu Rafi, he said: Jibril, upon him be peace, came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and sought permission upon him; he gave him permission but he was late. So he took his cloak and went out and said: We gave you permission. He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, but we do not enter a house in which there is an image nor a dog. So they looked, and behold in some of their houses a puppy. So he commanded Abu Rafi not to leave a dog in Madinah but to kill it. And behold a woman in a district of Madinah having a dog guarding her sheep. He said: So I pitied her, and I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he commanded me, and I killed it. Then some people came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, what is lawful for us of this nation which you commanded us to kill? So there was revealed: They ask you what is made lawful for them; say: made lawful for you are the good things, and what you have taught of hunting animals as trainers."
"5363","5726 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Jufi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to me, he said: Aban ibn Salih narrated to me, from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, from Salma Umm Rafi, from Abu Rafi, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the killing of dogs, people came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, what is lawful for us of this nation which you commanded to be killed? So there was revealed: They ask you what is made lawful for them; say: made lawful for you are the good things, and what you have taught of hunting animals as trainers. In this hadith also is the like of what preceded it, of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted after he commanded their killing, even though this hadith does not mention other than what opposes of them. And in it is an addition over the preceding hadiths in the permission we mentioned; for in it is the revelation of this verse after the forbidding of dogs, and that this verse returned the trained hunting animals to making them lawful. And when they became so, they were, in all things that are lawful, in the lawfulness of keeping them, the permissibility of their prices, and the liability of those who destroy them for what they destroyed of them, like others. And there has been narrated in that from those after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5364","5727 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Jurayj narrating from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, Abd Allah ibn Amr, that he ruled for a hunting dog killed by a man at forty dirhams, and ruled for a livestock dog at a ram."
"5365","5728 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that he forbade the price of the dog and the cat, except the hunting dog. And we have narrated from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in this chapter, that he forbade the price of the dog without specifying which dog it is. So that is not free of one of two aspects: either he meant other than benefit-dogs, or he meant all dogs, then the abrogation of the hunting dog from them was established with him, so he excepted it in this hadith."
"5366","5729 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Jabir, from Ata, he said: There is no harm in the price of the Saluqi hunting dog. So this is Ata saying this, and it has been narrated from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): ""The price of a dog is from unlawful earnings (suht)."" So that points to the meaning we mentioned regarding the hadith of Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"5367","5730 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me from Ibn Shihab that he said: When a trained dog is killed, its value is assessed and the one who killed it pays it as compensation. So this is al-Zuhri saying this, and it has been narrated from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The price of a dog is suht (unlawful earnings). So the discussion here is like the discussion regarding the hadith of Jabir."
"5368","5731 - Bahr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal informed me, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban al-Ansari, he said: It used to be said: For a vicious dog when killed, forty dirhams are set."
"5369","5732 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik and Muhammad ibn Fudayl informed us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no harm in the price of a hunting dog."
"5370","Chapter 13: Borrowing animals. 5733 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Rafi': The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) borrowed a young camel (bakr) from a man, then camels from the charity camels came to him, so he ordered Abu Rafi' to repay the man his young camel. Abu Rafi' returned to him and said: I found therein only a choice four-year-old camel. He said: ""Give it to him, for the best of people are those best in repayment."""
"5371","5734 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Shababah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, he said: I heard Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrating from Abu Hurayrah, he said: A man had a debt upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he demanded it harshly. The Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned on him and were about to attack him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Leave him, for the one owed has a right to speak. Buy him a camel of (similar) age and give it to him."" They said: We find only a camel better than his age. He said: ""Buy it and give it to him, for the best of you - or among the best of you - are those best in repayment."""
"5372","5735 - Husayn narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun say: Sufyan al-Thawri informed us, from Salamah, and he mentioned with its chain the same, except that he did not say ""buy for him"" but said ""seek."" Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to permitting borrowing animals, and they argued with these reports. Others opposed them and said: Borrowing animals is not permitted. They said: It is possible this was before the prohibition of riba, then riba was prohibited afterwards, and every loan bringing benefit was prohibited, and borrowed things were returned to their equivalents, so a loan is only permitted in what has an equivalent; and before the abrogation of riba, selling animal for animal on credit was also permitted."
"5373","5736 - And the proof of that is that Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us (hawwalah). 5737 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us; they both said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Muslim ibn Jubayr, from Abu Sufyan, from Amr ibn Huraysh, from Abdullah ibn Amr: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to equip an army, and the camels ran out, so he ordered him to take from the young she-camels (qilas, plural of qalus: a young she-camel) of charity, so he would take one camel for two camels owed against the charity camels, then that was abrogated."
"5374","5738 - And there was narrated in it what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Muhriz al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling animal for animal on credit."
"5375","5739 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Shihab ibn Abbad narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Ma'mar, and he mentioned with its chain the same."
"5376","5740 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Sayrafi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Amr ibn Salih al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ash'ath, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw no harm in selling animal for animal, two for one, but he disliked it on credit."
"5377","5741 - Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Salim al-Sa'igh and Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh and Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi narrated to us, they said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Dinar al-Tahi narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Umar: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling animal for animal on credit."
"5378","5742 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5379","5743 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Samurah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5380","5744 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Abu Ja'far said: So this abrogated what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting selling animal for animal on credit, and borrowing animals entered into that as well. The people of the first view said: This does not bind us, for we have seen wheat, some of which is not sold for some on credit, yet lending it is permitted; so likewise an animal, selling some for some on credit is not permitted, but lending it is permitted. As our proof against this view in establishing the first view: the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibition of selling animal for animal on credit may be because its equivalent cannot be ascertained, or it may be for the reason the first party mentioned about wheat in sale and loan. If he forbade it due to absence of an equivalent, the second view is established; if because they are one genus whose sale on credit is forbidden, there is no proof for the second party against the first. We considered this and saw measured goods: selling some for some on credit is impermissible, but lending them is fine; weighed goods have the same ruling as measured, except gold and silver; and non-measured non-weighed goods like clothes and the like: selling some for some is fine even with disparity, while selling some for some on credit is disputed - some say if of one genus it is invalid on credit, but if of two different genera it is fine, and among those holding this are Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah's mercy be upon them all); others say selling some for some is fine spot and on credit, whether of one genus or two. These are the rulings of measured, weighed and counted things other than animals, as we explained. What is not measured or weighed may be sold for what is of a different genus on credit, even if both are clothes, while an animal may not be sold for an animal on credit even if genera differ - a slave may not be sold for a camel, cow or sheep on credit. Had the prohibition been due to sameness of genus, selling a slave for a cow on credit would be permitted as of different genus, just as selling a linen garment for a described cotton garment on credit is permitted. Since it is void both within its genus and outside it, the prohibition is established to be due to absence of an equivalent and because it cannot be ascertained. And since selling some for some on credit is void because it cannot be ascertained, lending it is likewise void. This is the reasoning in this chapter. What also proves it is their consensus on borrowing slave-girls, that pledging (rahn) an animal is not permitted; so lending all other animals by analogy is likewise. If one says: we saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rule a ghurrah (a slave or slave-girl) for the fetus, and a hundred camels for blood-money, and what he ruled for limbs in camels, all animals due upon the liability - why should not all animals be likewise? It is said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled with animals in diyah and for the fetus as you mentioned, while forbidding selling animal for animal on credit as we explained; so prohibition of animals becoming due upon liability in exchange for wealth is established, while animals becoming due without exchange for wealth is permitted. These are two different principles which we uphold and to which we return all branches: what is in exchange for wealth takes the ruling of the loan we described, and what is in exchange for other than wealth takes the ruling of blood-moneys. Of the latter is the ghurrah mentioned: marriage for a medium slave-girl or slave, and khul' for a medium slave-girl or slave. Proof of what we described is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set a ghurrah of a slave or slave-girl for the fetus of a free woman, while Muslims agree that does not apply to the fetus of a slave-girl, for which dirhams or dinars are due, as they differed: some said a tenth of the fetus's value if female and half a tenth if male - among them Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah's mercy be upon them all) - others said half a tenth of the fetus's mother's value; and they agreed for animal fetuses that the mother's depreciation is due. And the blood-moneys due in camels as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated apply to free persons, not slaves. So what he ruled with animals made due upon liabilities is what is not in exchange for wealth, and he barred it in exchanges for wealth. Thus a loan, which is in exchange for wealth, cannot make an animal due upon the liability. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah's mercy be upon them all), and it was narrated from a group of the predecessors."
"5381","5745 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: Zayd ibn Khulaydah paid in advance to Itris ibn Urqub for young she-camels (qalais), each qalus for fifty; when the term fell due he came demanding, then he went to Ibn Mas'ud seeking respite, so he forbade him that and ordered him to take his capital."
"5382","5746 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, he said: Advance payment (salam) in everything to a named term is fine, except animals."
"5383","5747 - Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ammar al-Duhni, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: Hudhayfah used to dislike salam in animals."
"5384","5748 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from Abu Nadrah, that he asked Ibn Umar about advance payment for slaves (wusafa'), and he said: There is no harm in it. I said: Our governors forbid us that. He said: Obey your governors; and our governors in those days were Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah and the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5385","Book 17: Currency exchange. Chapter 1: Riba. 5749 - Fahd ibn Sulayman ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, from Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Riba is only in deferment (nasi'ah)."
"5386","5750 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5387","5751 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Khalid, i.e. Ibn Abdullah al-Wasiti, informed us, from Khalid, i.e. al-Hadhdha', from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no riba except in deferment."
"5388","5752 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Ata: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri met Ibn Abbas and said: Tell me your statement on exchange (sarf), i.e. gold for gold with disparity between them - is it something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or something you found in the Book of Allah (mighty and majestic)? Ibn Abbas said: As for the Book of Allah, I do not know it; as for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), you know him better than I. 5753 - But Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Riba is only in deferment."
"5389","5754 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Nafi' informed us, from Dawud ibn Qays, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id, he said: I said to Ibn Abbas: What about what you say, two dinars for a dinar and two dirhams for a dirham - I testify I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Dinar for dinar and dirham for dirham, with no disparity between them. Ibn Abbas said: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I said: Yes. He said: I did not hear this; Usamah ibn Zayd only informed me of it. Abu Sa'id said: And Ibn Abbas withdrew from it."
"5390","5755 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Qays, i.e. Ibn al-Rabi', informed us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu Salih al-Samman, he said: I said to Abu Sa'id: You forbid exchange (sarf) while Ibn Abbas permits it. He said: I met Ibn Abbas and said: What is this you give fatwa about exchange? Is it something you found in the Book of Allah, or something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: You have longer companionship with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than I, and I recite of the Qur'an only what you recite, but Usamah ibn Zayd (informed me)."
"5391","5756 - He narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no riba except in debt. Abu Ja'far said: So a group held that selling silver for silver and gold for gold, two for one, is permitted when hand to hand, arguing with what we narrated from Usamah ibn Zayd from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Others opposed them and said: Selling silver for silver and gold for gold is only permitted equal for equal, alike for alike, hand to hand. Their proof in interpreting the hadith of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) from Usamah (may Allah be pleased with him) mentioned in the first section is that the riba meant is the riba of the Qur'an, whose origin was in deferment: a man owed a debt, and his creditor would say: Grant me delay to such-and-such in return for such-and-such dirhams I add to your debt, so he buys a term for wealth; Allah (mighty and majestic) forbade them that by His saying: {O you who believe, fear Allah and give up what remains of riba, if you are believers}. Then the Sunnah came after that prohibiting riba in disparity - gold for gold, silver for silver, and all measured and weighed things, as Ubadah ibn al-Samit (may Allah be pleased with him) mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what we narrated from him earlier in this book in the chapter on selling wheat for barley. That was riba prohibited by the Sunnah, transmitted in reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until proof was established. Proof that the riba prohibited in these reports is other than the riba Ibn Abbas narrated from Usamah (may Allah be pleased with them): Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) returned to what Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which we mentioned in this chapter. Had what Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated been in the meaning Usamah (may Allah be pleased with him) had narrated, Abu Sa'id's hadith would not have been weightier to him than Usamah's. Rather, he had not known of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibition of this riba until Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated it to him; so he knew what Usamah (may Allah be pleased with him) had narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was about riba other than that riba."
"5392","5757 - Among what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to what Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) mentioned is what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from a freedman of theirs, from Malik ibn Abi Amir, from Uthman ibn Affan: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell a dinar for two dinars, nor a dirham for two dirhams."
"5393","5758 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me that Humayd ibn Qays narrated to him, from Mujahid al-Makki: A goldsmith - i.e. a worker of jewelry - asked Abdullah ibn Umar: I fashion (gold), then I sell an item of it for more than its weight, taking extra for my workmanship. Abdullah ibn Umar forbade him that. The goldsmith kept repeating the question, and Abdullah ibn Umar kept refusing him, until he reached his mount, or the mosque door. Abdullah said to him: Dinar for dinar, and dirham for dirham, with no disparity between them; this is our Prophet's covenant to us, and our covenant to you."
"5394","5759 - And Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Abu al-Khalil, from Muslim al-Makki, from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani, that he witnessed Ubadah's sermon, that he narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Gold for gold, weight for weight; silver for silver, weight for weight; wheat for wheat, measure for measure; barley for barley; there is no harm in selling barley for dates, the dates being the greater of the two, hand to hand; dates for dates, and salt for salt; whoever gives more or takes more has committed riba."
"5395","5760 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Hafs al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ash'ath, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Gold for gold, weight for weight; silver for silver, weight for weight; wheat for wheat, like for like; barley for barley, like for like; dates for dates, like for like; salt for salt, like for like; whoever gives more or takes more has committed riba."
"5396","5761 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Habib al-Sarraj narrated to us, he said: Hayyan Abu Zuhayr narrated to us, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) craved dates, so he sent one of his wives - I think only Umm Salamah - with two sa's of dates, and they brought a sa' of ajwah. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw it, he disapproved and said: Where is this from? They said: We sent two sa's and were given a sa'. He said: Return it, I have no need of it."
"5397","5762 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Muhammad narrated to me, he said: Nafi' narrated to me, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar walked to Rafi' ibn Khadij about a hadith that reached him from him on exchange, so he came to him, entered upon him, and asked him about it. Rafi' said: My ears heard it and my eyes saw it: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Do not give preponderance of dinar over dinar, nor dirham over dirham, nor sell what is absent of them for what is present, even if he asks you to wait until he enters his doorstep."
"5398","5763 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', he said: I went with Abdullah ibn Umar to Abu Sa'id, and he mentioned the same, except the words: ""even if he asks you to wait"" to the end of the hadith, for he did not mention it."
"5399","5764 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, and he mentioned with its chain the same."
"5400","5765 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid informed us, from Hakim ibn Jabir, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Gold for gold, like for like, pan for pan; silver for silver, like for like, pan for pan; wheat for wheat, like for like, hand to hand; barley for barley, like for like, hand to hand; dates for dates, like for like, hand to hand, until he mentioned salt."
